window.PRIMER_DATA={"built":"2026-11-01","cats":[{"k":"household","name":"Household","color":"#C2785C","dek":"How the house works, and what to do when it doesn’t."},{"k":"kitchen","name":"Kitchen","color":"#5E8C61","dek":"Knives, labels, coffee and the temperatures that matter."},{"k":"money","name":"Money","color":"#A9852F","dek":"Interest, credit, insurance, taxes and the paycheck."},{"k":"life","name":"Life skills","color":"#7B6A8E","dek":"Negotiating, remembering, deciding and difficult talks."},{"k":"health","name":"Health","color":"#3E9B8A","dek":"Sleep, caffeine, the immune system and first aid."},{"k":"history","name":"History","color":"#8C3B3B","dek":"Ten turning points, ten cards each."},{"k":"society","name":"Society","color":"#5B7FA1","dek":"How the government and the economy are wired."},{"k":"culture","name":"Culture","color":"#A2566F","dek":"Music, painting, buildings and ideas."},{"k":"science","name":"Science","color":"#3F6FB5","dek":"The internet, the climate, GPS and probability."}],"topics":[{"id":"house-wiring","title":"How your house is wired","dek":"Breakers, watts and the outlet with the two buttons","icon":"⚡","slides":[{"h":"Power arrives at the panel","p":"Utility lines feed a meter and then the breaker panel. In North America the service is 240 volts split into two 120-volt legs; most homes have 100 to 200 amps of capacity. Every circuit in the house starts at one breaker in that box.","v":{"t":"flow","items":[["🏭","Utility"],["🔢","Meter"],["🗄","Panel"],["〰","Circuits"],["🔌","Outlets"]]}},{"h":"A breaker is a switch that trips itself","p":"Each breaker guards one circuit. When the current climbs past its rating it snaps off before the wire in the wall can overheat. To reset one, push it firmly all the way to OFF, then back to ON.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🛏","15 amps","bedrooms, lights"],["🍳","20 amps","kitchen, bath, laundry"],["🚗","30–50 amps","dryer, range, EV"]]}},{"h":"Volts push, amps flow, watts work","p":"Watts are volts times amps. A 1,500-watt space heater on a 120-volt circuit pulls 12.5 amps, most of a 15-amp breaker by itself. That one line of arithmetic explains most tripped breakers.","v":{"t":"big","sub":"watts = volts × amps","n":"12.5 A","c":"1,500 W heater ÷ 120 V"}},{"h":"Why the same breaker keeps tripping","p":"Usually too much on one circuit: the heater and the hair dryer share a wall. Spread the big draws across rooms. A breaker that trips with nothing plugged in points to a short or a worn breaker, which is an electrician's job.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":" W","max":1800,"items":[["Hair dryer",1800],["Space heater",1500],["Microwave",1000],["Toaster",900],["Laptop",60],["Phone charger",10]]}},{"h":"GFCI: the outlet with the buttons","p":"A ground-fault circuit interrupter compares the current going out with the current coming back. A difference of about 5 milliamps, electricity leaking through water or through you, cuts the power in a fraction of a second. Codes require them near water: kitchens, baths, garages, outdoors.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Once a month, press TEST: the power should cut off","Press RESET: the power comes back","If it won't reset, the outlet has failed; replace it"]}},{"h":"AFCI: the breaker that listens for sparks","p":"An arc-fault breaker recognises the electrical signature of arcing, the sparking of a frayed cord or a loose connection that starts many house fires, and trips. Newer codes require them for bedrooms and living areas.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["GFCI","Guards against shock","Near water","Button on the outlet or breaker"],"b":["AFCI","Guards against fire","Living areas, bedrooms","Usually at the panel"]}},{"h":"Extension cords are temporary","p":"Cords are rated by wire gauge; a thin 16-gauge cord can't safely feed a heater. Never run a cord under a rug or plug one power strip into another. A strip is only a surge protector if it says so and prints a joule rating.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","Heavy 12- or 14-gauge cord for big loads"],["✗","Cords under rugs or through doorways"],["✗","A power strip plugged into a power strip"],["✓","Surge protector: look for the joule rating"]]}},{"h":"When to call an electrician","p":"Warm outlets or switch plates, a burning smell, lights that dim when an appliance starts, buzzing from the panel, or aluminum branch wiring from the late 1960s and 70s. Anything inside the panel is not a weekend project.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🔥","Warm plates"],["👃","Burning smell"],["💡","Dims on start"],["🐝","Buzzing panel"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A 1,500-watt heater on a 120-volt circuit draws about how much current?","a":["6 amps","12.5 amps","25 amps"],"c":1,"why":"Watts divided by volts is amps: 1,500 ÷ 120 = 12.5 amps, most of a 15-amp circuit on its own."},{"q":"A GFCI outlet is there to protect you from…","a":["Lightning strikes","Shock from current leaking to ground","High electric bills"],"c":1,"why":"It trips when the current going out and coming back differ by about 5 milliamps, which means some is leaking through water or a person."},{"q":"A breaker trips with nothing plugged into the circuit. The right move is to…","a":["Tape it in the ON position","Swap in a bigger breaker","Call an electrician"],"c":2,"why":"A bigger breaker lets the wire overheat. A breaker that trips on an empty circuit is reporting a fault."}],"take":["Watts = volts × amps; one space heater can fill a 15-amp circuit.","Test GFCI outlets monthly: TEST, then RESET.","Warm plates, burning smells and buzzing are calls to an electrician, not projects."],"src":[["Electrical Safety Foundation (ESFI)","https://www.esfi.org/"],["Residual-current device (GFCI) — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Residual-current_device"],["U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission","https://www.cpsc.gov/"]],"cat":"household"},{"id":"laundry-decoded","title":"Laundry, decoded","dek":"The care symbols, the temperatures and why towels go stiff","icon":"🧺","slides":[{"h":"The tag is a set of five symbols","p":"Every care label uses the same grammar: a tub for washing, a triangle for bleach, a square for drying, an iron for ironing and a circle for dry cleaning. Dots mean temperature, lines under a symbol mean a gentler cycle, and an X means don't.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🛁","Wash","dots = temperature"],["△","Bleach","crossed = none"],["◻","Dry","circle inside = tumble"],["♨","Iron","dots = heat"],["○","Dry clean","letters for the cleaner"]]}},{"h":"Dots are degrees","p":"One dot on the wash tub means cold (about 30 °C / 86 °F), two is warm (40 °C / 104 °F), three is hot (50 °C / 122 °F) and so on up to six. A hand in the tub means hand wash. The same dots on the iron mean low, medium and high heat.","v":{"t":"scale","min":20,"max":95,"unit":"°","zones":[[20,35,"cold · 1 dot"],[35,45,"warm · 2 dots"],[45,65,"hot · 3–4 dots"],[65,95,"very hot · 5–6"]],"marks":[[30,"most colours"],[60,"sheets, towels"]]}},{"h":"Cold water does most of the work","p":"Modern detergents are built for cold water, which keeps dyes in, slows shrinking and uses far less energy; heating the water is most of a wash cycle's electricity. Save hot water for sheets, towels and anything a sick person has used.","v":{"t":"donut","c":"energy per load","parts":[["heating water",90],["motor & pump",10]]}},{"h":"Sort by colour, then by weight","p":"Whites, lights and darks, because dye migrates, and heavy items (jeans, towels) apart from light ones (shirts, delicates), because heavy things need longer to dry and beat up thin fabric. Zip zippers, empty pockets, turn prints inside out.","v":{"t":"grid","cols":["Whites","Colours","Darks"],"rows":[["Water","hot or warm","cold","cold"],["Bleach","chlorine ok","oxygen only","none"],["Dry","medium","low","low, inside out"]]}},{"h":"Less detergent than you think","p":"The cap's lines are set for full, heavily soiled loads. Too much leaves residue that stiffens towels, traps odour and makes high-efficiency machines rinse extra. Start with half a cap; add more only if things come out dull.","v":{"t":"stack","c":"what a 'full cap' is designed for","parts":[["normal load",50],["heavy soil",25],["extra-large",25]]}},{"h":"Stains: act fast, start cold","p":"Blot, don't rub. Cold water first for protein stains (blood, egg, sweat): hot water cooks them in. Dish soap lifts grease; an oxygen bleach soak lifts coffee, wine and grass. Check that the stain is gone before the dryer sets it for good.","v":{"t":"grid","cols":["First","Then"],"rows":[["Blood, sweat","cold rinse","enzyme detergent"],["Grease, oil","dish soap","warm wash"],["Wine, coffee","cold rinse","oxygen bleach soak"],["Grass, mud","let dry, brush","enzyme + oxygen bleach"]]}},{"h":"The dryer is where clothes die","p":"Heat and tumbling shrink fibres and the lint trap is proof of wear. Dry on low or medium, pull things out slightly damp, and hang knits flat. Clean the lint screen every load: a clogged one lengthens drying and is a fire risk.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":" min","items":[["Air dry",240,"4 h"],["Low heat",60],["Medium heat",45],["High heat",35]]}},{"h":"Why towels go stiff, and the fix","p":"Fabric softener and detergent build up a waxy film that also makes towels less absorbent. Skip softener on towels, use less detergent, and once a month run them hot with a cup of white vinegar in the rinse, or a scoop of oxygen bleach.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Wash hot with no detergent and 1 cup of white vinegar","Wash again hot with ½ cup of baking soda","Dry on medium; shake out before folding"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two dots inside the wash tub symbol mean…","a":["Wash twice","Warm water, about 40 °C","Dry clean only"],"c":1,"why":"Dots are temperature: one is cold, two warm, three hot. Lines under the tub mean a gentler cycle."},{"q":"A fresh blood stain should first be rinsed in…","a":["Hot water","Cold water","Fabric softener"],"c":1,"why":"Protein stains set with heat. Cold water first, then an enzyme detergent."},{"q":"Towels have gone stiff and stopped absorbing. The likeliest cause is…","a":["Too little detergent","Build-up from softener and detergent","Washing in cold water"],"c":1,"why":"Softener and excess detergent coat the fibres. A hot wash with vinegar, then baking soda, strips the film."}],"take":["Tub, triangle, square, iron, circle: dots are heat, lines are gentleness, X is don't.","Cold water, half a cap of detergent, low heat in the dryer.","Cold first for protein stains; hot water cooks them in."],"src":[["Laundry symbol — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laundry_symbol"],["American Cleaning Institute: laundry basics","https://www.cleaninginstitute.org/"],["U.S. Department of Energy: laundry energy use","https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/laundry"]],"cat":"household"},{"id":"fridge-science","title":"Inside the refrigerator","dek":"How it makes cold, where things should go and what the numbers mean","icon":"🧊","slides":[{"h":"A fridge doesn't make cold, it moves heat","p":"A refrigerant circulates in a loop. The compressor squeezes it into a hot gas, the coils on the back shed that heat to the room, the liquid expands through a valve and turns bitterly cold inside the box, where it soaks up heat from your food, and the loop repeats.","v":{"t":"cycle","c":"the loop","items":["Compress","Shed heat (back coils)","Expand","Absorb heat (inside)"]}},{"h":"Two numbers to remember","p":"Keep the fridge at or below 40 °F (4 °C) and the freezer at 0 °F (−18 °C). Most bacteria multiply fastest between 40 and 140 °F. The dial often isn't a temperature at all; a $5 fridge thermometer tells you the truth.","v":{"t":"scale","min":-20,"max":50,"unit":"°F","zones":[[-20,0,"freezer"],[0,40,"fridge · safe"],[40,50,"danger zone"]],"marks":[[0,"freezer"],[37,"ideal fridge"]]}},{"h":"The door is the warmest shelf","p":"It swings into the kitchen several times a day. Condiments, juice and butter live there happily; milk and eggs don't. The back of the bottom shelf is the coldest place and the right home for raw meat, on a plate, where it can't drip.","v":{"t":"grid","cols":["Warmer","Colder"],"rows":[["Door","condiments, juice",""],["Top shelf","leftovers, drinks",""],["Middle","","dairy, eggs"],["Bottom","","raw meat, fish"],["Drawers","","produce (humidity)"]]}},{"h":"The crisper drawers have a dial for a reason","p":"High humidity (vent closed) keeps leafy greens and herbs from wilting. Low humidity (vent open) lets ethylene gas escape from apples, pears and stone fruit, which would otherwise ripen and rot their neighbours.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["High humidity","lettuce, spinach","herbs, broccoli","strawberries"],"b":["Low humidity","apples, pears","peaches, plums","avocados"]}},{"h":"Some things don't belong in there","p":"Cold ruins tomatoes and turns potato starch to sugar. Onions, garlic and winter squash want a cool, dry, dark cupboard. Bread goes stale faster in the fridge than on the counter; freeze it instead. Honey never spoils and only crystallises when chilled.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🍅","Tomatoes","counter"],["🥔","Potatoes","dark cupboard"],["🧅","Onions","dry, airy"],["🍞","Bread","counter or freezer"],["🍯","Honey","any shelf"],["🥑","Avocado","fridge once ripe"]]}},{"h":"Leftovers: two hours, four days","p":"Food that has sat out for more than two hours (one hour above 90 °F) is a gamble. Cooled and covered, most cooked leftovers keep three to four days; freeze what you won't eat by then. Shallow containers cool faster than one deep pot.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":" days","items":[["Cooked leftovers",4,"3–4 days"],["Opened deli meat",5,"3–5 days"],["Raw poultry",2,"1–2 days"],["Raw ground beef",2,"1–2 days"],["Hard cheese",28,"3–4 weeks"],["Eggs in shell",35,"3–5 weeks"]]}},{"h":"First in, first out","p":"Restaurant kitchens label and rotate so the oldest food gets used first. At home: new groceries go behind the old, leftovers get a date on the lid, and one shelf is the 'eat me soon' shelf you check before deciding what's for dinner.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["New groceries","go behind","older ones","eat-soon shelf","dinner"]}},{"h":"Keep it full, keep it clean","p":"A fuller fridge holds its temperature better when the door opens; fill gaps with jugs of water. Dust the coils once or twice a year, keep the door seal clean, and give it a few inches of breathing room so the heat it sheds can get away.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","A fridge thermometer, checked now and then"],["✓","Coils dusted twice a year"],["✓","A dollar bill held firm by the door seal"],["✗","Hot pots straight in: let them cool first"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The refrigerator should run at or below…","a":["50 °F / 10 °C","40 °F / 4 °C","32 °F / 0 °C"],"c":1,"why":"40 °F is the top of the safe range; the freezer should be at 0 °F. Above 40 °F bacteria multiply quickly."},{"q":"Raw chicken belongs…","a":["In the door","On the top shelf","On the bottom shelf, on a plate"],"c":2,"why":"The bottom back is the coldest spot, and nothing is underneath to drip onto."},{"q":"Cooked leftovers, cooled and covered, are good for about…","a":["1 day","3–4 days","2 weeks"],"c":1,"why":"Three to four days is the usual guidance; freeze anything you won't eat by then."}],"take":["40 °F fridge, 0 °F freezer; buy a thermometer.","Door warmest, bottom back coldest: raw meat low, milk in the middle.","Two hours out, then four days in."],"src":[["USDA: Refrigeration and food safety","https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/refrigeration"],["FDA: Are you storing food safely?","https://www.fda.gov/consumers/consumer-updates/are-you-storing-food-safely"],["Vapor-compression refrigeration — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vapor-compression_refrigeration"]],"cat":"household"},{"id":"home-emergencies","title":"Home emergencies: the first five minutes","dek":"Where the shutoffs are, what the smells mean, and when to just leave","icon":"🚨","slides":[{"h":"Find three valves before you need them","p":"The main water shutoff (usually where the pipe enters the house, near the meter or the water heater), the gas meter valve outside, and the main breaker at the top of the electrical panel. Walk the house once and show everyone who lives there.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🚰","Water main","where the pipe enters"],["🔥","Gas valve","at the meter, outside"],["⚡","Main breaker","top of the panel"]]}},{"h":"A burst pipe: water off, then power","p":"Close the main valve (clockwise, or a quarter turn so the lever is across the pipe). Open the lowest faucet to drain the line. If water is near outlets or the panel, cut the breakers, but don't stand in water to do it. Then call a plumber and start photographing for insurance.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Main water valve off","Open a low faucet to drain the pipes","Breakers off if water is near wiring","Photograph everything","Plumber, then insurer"]}},{"h":"A gas smell: leave, then call","p":"Natural gas is given its rotten-egg smell on purpose. If it's strong, don't flip switches, light anything or use a phone inside; open a door on the way out, get everyone away from the building and call the gas company or 911 from outside. Faint and localised near the stove? Check the burners are off and open windows.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✗","No switches, lighters or phones inside"],["✓","Everyone out, door left open"],["✓","Call the gas company or 911 from outside"],["✓","Don't go back until they say so"]]}},{"h":"Smoke alarms: one per level, test monthly","p":"Put them in every bedroom, outside sleeping areas and on every floor including the basement. Test monthly, change batteries yearly (or buy 10-year sealed units) and replace the whole alarm after ten years; the sensor wears out. Most fire deaths happen in homes with no working alarm.","v":{"t":"big","n":"10 yrs","c":"then replace the whole alarm, not just the battery"}},{"h":"Carbon monoxide: the alarm you can't smell","p":"CO comes from anything that burns: furnaces, water heaters, gas stoves, generators, cars in a garage. It causes headache, nausea and drowsiness before it kills. Install CO alarms near bedrooms, and never run a generator or grill indoors or in the garage, even with the door open.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🤕","Headache"],["🤢","Nausea"],["😴","Drowsy, confused"],["🚪","Get fresh air, call 911"]]}},{"h":"A kitchen fire: lid, not water","p":"Grease fires explode when water hits them. Turn off the heat, slide a lid or baking sheet over the pan and leave it. An oven fire: close the door and turn it off. If flames reach the cabinets or you hesitate, get out and call 911; a pan isn't worth it.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Do","Heat off","Lid or sheet over the pan","Baking soda on a small flame","Leave the lid on to cool"],"b":["Don't","Water on grease","Carry a flaming pan","Flour or sugar (they burn)","Open the oven door"]}},{"h":"Power out: fridge closed, generator outside","p":"A closed fridge keeps food safe about four hours, a full freezer about 48. Unplug electronics so the surge when power returns doesn't fry them. Generators run outside only, 20 feet from windows. Check whether the whole street is dark before calling the utility.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":" h","items":[["Fridge, closed",4],["Half-full freezer",24],["Full freezer",48]]}},{"h":"A kit by the door","p":"A flashlight that isn't your phone, a battery bank, a first-aid kit, a wrench that fits the gas valve, water (a gallon per person per day for three days), copies of IDs and insurance, and the phone numbers for the utilities written down, because the app needs a signal.","v":{"t":"list","items":["Flashlight and spare batteries","Battery bank for phones","First-aid kit and medications","Wrench for the gas valve, by the meter","3 days of water and food","Insurance and utility numbers on paper"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"You smell a strong gas odour in the kitchen. First move?","a":["Open windows and turn on the exhaust fan","Get everyone outside, then call from there","Flip the main breaker off"],"c":1,"why":"Any switch or spark can ignite it. Leave with the door open, then call the gas company or 911 from outside."},{"q":"A pan of oil catches fire on the stove. You should…","a":["Throw water on it","Carry it to the sink","Turn off the heat and cover it with a lid"],"c":2,"why":"Water makes burning oil erupt. Smother it, kill the heat and leave the lid on until it's cool."},{"q":"A smoke alarm should be replaced entirely after about…","a":["2 years","10 years","Never, just the battery"],"c":1,"why":"The sensor degrades. Batteries yearly, the whole unit at ten years; the date of manufacture is printed on the back."}],"take":["Know the water main, the gas valve and the main breaker before anything happens.","Gas smell: out first, call second. Grease fire: lid, never water.","Smoke alarms on every level; CO alarms near bedrooms; replace alarms at ten years."],"src":[["Ready.gov: Build a kit","https://www.ready.gov/kit"],["NFPA: Smoke alarms","https://www.nfpa.org/education-and-research/home-fire-safety/smoke-alarms"],["CDC: Carbon monoxide poisoning","https://www.cdc.gov/carbon-monoxide/"]],"cat":"household"},{"id":"cleaning-chemistry","title":"The chemistry under the sink","dek":"What soap actually does, what disinfecting means, and what never to mix","icon":"🧪","slides":[{"h":"Soap works by grabbing both sides","p":"A soap molecule has a water-loving head and an oil-loving tail. The tails bury themselves in grease, the heads face the water, and the grease is lifted off in tiny bundles (micelles) that rinse away. That is why water alone can't clean a greasy pan and soap can.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Tail grabs grease","Heads face water","Bundle forms","Rinses away"]}},{"h":"Clean, sanitize, disinfect: three different jobs","p":"Cleaning removes dirt and most germs with soap and water. Sanitizing lowers germs to a safe level. Disinfecting kills nearly all of them, and only works on a surface that has already been cleaned. For most of the house, cleaning is enough.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Clean: soap and water lift dirt and most germs","Sanitize: cut germs to a safe level (food surfaces)","Disinfect: kill nearly all of them (sick room, raw meat spill)"]}},{"h":"Disinfectants need time to work","p":"The label's 'contact time' is how long the surface must stay visibly wet: often four to ten minutes. A quick spray-and-wipe is cleaning, not disinfecting. Read the small print, let it sit, then wipe or let it dry.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":" min","items":[["Diluted bleach",1,"1 min"],["70% alcohol",1,"30 s–1 min"],["Quat sprays (most 'disinfecting' sprays)",10,"4–10 min"],["Hydrogen peroxide 3%",10,"up to 10 min"]]}},{"h":"Never mix these","p":"Bleach plus ammonia (in many glass cleaners) makes chloramine gas. Bleach plus vinegar or other acids releases chlorine gas. Bleach plus rubbing alcohol makes chloroform. Hydrogen peroxide plus vinegar makes peracetic acid. Use one product, rinse, then the other, with the window open.","v":{"t":"grid","cols":["+ Ammonia","+ Vinegar","+ Alcohol"],"rows":[["Bleach","chloramine gas","chlorine gas","chloroform"],["Peroxide","fine","peracetic acid","fine"]]}},{"h":"Acids for minerals, bases for grease","p":"Limescale, soap scum and rust are mineral deposits: vinegar, citric acid or a bathroom cleaner (acids) dissolve them. Grease and grime are organic: baking soda, degreasers and oven cleaners (bases) break them down. Match the cleaner to the mess and you scrub less.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Acids (vinegar, citric)","limescale on taps","soap scum","hard-water spots","not on stone or grout"],"b":["Bases (soda, degreaser)","greasy stovetop","oven","drains","not on aluminum"]}},{"h":"Vinegar is not a disinfectant","p":"It's a decent cleaner and a great descaler, but it doesn't reliably kill the germs that matter, and it etches marble, granite and grout and can cloud some finishes. Don't put it on natural stone, waxed wood or the rubber seals of appliances.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","Kettle, shower head, coffee maker descaling"],["✓","Glass and mirrors, diluted"],["✗","Marble, granite, travertine"],["✗","As a disinfectant for raw-chicken counters"]]}},{"h":"Microfibre beats paper","p":"Microfibre cloths are split fibres with far more surface area, so they lift dust and grease with water alone and trap it instead of pushing it around. Wash them without fabric softener, which clogs the fibres, and keep colours for kitchen, bath and floors.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🟦","Kitchen"],["🟩","Bathroom"],["🟨","Dusting"],["🟥","Floors"]]}},{"h":"A simple order for any room","p":"Top to bottom so dust falls onto surfaces you haven't done yet, dry before wet so you aren't making mud, and left to right so you don't miss a patch. Finish with the floor.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Declutter and open a window","Dust high to low","Wipe surfaces, cleanest to dirtiest","Disinfect the touch points: handles, switches, taps","Floors last"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Mixing bleach with an ammonia-based glass cleaner produces…","a":["A stronger cleaner","Toxic chloramine gas","Harmless salt water"],"c":1,"why":"Chloramine gas irritates the lungs and can be dangerous in a closed bathroom. One product at a time, rinse between."},{"q":"To actually disinfect a counter with a spray, you must…","a":["Spray and wipe immediately","Let it stay wet for the label's contact time","Use it on a dirty surface so it has something to kill"],"c":1,"why":"Disinfectants need minutes of wet contact, and work only on a surface that has already been cleaned."},{"q":"Limescale on a tap is best dissolved with…","a":["An acid such as vinegar or citric acid","A base such as baking soda","Bleach"],"c":0,"why":"Scale is a mineral deposit; acids dissolve it. Bases are for grease."}],"take":["Cleaning removes; disinfecting kills, and only on a clean surface, with time.","Bleach mixes with nothing: not ammonia, not vinegar, not alcohol.","Acids for scale, bases for grease; vinegar off the stone."],"src":[["CDC: Cleaning and disinfecting your home","https://www.cdc.gov/hygiene/about/when-and-how-to-clean-and-disinfect-your-home.html"],["American Cleaning Institute","https://www.cleaninginstitute.org/"],["Micelle — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Micelle"]],"cat":"household"},{"id":"heating-cooling","title":"How the heat and the AC work","dek":"The furnace, the heat pump, the filter and the thermostat","icon":"🌡","slides":[{"h":"A thermostat is just a switch with an opinion","p":"It compares the room to the setting and calls for heat or cooling. The system runs until the room overshoots by a degree or so, then rests. Programmable or smart thermostats save money by not heating an empty house, not by being clever about the furnace itself.","v":{"t":"cycle","c":"the control loop","items":["Room cools","Thermostat calls","Furnace runs","Room warms"]}},{"h":"A gas furnace in four parts","p":"Burners heat a metal heat exchanger; a blower pushes house air across it and through the ducts; exhaust leaves through a flue. Modern units are 90 to 98 percent efficient. The one part that must never crack is the exchanger, because that is what keeps the exhaust out of your air.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Gas burners","Heat exchanger","Blower","Ducts","Rooms"]}},{"h":"Air conditioning is a refrigerator for the house","p":"Same loop as the fridge: refrigerant absorbs heat indoors at the evaporator coil, the compressor pumps it outside, the condenser coil dumps the heat to the yard, and the fan you hear is blowing it away. Water dripping from the unit is humidity pulled out of the air, which is why rooms feel drier.","v":{"t":"cycle","c":"heat, moved outdoors","items":["Indoor coil absorbs","Compressor","Outdoor coil sheds","Expansion valve"]}},{"h":"A heat pump is an AC that runs backwards","p":"Flip a valve and the same loop pulls heat from the outdoor air, even cold air, and delivers it inside. Because it moves heat rather than making it, it delivers two to four units of heat per unit of electricity. Cold-climate models now work well below freezing; a backup heat strip covers the coldest nights.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"%","max":400,"items":[["Electric resistance heat",100],["Gas furnace (modern)",95],["Heat pump, mild day",350,"~300–400%"],["Heat pump, 17 °F",200,"~200%"]]}},{"h":"The filter is the cheapest fix in the house","p":"It protects the blower and coil from dust. A clogged filter starves the system of air, makes it run longer, and can freeze the coil. Check it monthly; most need changing every one to three months. MERV 8 to 11 balances filtration and airflow for most homes.","v":{"t":"scale","min":1,"max":16,"unit":"","zones":[[1,5,"basic dust"],[5,8,"pollen, mould"],[8,12,"most homes"],[12,16,"hospital; check airflow"]],"marks":[[8,"MERV 8"],[11,"MERV 11"]]}},{"h":"Where the money goes","p":"Heating and cooling are roughly half of a typical home's energy bill. The big levers are a tight envelope (attic insulation, sealing leaks), a sensible setback when you're out or asleep, and a system that is maintained, not a system that is new.","v":{"t":"donut","c":"typical home energy","parts":[["heating & cooling",50],["water heating",15],["appliances, lights, electronics",25],["everything else",10]]}},{"h":"Set it and leave it","p":"Turning the thermostat way up doesn't heat the house faster; the furnace has one speed. A setback of 7 to 10 degrees for eight hours a day can cut heating and cooling costs by up to about ten percent a year. Ceiling fans should push air down in summer and pull it up in winter.","v":{"t":"big","n":"~10%","c":"saved by setting back 7–10 °F for 8 hours a day"}},{"h":"A once-a-year checklist","p":"Change the filter, hose the outdoor unit's fins (power off first), keep two feet clear around it, clear the condensate drain, vacuum the supply registers, and have a technician check the heat exchanger and refrigerant charge. Noises, short cycling and ice on the lines are calls to make now, not in July.","v":{"t":"list","items":["Filter every 1–3 months","Outdoor unit rinsed and clear","Condensate drain flowing","Registers open and vacuumed","Technician: exchanger, charge, safety switches"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A heat pump heats a house by…","a":["Burning gas","Resistance coils like a toaster","Moving heat from outdoor air indoors"],"c":2,"why":"It's an air conditioner run in reverse. Because it moves heat instead of making it, it delivers more heat energy than the electricity it uses."},{"q":"The furnace filter should be checked…","a":["Every month","Once a year","Only when the system fails"],"c":0,"why":"Monthly checks, changes every one to three months. A clogged filter strains the blower and can freeze the coil."},{"q":"Setting the thermostat to 85 °F will heat a cold room…","a":["Much faster","At exactly the same speed as 70 °F","Slower"],"c":1,"why":"A single-stage furnace is on or off. It will just keep going past 70 and waste energy."}],"take":["Heating and cooling are about half the energy bill.","Filters monthly; MERV 8–11 for most homes.","A heat pump is an AC with a reversing valve, two to four times as efficient as resistance heat."],"src":[["U.S. Department of Energy: Home heating systems","https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/home-heating-systems"],["U.S. Department of Energy: Heat pump systems","https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/heat-pump-systems"],["ENERGY STAR: HVAC maintenance","https://www.energystar.gov/"]],"cat":"household"},{"id":"plumbing-basics","title":"How the plumbing works","dek":"Traps, vents, the water heater and the toilet that won't stop running","icon":"🚿","slides":[{"h":"Two systems that never touch","p":"Supply lines bring pressurised clean water in (about 40 to 60 psi). Drain-waste-vent pipes carry used water out by gravity alone, which is why they slope and are much larger. Every fixture sits where the two meet.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Supply","under pressure","small pipes (½–¾ in)","copper or PEX","shutoff at each fixture"],"b":["Drain & vent","gravity, sloped ¼ in per foot","big pipes (1½–4 in)","PVC or cast iron","vents out the roof"]}},{"h":"The P-trap is a water seal","p":"The U-bend under every sink holds a plug of water that blocks sewer gas from coming up the drain. A drain that smells after a vacation has usually just dried out; run the tap. The trap also catches rings and earrings, which is why it unscrews by hand.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Water flows down and around the U-bend","Some stays behind as a seal","Sewer gas can't get past the seal","Dry trap = smell; run water to refill"]}},{"h":"Why there's a pipe sticking out of the roof","p":"Vents let air into the drain system so water can fall freely, like the second hole in a juice can. Without them, draining water would suck the traps dry. A gurgling sink or a slow drain everywhere at once often means a blocked vent, not a blocked pipe.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🫧","Gurgle","air pulled through a trap"],["🐢","Slow everywhere","vent, not clog"],["🦨","Sewer smell","trap siphoned dry"]]}},{"h":"A running toilet is almost always the flapper","p":"Lift the tank lid. The rubber flapper seals the hole to the bowl; when it wears, water trickles out and the fill valve keeps topping up. A new flapper costs a few dollars. If the water level is above the overflow tube, adjust the float instead.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Put food colouring in the tank; wait 15 minutes","Colour in the bowl? The flapper leaks","Water over the overflow tube? Lower the float","Hissing fill valve? Replace it (also cheap)"]}},{"h":"The water heater: 120 degrees","p":"Set the thermostat to 120 °F (49 °C): hot enough for dishes and showers, cool enough to slow scalding and mineral build-up, and it saves energy. Drain a few gallons from the bottom valve once a year to flush sediment. Tank heaters last about ten to fifteen years.","v":{"t":"scale","min":100,"max":160,"unit":"°F","zones":[[100,115,"lukewarm"],[115,125,"recommended"],[125,140,"scald risk rises"],[140,160,"severe burns in seconds"]],"marks":[[120,"set here"]]}},{"h":"Chemical drain cleaners are a last resort","p":"They can damage old pipes, and if they don't clear the clog you're left with a sink full of caustic liquid. Try a plunger with the overflow hole covered, then a drain snake. Grease belongs in the trash, not the drain; it cools and sets like candle wax in the pipe.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","Plunger, overflow covered, firm strokes"],["✓","Hand snake or a zip strip for hair"],["✓","Boiling water for soap and grease (not PVC-only)"],["✗","Grease, wipes, 'flushable' anything down the drain"]]}},{"h":"Every fixture has its own shutoff","p":"Under sinks and behind toilets are small valves. Turn them off before any repair and once a year just to keep them from seizing. If a valve won't close or drips when turned, close the main and replace it: a stuck valve is the one you'll need at 2 a.m.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🚽","Behind the toilet"],["🚰","Under each sink"],["🧺","Behind the washer"],["🔥","On the water heater"]]}},{"h":"Frozen pipes and water hammer","p":"Pipes on outside walls freeze first; in a hard freeze, let a thread of water run and open the cabinet doors. If a pipe freezes, open the faucet and warm it slowly from the faucet back; never with a flame. Banging when a tap closes is water hammer: an arrestor or draining the system to refill the air chambers quiets it.","v":{"t":"grid","cols":["Sign","Do"],"rows":[["Freeze","no flow in cold snap","open tap, warm gently"],["Hammer","bang when tap shuts","arrestor, re-air chambers"],["Low pressure","one fixture only","clean the aerator"],["Low pressure","whole house","check main valve, regulator"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The water held in the P-trap under a sink is there to…","a":["Slow the drain","Block sewer gas","Catch grease"],"c":1,"why":"The plug of water is a seal. When a trap dries out the room smells; running the tap refills it."},{"q":"A toilet that runs on and off all day most likely needs…","a":["A new tank","A new flapper","A plumber immediately"],"c":1,"why":"A worn flapper lets water trickle into the bowl and the fill valve keeps topping up. The dye test confirms it."},{"q":"The recommended water heater setting is…","a":["105 °F","120 °F","150 °F"],"c":1,"why":"120 °F limits scalding, slows scale build-up and saves energy; most tanks ship set higher."}],"take":["Supply is pressure; drains are gravity and need vents to breathe.","Smell at the drain: refill the trap. Running toilet: flapper first.","Water heater at 120 °F; flush sediment yearly; exercise the shutoffs."],"src":[["Trap (plumbing) — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trap_(plumbing)"],["U.S. Department of Energy: Water heating","https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/water-heating"],["EPA WaterSense: Fix a leak","https://www.epa.gov/watersense/fix-leak-week"]],"cat":"household"},{"id":"recycle-compost","title":"What goes in which bin","dek":"Recycling without wishful thinking, and a compost pile that doesn't stink","icon":"♻️","slides":[{"h":"Rules are local, but the big ones travel","p":"Programs differ by city, so check yours. Nearly everywhere takes clean paper and cardboard, metal cans, glass bottles and jars, and plastic bottles and jugs. Nearly nowhere wants plastic bags, food-soiled paper, or anything smaller than a credit card.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Almost always yes","cardboard, flattened","paper, mail, magazines","aluminum & steel cans","bottles, jugs, jars"],"b":["Almost always no","plastic bags & film","greasy pizza box bottoms","cups with wax or foam","batteries, cords, tanks"]}},{"h":"The number inside the triangle isn't a promise","p":"The resin code (1 to 7) says what kind of plastic it is, not that your program recycles it. Bottles and jugs stamped 1 (PET) and 2 (HDPE) have real markets. Tubs marked 5 (PP) sometimes do. Films, foam (6) and mixed 'other' (7) mostly don't.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":100,"items":[["#1 PET bottles",90,"widely accepted"],["#2 HDPE jugs",90,"widely accepted"],["#5 PP tubs",50,"check locally"],["#3, #4, #6, #7",10,"rarely"]]}},{"h":"Empty, clean, dry","p":"A little residue is fine; a half-full yogurt cup contaminates the paper around it. Rinse, shake out, and leave caps on bottles (they're captured in the process now). Don't bag recyclables: bags jam the sorting machinery and workers pull them out by hand.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Empty it","Quick rinse, no need to scrub","Caps on, labels can stay","Loose in the bin, never bagged"]}},{"h":"Wish-cycling makes things worse","p":"Tossing a doubtful item in 'just in case' sends it down a line built for something else. Plastic bags wrap around sorting drums; batteries start fires in trucks and facilities. When in doubt, throw it out, and take bags back to the grocery store bin.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🛍","Bags","store drop-off"],["🔋","Batteries","hardware store, hazmat day"],["💡","Bulbs","hazmat; LEDs in trash"],["📱","Electronics","e-waste events"]]}},{"h":"Compost is browns plus greens","p":"Carbon-rich 'browns' (dry leaves, cardboard, paper, straw) and nitrogen-rich 'greens' (fruit and vegetable scraps, coffee grounds, grass clippings), roughly two to three parts brown to one green by volume. Too many greens and it smells; too many browns and nothing happens.","v":{"t":"stack","c":"by volume","parts":[["browns: leaves, cardboard, paper",70],["greens: scraps, grounds, grass",30]]}},{"h":"What stays out of a backyard pile","p":"Meat, fish, dairy and oily food draw animals and go rancid. Pet waste and diseased plants carry pathogens. Glossy paper and 'compostable' plastics need an industrial facility's heat. Eggshells, tea bags without staples and paper towels are fine.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","Fruit & veg scraps, coffee grounds, eggshells"],["✓","Leaves, shredded cardboard, plain paper"],["✗","Meat, bones, dairy, cooking oil"],["✗","Dog or cat waste"],["✗","'Compostable' cups and forks (industrial only)"]]}},{"h":"Air and water are the other ingredients","p":"The microbes doing the work need the pile as damp as a wrung-out sponge and turned every week or two to let oxygen in. A hot, working pile reaches 130 to 160 °F in the middle and finishes in a few months; a lazy pile just takes a year.","v":{"t":"cycle","c":"every 1–2 weeks","items":["Add scraps","Cover with browns","Water if dry","Turn for air"]}},{"h":"Reduce still beats recycle","p":"Recycling saves energy (an aluminum can from recycled metal takes about 95 percent less than from ore), but about a third of what goes in a bin is contaminated or unrecyclable. The bigger win is less packaging in the first place: refills, bulk, and the bag you already own.","v":{"t":"big","n":"95%","c":"less energy to make a can from recycled aluminum than from ore"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Plastic grocery bags should go…","a":["In the curbside recycling bin","Back to the store's drop-off bin or the trash","In with cardboard"],"c":1,"why":"Bags and film jam the sorting machines. Grocery stores collect them separately."},{"q":"A healthy backyard compost pile is mostly…","a":["Kitchen scraps","Dry 'browns' like leaves and cardboard","Grass clippings"],"c":1,"why":"Roughly two to three parts browns to one part greens. Too many greens and it turns slimy and smells."},{"q":"The number in the recycling triangle on a plastic container tells you…","a":["That it is recyclable","What kind of plastic it is","How many times it's been recycled"],"c":1,"why":"It's a resin code. Whether your program accepts it is a separate question; bottles and jugs (1 and 2) usually, films and foam rarely."}],"take":["Empty, clean, dry, loose: never bagged.","The triangle number is a material, not a promise; 1 and 2 bottles and jugs are the sure things.","Compost: two parts brown to one green, damp as a sponge, turned for air; no meat or dairy."],"src":[["EPA: How do I recycle common recyclables?","https://www.epa.gov/recycle/how-do-i-recycle-common-recyclables"],["EPA: Composting at home","https://www.epa.gov/recycle/composting-home"],["Resin identification code — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resin_identification_code"]],"cat":"household"},{"id":"knife-skills","title":"Knife skills in eight cards","dek":"The grip, the claw, the three cuts and why a dull knife is the dangerous one","icon":"🔪","slides":[{"h":"One good knife does almost everything","p":"An 8-inch chef's knife handles ninety percent of kitchen work. Add a small paring knife for peeling and a serrated bread knife for crusts and tomatoes, and you're equipped. Price matters less than keeping it sharp.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🔪","Chef's, 8 in","nearly everything"],["🗡","Paring","peeling, trimming"],["🍞","Serrated","bread, tomatoes"]]}},{"h":"Pinch the blade, don't hold the handle","p":"Pinch the blade just ahead of the handle between thumb and the side of your bent index finger, with the other three fingers around the handle. It feels odd for a day and then gives you control the handle grip never will.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Thumb on one side of the blade's heel","Bent index finger on the other","Remaining fingers wrap the handle","Wrist loose, elbow does the lifting"]}},{"h":"The other hand makes a claw","p":"Curl the fingertips of your guiding hand under so the knuckles face the blade. The flat of the blade rides against the knuckles; the fingertips are tucked out of reach. Move the claw back a little for each slice and the knife follows.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","Fingertips tucked, knuckles forward"],["✓","Blade's flat side touches the knuckles"],["✓","Thumb and pinky hold the food from behind"],["✗","Fingers flat on the board, pointing at the blade"]]}},{"h":"Rock, don't chop","p":"Keep the tip on the board and rock the blade down and forward through the food in one motion, then lift the heel and bring it back. Slicing forward does the cutting; pushing straight down crushes. Let the weight of the knife work.","v":{"t":"cycle","c":"one stroke","items":["Tip on board","Heel rocks down","Push slightly forward","Lift heel, reset"]}},{"h":"Three cuts cover most recipes","p":"A dice is cubes: small (¼ in), medium (½ in), large (¾ in). Julienne is matchsticks, about 2 inches by ⅛ inch. Chiffonade is leafy herbs stacked, rolled and sliced into ribbons. Even sizes matter more than tiny sizes: even pieces cook at the same rate.","v":{"t":"grid","cols":["Shape","Size","Use"],"rows":[["Dice","cubes","¼–¾ in","onions, carrots, potatoes"],["Julienne","matchsticks","2 × ⅛ in","stir-fries, slaws"],["Chiffonade","ribbons","thin","basil, mint, kale"]]}},{"h":"Make a flat side first","p":"A round thing rolls; a rolling thing cuts you. Halve the onion, trim a slice off the carrot, cut the squash in two so it sits flat. Then everything you do is on a stable base. The same goes for the board: a damp towel underneath stops it skating.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Round & rolling","One flat cut","Flat side down","Safe, even slices"]}},{"h":"A dull knife is the dangerous one","p":"A dull edge needs force and slips off the skin of a tomato instead of biting. Hone before each use (the steel realigns the edge; it doesn't sharpen) and sharpen when honing stops helping, every few months with a whetstone or a decent pull-through sharpener.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Honing (steel)","before each use","straightens the edge","20 seconds","no metal removed"],"b":["Sharpening (stone)","every few months","grinds a new edge","15–20° per side","removes a little metal"]}},{"h":"Boards, washing and the one rule","p":"Wood or plastic are both fine if they're cleaned; a separate board for raw meat is the real safeguard. Wash knives by hand right away and put them back; a knife in a sink of suds is a trap. Never try to catch a falling knife. Step back, let it land.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","Separate board for raw meat"],["✓","Hand wash, dry, put away at once"],["✓","Carry it point down, blade back"],["✗","Dishwasher: dulls the edge, loosens handles"],["✗","Catching a falling knife"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The safest way to hold the food you're cutting is…","a":["Fingers flat, pointing at the blade","A claw, fingertips tucked, knuckles against the blade","Holding it in the air"],"c":1,"why":"The knuckles become a guide for the flat of the blade and the fingertips are out of its path."},{"q":"Running a knife along a honing steel…","a":["Grinds a new edge","Realigns the existing edge","Is only for serrated knives"],"c":1,"why":"Honing straightens the microscopic edge that folds over in use. Sharpening on a stone removes metal to create a new edge."},{"q":"A knife slips from your hand toward the floor. You should…","a":["Catch it by the handle","Step back and let it fall","Kick it away"],"c":1,"why":"Hands and feet out of the way. A knife can be picked up; a cut tendon can't be undone."}],"take":["Pinch grip on the blade, claw on the food, rock the tip on the board.","Flat side first: nothing rolls.","Hone every time, sharpen every few months; the dull knife is the one that cuts you."],"src":[["Chef's knife — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chef%27s_knife"],["Serious Eats: Knife skills","https://www.seriouseats.com/knife-skills"],["Julienning — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julienning"]],"cat":"kitchen"},{"id":"nutrition-label","title":"How to read a nutrition label","dek":"The serving-size trap, the 5/20 rule and the one line that changed","icon":"🏷","slides":[{"h":"Start at the top: the serving","p":"Every number on the label is per serving, and the serving is what people typically eat, not what they should. A 20-ounce bottle may be 2.5 servings. Multiply by what you actually ate before anything else makes sense.","v":{"t":"big","n":"× 2.5","c":"a 20 oz soda is often 2.5 servings; multiply everything"}},{"h":"Calories are big for a reason","p":"The redesigned U.S. label puts calories in large bold type. Two thousand a day is the reference used for the percentages; most people need somewhere between 1,600 and 3,000 depending on size, age and activity.","v":{"t":"stack","c":"where a day's calories commonly come from","parts":[["carbohydrate 45–65%",55],["fat 20–35%",28],["protein 10–35%",17]]}},{"h":"The 5/20 rule","p":"Percent Daily Value (%DV) is on the right. Five percent or less is low; twenty percent or more is high. Aim low for saturated fat, sodium and added sugars; aim high for fiber, vitamin D, calcium, iron and potassium.","v":{"t":"scale","min":0,"max":40,"unit":"%","zones":[[0,5,"low"],[5,20,"moderate"],[20,40,"high"]],"marks":[[5,"5% low"],[20,"20% high"]]}},{"h":"Added sugars finally have their own line","p":"Since 2020, labels separate the sugar that occurs naturally (in milk, fruit) from sugar that was added. The daily value for added sugar is 50 grams; a can of soda can use most of it. Four grams is roughly a teaspoon.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":" g","max":50,"items":[["Daily value",50,"50 g (about 12 tsp)"],["Can of cola",39],["Flavored yogurt",15],["Ketchup, 1 tbsp",4],["Plain milk",0,"0 added"]]}},{"h":"Sodium hides in bread, not just chips","p":"The limit most guidelines use is 2,300 milligrams a day, about a teaspoon of salt. More than seventy percent of it comes from packaged and restaurant food: bread, deli meat, soup, sauces, cheese. Look at the mg, not just the taste.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":" mg","max":2300,"items":[["Daily limit",2300],["Canned soup, 1 cup",800],["Deli turkey, 3 oz",700],["Frozen pizza, 1 slice",600],["Slice of bread",150]]}},{"h":"Fats: which kind matters more than how much","p":"Saturated fat (meat, butter, coconut) is listed with a daily value of 20 grams; trans fat should be zero. Unsaturated fats (olive oil, nuts, fish) aren't limited and aren't always listed. Low-fat often means more sugar; read the whole panel.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Limit","saturated: DV 20 g","trans: avoid","butter, fatty meat, coconut oil"],"b":["No limit on the label","mono- and polyunsaturated","olive oil, avocado","nuts, seeds, fish"]}},{"h":"Fiber and protein: the lines to want more of","p":"The fiber daily value is 28 grams and most people get about half. Whole grains, beans, fruit and vegetables carry it. Protein has no %DV unless a claim is made; 15 to 20 grams per meal is a common target.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":" g","max":28,"items":[["Fiber DV",28],["Average U.S. intake",15],["Cup of lentils",16],["Apple with skin",4],["White bread, slice",1]]}},{"h":"The ingredient list is in order of weight","p":"The first ingredient is the biggest. If sugar (or any of its names: corn syrup, dextrose, cane juice, maltose) is in the top three, it's a sweet. Short lists you can picture are usually a good sign. 'Natural flavors' is a flavor you aren't told about.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Most by weight","…","Least by weight"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A %DV of 25% for sodium on a label means the serving is…","a":["Low in sodium","High in sodium","About average"],"c":1,"why":"Twenty percent or more is high; five percent or less is low. Sodium is one to aim low on."},{"q":"Ingredients on a label are listed…","a":["Alphabetically","By weight, heaviest first","In the order they were added"],"c":1,"why":"The first ingredient is the largest share. Sugar near the top means a sugary product, whatever the front says."},{"q":"The 'Added Sugars' line counts…","a":["All sugar in the food","Only sugar from fruit and milk","Sugar put in during processing"],"c":2,"why":"Natural sugar in milk or fruit is in 'Total Sugars'; the added line is what the maker put in. Daily value: 50 grams."}],"take":["Serving size first; multiply by what you ate.","5% is low, 20% is high: low for saturated fat, sodium and added sugar, high for fiber.","Ingredients are listed by weight; sugar in the top three means dessert."],"src":[["FDA: How to understand and use the Nutrition Facts label","https://www.fda.gov/food/nutrition-facts-label/how-understand-and-use-nutrition-facts-label"],["Dietary Guidelines for Americans","https://www.dietaryguidelines.gov/"],["CDC: Sodium","https://www.cdc.gov/salt/"]],"cat":"kitchen"},{"id":"coffee-bean-to-cup","title":"Coffee, from bean to cup","dek":"Two species, four roasts, one ratio and why your brew tastes sour or bitter","icon":"☕","slides":[{"h":"Two beans run the world","p":"Arabica is about sixty percent of production: sweeter, more acidic, grown high and cool. Robusta is hardier, higher in caffeine (about twice as much), harsher, and the base of most instant coffee and many espresso blends for its crema and punch.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Arabica","~60% of the world's coffee","sweet, fruity, acidic","about 1.2–1.5% caffeine","grown at altitude"],"b":["Robusta","~40%","earthy, bitter, bold","about 2.2–2.7% caffeine","hardy, low altitude"]}},{"h":"A coffee 'bean' is a fruit seed","p":"The cherry is picked, the fruit removed (washed, or dried on the seed as a 'natural'), the green seeds dried, sorted and shipped. Everything you taste as 'coffee' comes later, in the roaster, in about ten to fifteen minutes.","v":{"t":"flow","items":[["🍒","Cherry"],["🫧","Process"],["🌾","Green bean"],["🔥","Roast"],["☕","Brew"]]}},{"h":"Roast is a dial from bright to burnt","p":"Light roasts stop soon after the beans first crack, keeping the origin's acidity and fruit. Medium balances sweetness and body. Dark goes past the second crack: oily, smoky, bitter, with the origin mostly gone. Darker roasts have slightly less caffeine by volume, not more.","v":{"t":"scale","min":0,"max":100,"unit":"","zones":[[0,30,"light · fruity, acidic"],[30,60,"medium · balanced"],[60,80,"medium-dark"],[80,100,"dark · smoky, bitter"]],"marks":[[22,"first crack"],[72,"second crack"]]}},{"h":"Grind to match the method","p":"The finer the grind, the faster water pulls flavor out. Espresso's 25-second shot needs powder; a French press steeping for four minutes needs coarse grounds that won't slip through the mesh. Buy whole beans and grind just before brewing; ground coffee goes stale in days.","v":{"t":"grid","cols":["Grind","Time"],"rows":[["Espresso","fine, like sugar","25–30 s"],["Pour-over","medium-fine","2½–3½ min"],["Drip machine","medium","4–6 min"],["French press","coarse","4 min"],["Cold brew","extra coarse","12–18 h"]]}},{"h":"The golden ratio","p":"About one gram of coffee to sixteen grams of water, give or take: 60 grams per litre, or two level tablespoons per 6-ounce cup. A kitchen scale ends the guessing. Stronger coffee means more grounds, not a darker roast or a longer brew.","v":{"t":"big","n":"1 : 16","c":"coffee to water by weight; about 60 g per litre"}},{"h":"Water: hot, but not boiling","p":"Brew between about 195 and 205 °F (90–96 °C). Boiling water scorches the grounds; water much cooler under-extracts. Off the boil for thirty seconds is right. Coffee is 98 percent water, so if your tap tastes bad, so will the cup; filtered is better than distilled.","v":{"t":"scale","min":170,"max":212,"unit":"°F","zones":[[170,195,"under-extracts"],[195,205,"sweet spot"],[205,212,"scorches"]],"marks":[[200,"aim here"]]}},{"h":"Sour means under, bitter means over","p":"Extraction pulls out acids first, then sugars, then bitter compounds. Sour, thin and salty means you stopped early: grind finer, brew longer, water hotter. Bitter, harsh and drying means you went too far: grind coarser, brew shorter. Adjust one thing at a time.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Sour, thin (under)","grind finer","brew longer","hotter water","more coffee"],"b":["Bitter, harsh (over)","grind coarser","brew shorter","slightly cooler water","less coffee"]}},{"h":"Fresh beats fancy","p":"Roasted coffee peaks from a few days to about a month after the roast date; after that the aromatics fade. Buy small bags with a roast date, keep them sealed and dark at room temperature, and skip the fridge, where beans pick up moisture and smells.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[0,60],[3,95],[10,100],[21,85],[30,65],[45,40],[60,25]],"xl":"days since roast","yl":"flavor","l1":"peak 1–3 wks"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your coffee tastes sour and thin. The usual fix is to…","a":["Grind coarser and brew shorter","Grind finer and brew a little longer","Use less coffee"],"c":1,"why":"Sour means under-extraction: the acids came out but the sugars didn't. More contact time or a finer grind pulls the sweetness through."},{"q":"Which has more caffeine per bean?","a":["Arabica","Robusta","They're identical"],"c":1,"why":"Robusta carries roughly twice the caffeine of arabica, part of why it's harsher and more bitter."},{"q":"A French press needs grounds that are…","a":["Fine, like espresso","Coarse","Pre-ground from the supermarket"],"c":1,"why":"Four minutes of steeping extracts slowly, and coarse grounds stay behind the mesh plunger."}],"take":["1:16 by weight, water just off the boil, grind matched to the method.","Sour is under-extracted, bitter is over; change one variable at a time.","Whole beans, roast date on the bag, used within a month."],"src":[["Coffee roasting — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coffee_roasting"],["Specialty Coffee Association: brewing resources","https://sca.coffee/"],["Coffea arabica — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coffea_arabica"]],"cat":"kitchen"},{"id":"food-safety-temps","title":"The temperatures that keep you safe","dek":"The danger zone, the thermometer numbers and the two-hour rule","icon":"🌡️","slides":[{"h":"The danger zone: 40 to 140","p":"Between 40 °F and 140 °F (4–60 °C) bacteria can double every twenty minutes. Cold storage keeps food below it; cooking takes food above it; the time in between is what makes people sick. Nearly every rule on these cards is about that window.","v":{"t":"scale","min":0,"max":180,"unit":"°F","zones":[[0,40,"cold · safe"],[40,140,"danger zone"],[140,180,"hot · safe"]],"marks":[[40,"fridge max"],[140,"hot holding"]]}},{"h":"Two hours, or one when it's hot","p":"Perishable food (meat, dairy, cooked rice, cut fruit) that sits out more than two hours should be thrown away; one hour if the room or the picnic is above 90 °F. The clock includes the time on the counter before and after the meal.","v":{"t":"big","n":"2 h","c":"out of the fridge; 1 hour above 90 °F"}},{"h":"A thermometer, not a color","p":"Juices and pink centers lie; burgers brown before they're safe and chicken can stay pink when done. Push an instant-read thermometer into the thickest part, away from bone, and read it. It is the single most useful cheap tool in the kitchen.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":" °F","max":165,"items":[["Poultry, leftovers, casseroles",165],["Ground meat, egg dishes",160],["Pork, beef, lamb, veal (whole cuts)",145,"145 °F + 3 min rest"],["Fish",145],["Ham, precooked",140]]}},{"h":"Thaw in the fridge, not on the counter","p":"A frozen chicken left out thaws from the outside in; the surface spends hours in the danger zone while the middle is still ice. Thaw in the fridge (a day per 4–5 pounds), in cold water changed every 30 minutes, or in the microwave if you'll cook it right away.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","Fridge: slow, safest, can refreeze"],["✓","Cold water, sealed bag, changed every 30 min"],["✓","Microwave, then cook immediately"],["✗","On the counter or in the sink overnight"]]}},{"h":"Don't wash the chicken","p":"Rinsing raw poultry spreads bacteria up to three feet around the sink in droplets; cooking kills them, water doesn't. Wash your hands for 20 seconds, the board, the knife and the counter instead. Produce, on the other hand, does get rinsed under running water.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Rinse","fruit & vegetables","canned beans","rice & grains"],"b":["Don't rinse","raw chicken & turkey","raw meat","eggs (in the U.S.)"]}},{"h":"Cross-contamination is the quiet culprit","p":"Raw juices on a board, a knife or your hands move onto food that won't be cooked. Separate boards (or wash between), raw meat on the bottom fridge shelf, a clean plate for the cooked burgers, never the one they came out on.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Raw meat on its own board","Wash hands, board and knife before touching anything else","Cooked food onto a clean plate","Marinade that touched raw meat is not a sauce (boil it, or make fresh)"]}},{"h":"Cool fast, reheat hot","p":"A deep pot of soup can sit in the danger zone for hours in the fridge. Split it into shallow containers, leave the lids loose until cold, and it's safe. Reheat leftovers to 165 °F, steaming throughout; stir microwaved food, which heats unevenly.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Shallow containers","Fridge within 2 h","Eat within 3–4 days","Reheat to 165 °F"]}},{"h":"Rice and the bacteria that survive cooking","p":"Bacillus cereus spores live through cooking and multiply in rice left warm. Serve it hot, cool it within an hour and refrigerate, and eat it within a day. The same goes for pasta and cooked grains. Reheated rice is fine; rice that sat out warm isn't.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🍚","Serve hot"],["❄️","Cool within 1 h"],["📅","Eat within a day"],["♨️","Reheat steaming"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The food-safety danger zone is…","a":["0–32 °F","40–140 °F","140–212 °F"],"c":1,"why":"Between 40 and 140 °F bacteria multiply fast. Keep cold food below it and hot food above it."},{"q":"Chicken is safe to eat at an internal temperature of…","a":["145 °F","155 °F","165 °F"],"c":2,"why":"165 °F in the thickest part, away from bone. Color and juices are not reliable."},{"q":"The best way to thaw a frozen roast is…","a":["On the counter overnight","In the fridge, a day per 4–5 pounds","In hot water"],"c":1,"why":"The counter leaves the surface in the danger zone for hours. The fridge, cold water or the microwave keep it safe."}],"take":["40–140 °F is the danger zone; two hours is the limit outside the fridge.","Thermometer, not color: 165 poultry, 160 ground, 145 whole cuts and fish.","Don't wash the chicken; do wash your hands, the board and the knife."],"src":[["USDA: Safe minimum internal temperatures","https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/safe-temperature-chart"],["USDA: The 'Danger Zone' (40 °F–140 °F)","https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/danger-zone-40f-140f"],["CDC: Four steps to food safety","https://www.cdc.gov/food-safety/prevention/"]],"cat":"kitchen"},{"id":"compound-interest","title":"Compound interest","dek":"Why time matters more than the amount, and the rule of 72","icon":"📈","slides":[{"h":"Interest on the interest","p":"Simple interest pays on the original sum. Compound interest pays on the sum plus everything it has already earned, so each year's growth is a little bigger than the last. Over a few years the difference is small; over decades it is everything.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[0,1000],[5,1403],[10,1967],[15,2759],[20,3870],[25,5427],[30,7612]],"pts2":[[0,1000],[10,1700],[20,2400],[30,3100]],"xl":"years","l1":"7% compound: $7,612","l2":"simple: $3,100"}},{"h":"The rule of 72","p":"Divide 72 by the annual rate to get roughly how many years money takes to double. At 7 percent, about ten years; at 3 percent, about twenty-four. It works in reverse for prices: 3 percent inflation halves what a dollar buys in about 24 years.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":" yrs","max":36,"items":[["2%",36,"36 years"],["4%",18,"18 years"],["6%",12,"12 years"],["8%",9,"9 years"],["12%",6,"6 years"]]}},{"h":"Starting early beats saving more","p":"At 7 percent, $200 a month from age 25 to 35 and then nothing grows to more than $200 a month from 35 to 65. Ten years of contributions beat thirty, because the early money had the longest to compound.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Starts at 25, stops at 35","$200/month × 10 years","$24,000 put in","about $270,000 at 65"],"b":["Starts at 35, stops at 65","$200/month × 30 years","$72,000 put in","about $245,000 at 65"]}},{"h":"Where the money comes from, year by year","p":"In the first decade most of a balance is your own deposits. By the third decade most of it is growth, and most of that growth is growth on earlier growth. The curve bends late; the people who quit at year eight never saw it.","v":{"t":"cols","unit":"","items":[["yr 10",35,"35% growth"],["yr 20",60,"60%"],["yr 30",76,"76%"],["yr 40",86,"86%"]]}},{"h":"It works against you on debt","p":"A credit card at 24 percent compounds daily. A $5,000 balance with minimum payments can take more than two decades to clear and cost more in interest than the original purchases. The rule of 72 says that debt doubles in three years if nothing is paid.","v":{"t":"big","n":"3 yrs","c":"for a balance at 24% to double if untouched (72 ÷ 24)"}},{"h":"Fees compound too","p":"A fund charging 1 percent a year instead of 0.1 doesn't cost you 1 percent: it costs that percent of a growing balance every year, plus the growth that money would have made. Over 30 years a 1 percent fee can take roughly a quarter of the final balance.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[0,100],[10,196],[20,386],[30,761]],"pts2":[[0,100],[10,179],[20,321],[30,574]],"xl":"years","l1":"7% net: $761","l2":"6% after 1% fee: $574"}},{"h":"Inflation is compounding in reverse","p":"Prices rising 3 percent a year means cash loses about half its buying power in 24 years. Money that needs to last decades has to earn more than inflation just to stand still, which is the case for owning assets rather than holding cash for the long run.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[0,100],[12,70],[24,49],[36,35],[48,24]],"xl":"years at 3% inflation","yl":"what $100 buys","l1":"$24"}},{"h":"The practical version","p":"Automate a contribution so it happens before you can spend it, keep fees low, don't interrupt it (early withdrawals reset the clock), and pay off anything compounding against you at a double-digit rate first. Then wait. Waiting is the active ingredient.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Pay off double-digit debt first","Automate a monthly contribution","Keep fees near zero","Don't touch it; time is the ingredient"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"By the rule of 72, money earning 6% a year doubles in about…","a":["6 years","12 years","24 years"],"c":1,"why":"72 ÷ 6 = 12. It's an approximation, but a good one for ordinary rates."},{"q":"Who ends up with more at 65, at the same 7% return?","a":["$200/month from 25 to 35, then nothing","$200/month from 35 to 65","They tie"],"c":0,"why":"Ten early years beat thirty later ones because the early deposits compound for four decades."},{"q":"A 1% annual fund fee over 30 years costs roughly…","a":["1% of the final balance","About a quarter of the final balance","Nothing, it's rounding"],"c":1,"why":"The fee comes out of a growing balance every year and takes the growth that money would have earned with it."}],"take":["Growth on growth: the curve bends late, so start early and don't interrupt it.","Rule of 72: years to double ≈ 72 ÷ rate.","Compounding works against you on card debt and inside fund fees."],"src":[["Compound interest — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compound_interest"],["Investor.gov: Compound interest calculator","https://www.investor.gov/financial-tools-calculators/calculators/compound-interest-calculator"],["Rule of 72 — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rule_of_72"]],"cat":"money"},{"id":"credit-scores","title":"How a credit score works","dek":"The five ingredients, the two that matter most and what moves it fastest","icon":"💳","slides":[{"h":"A score is a guess about the next two years","p":"A credit score is a number, usually 300 to 850, built from your credit reports to predict how likely you are to fall 90 days behind on a payment in the next 24 months. Lenders, landlords and insurers use it. It isn't a measure of wealth or of being a good person.","v":{"t":"scale","min":300,"max":850,"unit":"","zones":[[300,580,"poor"],[580,670,"fair"],[670,740,"good"],[740,800,"very good"],[800,850,"exceptional"]],"marks":[[715,"U.S. average ≈ 715"]]}},{"h":"Five ingredients","p":"The FICO recipe: payment history 35 percent, amounts owed 30, length of history 15, new credit 10, mix of credit 10. Two of the five, paying on time and not using much of your available credit, are nearly two-thirds of the score.","v":{"t":"donut","parts":[["payment history",35],["amounts owed (utilization)",30],["length of history",15],["new credit",10],["credit mix",10]]}},{"h":"Payment history: never 30 days late","p":"A payment reported 30 or more days late is the single most damaging ordinary event and stays on the report seven years, fading as it ages. A few days late costs a fee but usually isn't reported. Autopay the minimum on everything, then pay the rest by hand.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Autopay at least the minimum on every account","Pay the full balance manually before the due date","If you'll be late, call first; many lenders will work with you once"]}},{"h":"Utilization: the fast lever","p":"The share of your credit limits you're using, per card and overall, is measured on the day the statement closes. Under 30 percent is the usual advice; under 10 is better. Because it's a snapshot, paying a card down before the statement date can lift a score within a month.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":100,"items":[["Under 10%",10,"best"],["Under 30%",30,"good"],["50%",50,"hurts"],["Maxed out",100,"hurts a lot"]]}},{"h":"Length and age: don't close the old card","p":"The age of your oldest account and the average age of all of them count. Closing a long-held card shortens your history over time and cuts your available credit, raising utilization. Keep old no-fee cards open with a small recurring charge on autopay.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","Keep the oldest no-fee card alive"],["✓","A small subscription on it, autopaid"],["✗","Closing cards to 'clean up'"],["✗","Opening several cards in a few months"]]}},{"h":"Hard pulls, soft pulls","p":"Applying for credit creates a hard inquiry that trims a few points for a few months. Checking your own score, or a lender pre-approving you, is a soft pull and costs nothing. Rate shopping for a mortgage or car loan within about two weeks counts as one inquiry.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Hard inquiry","you applied for credit","small dip, up to 12 months","shopping window ≈ 14–45 days"],"b":["Soft inquiry","you checked your own","pre-approval offers","no effect on the score"]}},{"h":"You have more than one score","p":"Three bureaus (Equifax, Experian, TransUnion) hold slightly different reports, and FICO and VantageScore each have several versions. The free score in your banking app is a real score, just maybe not the one a mortgage lender pulls. The direction matters more than the digit.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🏢","Equifax"],["🏢","Experian"],["🏢","TransUnion"],["🧮","FICO (most lenders)"],["🧮","VantageScore (most apps)"]]}},{"h":"Check the report, not just the number","p":"You're entitled to free reports from all three bureaus at AnnualCreditReport.com (weekly, at the moment). Look for accounts you don't recognise, wrong late marks and old addresses; disputes are fixed often enough to be worth the half hour. A freeze blocks new accounts in your name.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Pull all three reports at annualcreditreport.com","Dispute anything wrong online with the bureau","Freeze your credit; thaw it when you apply"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The two biggest ingredients in a FICO score are…","a":["Income and job title","Payment history and how much of your credit you use","Number of cards and age"],"c":1,"why":"Payment history (35%) and amounts owed (30%) are nearly two-thirds of the score. Income isn't in it at all."},{"q":"The quickest way to raise a score in a month is usually to…","a":["Open a new card","Pay card balances down before the statement closes","Close unused cards"],"c":1,"why":"Utilization is a snapshot taken on the statement date. Lower balances that day mean a lower ratio reported."},{"q":"Checking your own credit score…","a":["Lowers it slightly","Has no effect","Raises it"],"c":1,"why":"That's a soft inquiry. Only applications for new credit create hard inquiries."}],"take":["Never 30 days late; autopay the minimum as insurance.","Keep utilization under 30% (better, under 10%); it's a snapshot you can time.","Keep old cards open, apply rarely, read the reports once a year."],"src":[["Consumer Financial Protection Bureau: Credit reports and scores","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/credit-reports-and-scores/"],["myFICO: What's in my FICO scores","https://www.myfico.com/credit-education/whats-in-your-credit-score"],["AnnualCreditReport.com","https://www.annualcreditreport.com/"]],"cat":"money"},{"id":"reading-a-paycheck","title":"Reading your paycheck","dek":"Gross to net, what FICA is, and the W-4 that sets your withholding","icon":"🧾","slides":[{"h":"Gross is what you earned; net is what arrived","p":"The stub starts with gross pay for the period and ends with net pay, the deposit. Everything in between is either taken by law (taxes), chosen by you (retirement, insurance) or both. A typical U.S. worker nets 70 to 80 percent of gross.","v":{"t":"stack","c":"an illustrative $4,000 gross paycheck","parts":[["net pay",74],["federal income tax",10],["Social Security + Medicare",8],["state tax",4],["benefits & 401(k)",4]]}},{"h":"FICA: Social Security and Medicare","p":"FICA takes 6.2 percent for Social Security (up to an annual wage cap that rises each year) and 1.45 percent for Medicare (no cap), 7.65 percent in all. Your employer pays the same again on your behalf. Self-employed people pay both halves.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"%","max":8,"items":[["Social Security (you)",6.2],["Medicare (you)",1.45],["Employer matches",7.65,"7.65% more, unseen"]]}},{"h":"Federal withholding is an estimate","p":"The federal income tax line is a guess at your year's bill, spread across paychecks using the W-4 you filled out. A big refund in April means you over-withheld and lent the government money all year; a bill means you under-withheld. Either way the true tax was set by the return, not the stub.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Big refund","too much withheld","interest-free loan to the IRS","fix: adjust the W-4"],"b":["Owe at filing","too little withheld","possible penalty if large","fix: adjust the W-4"]}},{"h":"The W-4 is a dial you can turn","p":"The form no longer uses 'allowances'. It asks about multiple jobs, dependents and other income or deductions, and has a line for extra dollars per paycheck. Change it any time with HR, especially after a marriage, a second job, a new child or a big raise.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Step 1: filing status","Step 2: more than one job, or a working spouse","Step 3: dependents","Step 4: other income, deductions, extra withholding","Use the IRS estimator and resubmit after life changes"]}},{"h":"Pre-tax beats post-tax","p":"Traditional 401(k) contributions, health premiums under a Section 125 plan, HSA and FSA money come out before income tax is calculated, so a $100 contribution costs you less than $100 of take-home. A Roth 401(k) is taxed now and free later: a bet on future rates.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Gross pay","minus pre-tax: 401(k), health premiums, HSA","equals taxable wages: income tax is figured on this","minus taxes, then post-tax items (Roth, dues)","equals net pay"]}},{"h":"The employer match is part of your pay","p":"If your employer matches 401(k) contributions (say, 50 cents per dollar up to 6 percent of pay), not contributing that much leaves salary on the table. Vesting schedules decide how much of the match you keep if you leave; your own contributions are always yours.","v":{"t":"big","n":"+50%","c":"an instant return on every dollar a typical match covers"}},{"h":"Year-to-date tells the story","p":"YTD columns show totals since January. They're how you spot an error (a deduction that stopped, a bonus taxed oddly), check against the W-2 in January, and see whether you'll hit the Social Security wage cap or the 401(k) limit before year end.","v":{"t":"grid","cols":["This check","Year to date"],"rows":[["Gross","$4,000","$36,000"],["Federal tax","$400","$3,600"],["401(k)","$240","$2,160"],["Net","$2,960","$26,640"]]}},{"h":"Three things to check every stub","p":"That the hours or salary are right, that your 401(k) and insurance deductions are what you elected, and that the net matches the deposit. Payroll errors are common and easiest to fix in the same quarter. Keep the last stub of the year with your tax papers.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","Hours × rate, or salary ÷ periods"],["✓","Deductions match what you chose"],["✓","Net equals the bank deposit"],["✓","Final stub of the year, kept"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"FICA on your paycheck pays for…","a":["Federal income tax","Social Security and Medicare","State unemployment"],"c":1,"why":"6.2% for Social Security (to the wage cap) plus 1.45% for Medicare, and your employer pays the same again."},{"q":"A large tax refund every April most likely means…","a":["You earned a bonus from the IRS","Too much was withheld from each paycheck","Your employer underpaid you"],"c":1,"why":"Withholding is an estimate. A refund returns your own money, interest-free; adjusting the W-4 keeps more in each check."},{"q":"Traditional 401(k) contributions are taken out…","a":["Before income tax is calculated","After all taxes","Only from bonuses"],"c":0,"why":"They reduce taxable wages now; the money is taxed when withdrawn. Roth contributions work the other way around."}],"take":["Gross − taxes − deductions = net; most people keep 70–80%.","FICA is 7.65% from you plus 7.65% from your employer.","The W-4 is a dial: revisit it after any life change, and never leave the match unclaimed."],"src":[["IRS: Tax withholding estimator","https://www.irs.gov/individuals/tax-withholding-estimator"],["IRS: Form W-4","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-w-4"],["Social Security Administration: contribution and benefit base","https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/cbb.html"]],"cat":"money"},{"id":"insurance-basics","title":"Insurance, explained","dek":"Premium, deductible, out-of-pocket max and the one principle behind all of it","icon":"☂️","slides":[{"h":"Insurance is a pool","p":"Many people pay a little in so that the few who suffer a loss can take a lot out. You're not buying your money back; you're buying the certainty that a fire, a crash or a diagnosis won't ruin you. Insure the losses you couldn't absorb; self-insure the ones you could.","v":{"t":"big","n":"many → few","c":"small certain payments cover rare large losses"}},{"h":"Four words on every policy","p":"The premium is what you pay to have coverage. The deductible is what you pay first when something happens. Coinsurance is the share you pay after that. The out-of-pocket maximum is the most you'll pay in a year, after which the insurer pays all of it.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Premium (monthly)","Deductible (you pay first)","Coinsurance (you pay a share)","Out-of-pocket max (then 100% covered)"]}},{"h":"A worked health example","p":"Plan: $3,000 deductible, 20 percent coinsurance, $7,000 out-of-pocket max. A $20,000 hospital bill: you pay the first $3,000, then 20 percent of the next $17,000 ($3,400), total $6,400, under the max. A $50,000 bill stops at $7,000 for you.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":50000,"items":[["$20,000 bill → you pay",6400,"$6,400"],["$50,000 bill → you pay",7000,"$7,000 (the max)"],["Insurer pays on $50,000",43000,"$43,000"]]}},{"h":"Higher deductible, lower premium","p":"Every policy trades the two. If you have an emergency fund, a higher deductible usually wins over time because you pay less every month and rarely claim. If a surprise $2,000 would go on a credit card, pay the premium for the lower deductible.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["High deductible","lower premium","you absorb small losses","good with savings","HSA-eligible (health)"],"b":["Low deductible","higher premium","insurer absorbs more","good without a cushion","predictable costs"]}},{"h":"Liability is the part that protects your future","p":"On car and home policies, liability pays when you're responsible for someone else's injury or property. State minimums are often tiny next to a real injury claim. A $1 million umbrella policy over your car and home coverage usually costs a few hundred dollars a year.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🚗","Auto liability","bodily injury / property"],["🏠","Home liability","guest slips on the stairs"],["☂️","Umbrella","$1M+ on top of both"]]}},{"h":"Replacement cost vs actual cash value","p":"Replacement cost pays what it takes to buy the thing new. Actual cash value pays what the old thing was worth after depreciation, which for a ten-year-old roof or sofa is not much. The premium difference is usually worth it on a home policy.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Replacement cost","new roof: $15,000","pays $15,000 (less deductible)","higher premium"],"b":["Actual cash value","same roof, 15 years old","pays maybe $5,000","lower premium"]}},{"h":"Term life: cheap, simple, enough","p":"If someone depends on your income, term life insurance replaces it for a set period (20 or 30 years) for a small premium. Whole life bundles insurance with an expensive savings account; most people are better off with term plus their own investing.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":500,"items":[["Term, $500k, 20 yr, healthy 35-yr-old",30,"≈ $25–40 / month"],["Whole life, $500k, same person",450,"≈ $400–500 / month"]]}},{"h":"The ones to skip, and the one to read","p":"Extended warranties, phone insurance and travel insurance on cheap trips mostly cover losses you can absorb at prices that favor the seller. Flood is not in a standard home policy; earthquake isn't either. Read the exclusions page once; it's where the surprises live.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✗","Extended warranties on small electronics"],["✗","Flight insurance, rental-car damage if your card covers it"],["✓","Flood, if there's any water nearby (separate policy)"],["✓","The exclusions page of every policy you hold"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The deductible is…","a":["What you pay every month","What you pay first when you make a claim","The most you'll pay in a year"],"c":1,"why":"Premium is monthly, deductible is first-dollar on a claim, the out-of-pocket maximum is the yearly ceiling."},{"q":"With a $3,000 deductible, 20% coinsurance and a $7,000 out-of-pocket max, a $50,000 hospital bill costs you…","a":["$3,000","$7,000","$12,400"],"c":1,"why":"$3,000 plus 20% of $47,000 would be $12,400, but the out-of-pocket maximum caps you at $7,000."},{"q":"A standard homeowner's policy covers flood damage.","a":["True","False"],"c":1,"why":"Flood needs a separate policy (in the U.S., often through the National Flood Insurance Program). Earthquake is separate too."}],"take":["Insure what would ruin you; self-insure what wouldn't.","Premium, deductible, coinsurance, out-of-pocket max: four words, one bill.","Buy liability generously, replacement cost on the house, term over whole life, and read the exclusions."],"src":[["HealthCare.gov glossary: deductible, coinsurance, out-of-pocket maximum","https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/"],["Insurance Information Institute","https://www.iii.org/"],["FEMA: National Flood Insurance Program","https://www.floodsmart.gov/"]],"cat":"money"},{"id":"how-mortgages-work","title":"How a mortgage works","dek":"Amortization, points, PMI and why the first payments are almost all interest","icon":"🏠","slides":[{"h":"A fixed payment, a moving mix","p":"A 30-year fixed mortgage has the same payment every month, but early on most of it is interest on the big balance and only a sliver is principal. As the balance falls, the mix flips. On a $400,000 loan at 6.5 percent, the first payment is about $2,170 in interest and $360 in principal.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[0,360],[5,500],[10,690],[15,950],[20,1310],[25,1810],[30,2520]],"pts2":[[0,2170],[5,2030],[10,1840],[15,1580],[20,1220],[25,720],[30,10]],"xl":"year","l1":"principal","l2":"interest"}},{"h":"Thirty years of interest adds up","p":"That $400,000 loan at 6.5 percent costs about $510,000 in interest over 30 years, more than the house. A 15-year loan at a slightly lower rate pays about $190,000 in interest, with a monthly payment about 40 percent higher. Neither is right for everyone; both should be seen before signing.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":910000,"items":[["30-year: principal",400000,"$400k"],["30-year: interest",510000,"≈ $510k"],["15-year: interest",190000,"≈ $190k"]]}},{"h":"PITI: the real monthly number","p":"The payment the bank quotes is principal and interest. Add property taxes and homeowner's insurance, usually collected into an escrow account and paid by the servicer, plus any HOA dues and mortgage insurance. PITI is the figure to budget against.","v":{"t":"stack","c":"an illustrative $3,300 monthly payment","parts":[["principal & interest",77],["property tax",14],["insurance",5],["PMI / HOA",4]]}},{"h":"The down payment and PMI","p":"Put down less than 20 percent on a conventional loan and you'll pay private mortgage insurance, often 0.5 to 1.5 percent of the loan a year, protecting the lender, not you. It can be cancelled once you reach 20 percent equity; ask, because it doesn't always fall off by itself.","v":{"t":"scale","min":0,"max":30,"unit":"%","zones":[[0,3,"FHA / some programs"],[3,20,"conventional, with PMI"],[20,30,"no PMI"]],"marks":[[20,"PMI ends"]]}},{"h":"Rate, APR and points","p":"The interest rate is the cost of the money; the APR folds in fees and points to compare offers. A point is one percent of the loan paid up front to lower the rate, typically by about a quarter percent. It pays off only if you keep the loan past the break-even, often five to seven years.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Pay 1 point","$4,000 up front on $400k","rate 6.25% instead of 6.5%","saves ≈ $65/month","break-even ≈ 5 years"],"b":["No points","nothing up front","higher rate","better if you'll move or refinance soon"]}},{"h":"Fixed or adjustable","p":"A fixed rate never changes. An ARM (say, 5/1 or 7/6) holds a lower rate for the first years, then adjusts with the market, within caps. It suits people who will sell or refinance before the reset and can afford the worst case; the 2008 crisis was in part people who couldn't.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[0,6.5],[10,6.5],[20,6.5],[30,6.5]],"pts2":[[0,5.75],[7,5.75],[8,7.5],[10,8.5],[20,7],[30,7.8]],"xl":"year","yl":"rate","l1":"fixed","l2":"7/6 ARM (one path)"}},{"h":"Extra principal is a guaranteed return","p":"Any extra payment marked 'principal only' shortens the loan and avoids interest at the loan's rate, risk-free. One extra payment a year on a 30-year loan knocks about four years off. Weigh it against higher-rate debt and the employer match, which come first.","v":{"t":"big","n":"−4 yrs","c":"from one extra monthly payment a year on a 30-year loan"}},{"h":"Refinancing and the closing costs","p":"Refinancing replaces the loan with a new one, usually to cut the rate, change the term or pull out cash. It costs 2 to 5 percent of the loan in closing costs, so it pays only if you'll stay past the break-even. Resetting a 25-year balance to 30 years means more total interest unless you keep paying the old amount.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Get the new rate and the total closing costs","Monthly saving = old payment − new payment","Break-even months = costs ÷ saving","Refinance if you'll stay well past that"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"In the first year of a 30-year fixed mortgage, most of each payment goes to…","a":["Principal","Interest","Property tax"],"c":1,"why":"Interest is charged on the full balance, so early payments are mostly interest. The mix flips in the later years."},{"q":"PMI is required on a conventional loan when…","a":["The home is more than 30 years old","The down payment is under 20%","The buyer is self-employed"],"c":1,"why":"Under 20% down, the lender wants insurance against default. It can be cancelled once you reach 20% equity."},{"q":"Paying discount points makes sense when…","a":["You'll keep the loan well past the break-even","You plan to sell within two years","Rates are falling"],"c":0,"why":"A point buys a lower rate for cash up front. It pays off only if you stay long enough for the monthly savings to exceed the cost."}],"take":["Early payments are mostly interest; a 30-year loan can cost more in interest than the house.","Budget PITI, not the bank's quote; PMI ends at 20% equity if you ask.","Points, ARMs and refinancing are bets on how long you'll stay."],"src":[["CFPB: Buying a house","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/"],["Amortization (finance) — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amortization_(finance)"],["Freddie Mac: Primary Mortgage Market Survey","https://www.freddiemac.com/pmms"]],"cat":"money"},{"id":"budget-and-cushion","title":"A budget that survives real life","dek":"50/30/20, the emergency fund and paying yourself first","icon":"🗂","slides":[{"h":"Three buckets, not thirty categories","p":"The 50/30/20 rule splits take-home pay into needs (housing, food, utilities, transport, minimum debt payments), wants (everything that makes life nice), and saving or extra debt payoff. It's a shape, not a law; in expensive cities needs run higher and the point is knowing it.","v":{"t":"donut","c":"of take-home","parts":[["needs",50],["wants",30],["saving & debt",20]]}},{"h":"Pay yourself first","p":"Budgets fail when saving is what's left over. Move the saving on payday, automatically, into an account you don't see, then live on the rest. People who do this save more than people who track every latte, because the decision is made once.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Payday","Automatic transfer to savings","Bills on autopay","Spend what's left, guilt-free"]}},{"h":"The emergency fund comes before investing","p":"Three to six months of essential expenses in a high-yield savings account turns a job loss or a transmission into a problem instead of a crisis. Start with one month, or $1,000, whichever is reachable, and build from there. It is boring and it is the foundation.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":6,"items":[["Starter: $1,000 or 1 month",1],["Dual income, stable jobs",3,"3 months"],["Single income or variable pay",6,"6 months"]]}},{"h":"Where it lives matters","p":"An emergency fund has one job: be there, fast, in full. That means a federally insured savings or money-market account, ideally at a different bank from your checking so it isn't a tap away. Not in stocks, which may be down the week you need it, and not under the mattress.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Right home","high-yield savings","money market account","FDIC/NCUA insured","reachable in 1–3 days"],"b":["Wrong home","stocks or crypto","a CD with penalties","cash at home","the checking account"]}},{"h":"Order of operations for every spare dollar","p":"Starter fund, then any employer match (free money), then high-interest debt (cards), then the full emergency fund, then tax-advantaged retirement accounts, then everything else. The order is roughly by guaranteed return.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["$1,000 starter fund","401(k) up to the match","Pay off double-digit-rate debt","3–6 months of expenses","Max tax-advantaged accounts","Taxable investing, extra mortgage principal"]}},{"h":"The leaks: subscriptions and small defaults","p":"The budget killers are rarely one big purchase. They're the twelve subscriptions, the delivery fees, the premium tier nobody chose, the insurance that auto-renewed higher. Once a quarter, read a full statement line by line and cancel or renegotiate.","v":{"t":"cols","unit":"","items":[["Streaming ×4",60,"$60"],["Apps & cloud",25,"$25"],["Delivery fees",80,"$80"],["Gym unused",45,"$45"],["Phone add-ons",20,"$20"]]}},{"h":"Irregular bills break monthly budgets","p":"Car insurance twice a year, holidays, the property tax bill, the dentist: predictable but lumpy. Add them up for the year, divide by twelve, and move that amount into a 'sinking fund' every month. December stops being a surprise.","v":{"t":"big","n":"÷ 12","c":"annual lumps, saved monthly: no December panic"}},{"h":"Review, don't track","p":"Logging every coffee lasts three weeks. A monthly thirty-minute review lasts years: did the saving transfer go, did needs stay near half, what one thing drifted. Adjust one number and move on. The budget's job is to make the next month a little more deliberate.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","Saving transfer happened"],["✓","Needs ≈ 50%, or you know why not"],["✓","One subscription or fee questioned"],["✓","Sinking funds topped up"],["✓","One number adjusted for next month"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"In the 50/30/20 rule, the 20 is for…","a":["Rent and groceries","Dining out and travel","Saving and paying down debt"],"c":2,"why":"Fifty for needs, thirty for wants, twenty for saving and extra debt payments. It's a target shape, not a rule."},{"q":"The emergency fund should be kept in…","a":["An index fund","An insured savings or money-market account","Cash at home"],"c":1,"why":"It has to be whole and reachable within days. Markets can be down exactly when you need the money."},{"q":"Of these, which usually comes first for a spare dollar?","a":["Extra mortgage principal","Enough 401(k) to get the full employer match","A taxable brokerage account"],"c":1,"why":"A match is an instant 50 or 100 percent return. Only a small starter fund comes before it."}],"take":["Needs, wants, saving: roughly 50/30/20, moved automatically on payday.","Three to six months of expenses in an insured account, before investing.","Starter fund → match → card debt → full fund → retirement accounts."],"src":[["CFPB: Build an emergency fund","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/an-essential-guide-to-building-an-emergency-fund/"],["Elizabeth Warren & Amelia Tyagi, All Your Worth (the 50/30/20 rule) — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_Warren#Books"],["FDIC: Deposit insurance","https://www.fdic.gov/resources/deposit-insurance/"]],"cat":"money"},{"id":"tax-brackets","title":"How tax brackets actually work","dek":"Marginal versus effective, and why a raise never costs you money","icon":"🧮","slides":[{"h":"Brackets are stairs, not buckets","p":"Income tax is progressive: each slice of income is taxed at its own rate. Moving into a higher bracket taxes only the dollars above the line, never the dollars below it. The myth that a raise can leave you with less after tax is just that.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["First slice taxed at the lowest rate","Next slice at the next rate","Only income above each line is taxed at that line's rate","Your top rate applies to your last dollar, not your first"]}},{"h":"A worked example with round numbers","p":"Imagine three brackets: 10 percent to $10,000, 20 percent to $50,000, 30 percent above. On $60,000: $1,000 + $8,000 + $3,000 = $12,000. The top rate is 30, but the tax is 20 percent of income. Real U.S. brackets have seven steps and are adjusted for inflation yearly.","v":{"t":"stack","c":"$60,000 under the example brackets","parts":[["$10k at 10% = $1,000",17],["$40k at 20% = $8,000",66],["$10k at 30% = $3,000",17]]}},{"h":"Marginal rate vs effective rate","p":"Your marginal rate is what the next dollar is taxed at; it's what matters for deciding on overtime or a deduction. Your effective rate is total tax divided by total income; it's what you actually pay and it's always lower. People quote the first and feel the second.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Marginal","rate on the next dollar","30% in the example","use it to weigh a deduction or a bonus"],"b":["Effective","total tax ÷ income","20% in the example","use it to understand your real burden"]}},{"h":"Deductions come off the top","p":"Taxable income is gross income minus deductions. Nearly everyone takes the standard deduction, a flat amount set each year; itemizing (mortgage interest, state taxes up to a cap, charity, big medical bills) only helps if those exceed it. A deduction is worth your marginal rate times its size.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Gross income","− adjustments (401k, HSA)","− standard or itemized deduction","= taxable income","× bracket rates"]}},{"h":"A credit beats a deduction","p":"A deduction lowers the income that gets taxed; a credit lowers the tax itself, dollar for dollar. A $1,000 deduction saves a 22 percent taxpayer $220; a $1,000 credit saves $1,000. Refundable credits (like the earned income credit) can pay out even when no tax is owed.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":1000,"items":[["$1,000 deduction at 22%",220,"saves $220"],["$1,000 credit",1000,"saves $1,000"]]}},{"h":"Capital gains have their own ladder","p":"Profit on investments held more than a year is taxed at long-term rates (0, 15 or 20 percent in the U.S., by income), lower than wage rates. Held a year or less, gains are taxed as ordinary income. That one-year line is worth a calendar reminder.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Short-term (≤ 1 year)","taxed as ordinary income","up to the top bracket"],"b":["Long-term (> 1 year)","0% / 15% / 20%","plus 3.8% NIIT for high incomes"]}},{"h":"Where the money actually goes","p":"Income tax is one piece. Add payroll taxes (Social Security and Medicare), state and local income tax in most states, sales tax, and property tax. For many households payroll tax is bigger than income tax. The stub shows two of these; the others arrive by other routes.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🏛","Federal income"],["🩺","Payroll (FICA)"],["🗺","State & local"],["🛒","Sales"],["🏠","Property"]]}},{"h":"Legal ways to lower the bill","p":"Pre-tax retirement and HSA contributions shrink taxable income. Holding investments past a year moves them to the lower ladder. Harvesting losses offsets gains. Bunching charitable gifts into one year can make itemizing worth it. All boring, all legal, all better than a scheme.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","401(k)/IRA and HSA contributions"],["✓","Hold winners past one year"],["✓","Offset gains with losses"],["✓","Bunch deductions into one year"],["✗","Anything that promises zero tax"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A raise pushes some of your income into a higher bracket. What happens to the income below the line?","a":["It's now taxed at the higher rate too","It's taxed exactly as before","It becomes tax-free"],"c":1,"why":"Only the dollars above the threshold are taxed at the new rate. Your take-home always rises with a raise."},{"q":"Which saves a 22%-bracket taxpayer more money?","a":["A $1,000 deduction","A $1,000 credit","They're equal"],"c":1,"why":"A credit cuts the tax bill dollar for dollar; a deduction cuts income, saving only your marginal rate times its size."},{"q":"Your effective tax rate is…","a":["The rate on your next dollar","Total tax divided by total income","Your top bracket"],"c":1,"why":"That's the share you actually paid, and it's always lower than the marginal rate people quote."}],"take":["Brackets are stairs: only the dollars above each line pay that rate.","Marginal is the next dollar; effective is what you really pay.","Credits beat deductions; a year's holding period moves gains to the lower ladder."],"src":[["IRS: Federal income tax rates and brackets","https://www.irs.gov/filing/federal-income-tax-rates-and-brackets"],["Tax Policy Center: How do federal income tax rates work?","https://www.taxpolicycenter.org/briefing-book/how-do-federal-income-tax-rates-work"],["Progressive tax — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Progressive_tax"]],"cat":"money"},{"id":"index-funds","title":"Index funds vs picking stocks","dek":"What an index is, what the evidence says and the three-fund idea","icon":"🧺","slides":[{"h":"An index is a list; a fund buys the list","p":"The S&P 500 is a list of about 500 large U.S. companies weighted by size. An index fund simply owns all of them in those proportions, so you get the market's return minus a tiny fee. A total-market fund extends the list to thousands of companies.","v":{"t":"big","n":"≈ 500","c":"companies in one share of an S&P 500 fund"}},{"h":"Most professionals don't beat it","p":"Over 15-year periods, roughly nine in ten actively managed U.S. large-cap funds have trailed the S&P 500 after fees, according to S&P's own scorecards. Some win in any given year; picking in advance which ones will keep winning is the part nobody has managed to do reliably.","v":{"t":"donut","c":"15 years","parts":[["active funds that trailed the index",90],["beat it",10]]}},{"h":"Fees are the one thing you control","p":"Index funds commonly charge 0.03 to 0.2 percent a year; active funds often charge 0.5 to 1 percent or more. The gap compounds. Over 30 years a 1 percent difference can take roughly a quarter of the final balance, which is most of the active-versus-passive story.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"%","max":1,"items":[["Total-market index fund",0.03,"0.03%"],["S&P 500 index fund",0.05,"0.05%"],["Typical active fund",0.7,"0.7%"],["Advisor + active fund",1,"1%+"]]}},{"h":"Diversification is the free lunch","p":"A single company can go to zero; a market of thousands historically hasn't. Owning everything means you hold the few huge winners (which drive most of the return) without having to find them. The price is that you also hold the losers, which, it turns out, is fine.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["One stock","can lose everything","depends on one management team","needs research and luck"],"b":["Whole market","has never gone to zero","holds every future winner","needs patience"]}},{"h":"The three-fund portfolio","p":"Many investors hold just three index funds: U.S. total stock market, international stocks and bonds. The share in bonds rises as you approach needing the money, because bonds fall less in a crash. A target-date fund does the same rebalancing for you in one ticker.","v":{"t":"stack","c":"one common mix for a 35-year-old","parts":[["U.S. stocks",55],["international stocks",30],["bonds",15]]}},{"h":"Time in the market, not timing it","p":"Missing just the ten best days over a couple of decades roughly halves the return, and the best days cluster right after the worst ones. Nobody rings a bell at the bottom. A fixed amount every month, through crashes, buys more shares when they're cheap.","v":{"t":"cols","unit":"","items":[["stayed in",100,"100%"],["missed 10 best days",50,"≈ 50%"],["missed 20",30,"≈ 30%"],["missed 30",15,"≈ 15%"]]}},{"h":"Where the account lives changes the tax","p":"Same fund, different wrapper. A 401(k) or traditional IRA defers tax; a Roth is taxed now and never again; a taxable brokerage account pays on dividends and realised gains. Fill the tax-advantaged accounts first; put bonds in the deferred ones and stocks in the Roth if you want to be fancy.","v":{"t":"grid","cols":["Tax in","Tax out"],"rows":[["401(k) / trad. IRA","none","ordinary income"],["Roth","paid","none"],["Taxable","paid","capital gains"],["HSA","none","none (medical)"]]}},{"h":"What this isn't","p":"Index investing doesn't avoid crashes; it rides them, and a 30 to 50 percent drop will happen more than once in a working life. It isn't a path to fast wealth. It's the method with the strongest evidence for most people, most of the time, and this card isn't advice for your situation.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","Low cost, diversified, automatic, held for decades"],["✓","A written plan you'll follow in a crash"],["✗","A promise of steady returns"],["✗","A reason to skip the emergency fund"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"An S&P 500 index fund…","a":["Picks the 500 best stocks each year","Owns about 500 large U.S. companies in proportion to their size","Guarantees 10% a year"],"c":1,"why":"It copies the list. There's no picking and no guarantee; the return is the market's, minus a tiny fee."},{"q":"Over 15 years, about what share of active U.S. large-cap funds has trailed the index after fees?","a":["About half","About nine in ten","Almost none"],"c":1,"why":"S&P's SPIVA scorecards have shown roughly 85–95 percent trailing over long periods, mostly because of fees."},{"q":"The usual role of bonds in a portfolio is to…","a":["Beat stocks over time","Fall less in a crash and steady the ride","Avoid all taxes"],"c":1,"why":"Bonds return less than stocks over long periods but cushion the drops, which matters more the closer you are to needing the money."}],"take":["An index fund buys the whole list; most professionals don't beat it after fees.","Fees compound; keep them near zero.","Three funds, automatic contributions, decades, no timing."],"src":[["S&P Dow Jones Indices: SPIVA scorecards","https://www.spglobal.com/spdji/en/research-insights/spiva/"],["Investor.gov: Mutual funds and ETFs","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/mutual-funds-and-exchange-traded-1"],["Index fund — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Index_fund"]],"cat":"money"},{"id":"negotiating","title":"How to negotiate anything","dek":"BATNA, anchors, interests over positions and the power of silence","icon":"🤝","slides":[{"h":"Know your walk-away before you sit down","p":"Your BATNA is your Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement: what you'll do if this falls through. The better it is, the calmer you are, and the other side can tell. Improve it before you negotiate (get a second offer, a second quote) and never reveal it if it's weak.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Line up an alternative","Know your walk-away number","Negotiate from calm","Walk if it's worse than the alternative"]}},{"h":"Positions are what people say; interests are why","p":"'I need $90,000' is a position. 'I need to cover the move and not take a pay cut' is an interest, and interests can be met in more than one way: a signing bonus, a review in six months, remote days. Ask 'what would that do for you?' until you hear the why.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Position","'$90,000, non-negotiable'","'Friday is the deadline'","'I won't pay for shipping'"],"b":["Interest behind it","covering a relocation","a board meeting Monday","feeling the total is fair"]}},{"h":"Anchors pull the whole conversation","p":"The first number sets the range. Research shows final agreements land closer to whoever anchored. If you know the market, anchor first, high but defensible. If you don't, ask them to go first, and treat their number as information, not a ceiling.","v":{"t":"scale","min":70,"max":110,"unit":"k","zones":[[70,85,"their anchor"],[85,95,"where deals land"],[95,110,"your anchor"]],"marks":[[90,"midpoint drifts toward the first number"]]}},{"h":"Find the zone, or there's no deal","p":"The ZOPA is the overlap between the most they'll pay and the least you'll take. If it exists, the negotiation is about where in it you land. If it doesn't, no technique will make it appear, and the best outcome is to find that out quickly and politely.","v":{"t":"scale","min":0,"max":100,"unit":"","zones":[[0,40,"below your minimum"],[40,65,"ZOPA: a deal is possible"],[65,100,"above their maximum"]],"marks":[[40,"your floor"],[65,"their ceiling"]]}},{"h":"Trade, don't split","p":"Splitting the difference leaves value on the table. Instead, give on what's cheap for you and valuable to them, and ask for what's cheap for them and valuable to you: delivery date for price, title for salary, a longer contract for a lower rate. Every concession should come with an 'if'.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","'If you can do X, I can do Y.'"],["✓","Several issues on the table at once"],["✓","Ask what matters most to them"],["✗","Conceding one thing at a time for nothing"]]}},{"h":"Silence is a tool","p":"After you state a number, stop talking. People fill silence by negotiating against themselves. When you hear an offer, pause, repeat the key figure back as a question ('Forty-two?'), and wait. Most of the time the next sentence improves it.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["State your number clearly","Stop. Count to five if you must.","Mirror their last words as a question","Let them fill the silence"]}},{"h":"Make it easy to say yes","p":"Give reasons ('because' raises compliance even for weak reasons), offer a choice between two acceptable options rather than yes or no, and let the other side save face. People agree to things they can explain to their boss. Help them write that explanation.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🧾","Give a because"],["🔀","Offer two good options"],["🪞","Leave them a way to say yes"],["📝","Put it in writing the same day"]]}},{"h":"What to do at the end","p":"Summarise what was agreed, in writing, today. Confirm who does what by when. Thank them; you'll likely meet again. And if there's no deal, leave the door open: 'If anything changes on your side, call me.' A good no is worth more than a bad yes.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Summarise aloud","Email it today","Owners and dates","Keep the relationship"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your BATNA is…","a":["Your opening offer","What you'll do if no agreement is reached","The midpoint of the two positions"],"c":1,"why":"Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement. A strong one is the real source of calm and leverage."},{"q":"Research on anchoring suggests final deals tend to land…","a":["Closer to whoever named the first number","Exactly in the middle","Closer to the second number"],"c":0,"why":"The first number frames the range. Anchor first when you know the market; otherwise treat their anchor as information."},{"q":"The ZOPA is…","a":["A negotiation tactic involving silence","The overlap between what you'll accept and what they'll pay","The final written agreement"],"c":1,"why":"Zone Of Possible Agreement. If it doesn't exist, no technique creates it; finding out fast is the win."}],"take":["Improve your alternative before you negotiate; it's your real leverage.","Ask for the interest behind the position; trade across issues instead of splitting.","Anchor first when informed, then stop talking."],"src":[["Getting to Yes (Fisher & Ury) — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Getting_to_Yes"],["Harvard Program on Negotiation: BATNA","https://www.pon.harvard.edu/tag/batna/"],["Anchoring effect — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anchoring_effect"]],"cat":"life"},{"id":"how-to-remember","title":"How to remember anything","dek":"The forgetting curve, spaced repetition, testing yourself and sleep","icon":"🧠","slides":[{"h":"You forget most of it within a day","p":"In the 1880s Hermann Ebbinghaus memorised nonsense syllables and tested himself over time. He found a steep curve: most of what's learned once is gone within a day or two unless it is revisited. The shape has been replicated many times since.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[0,100],[1,58],[9,44],[24,34],[48,28],[144,25],[744,21]],"xl":"hours since learning","yl":"retained","l1":"≈ 21% after a month"}},{"h":"Each review flattens the curve","p":"Reviewing just as you're about to forget resets the curve higher and makes the next decline slower. Four or five well-timed reviews can carry something for years. That's spaced repetition, and it beats cramming by a wide margin for the same total time.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[0,100],[1,60],[1.01,100],[3,70],[3.01,100],[7,78],[7.01,100],[16,85],[30,82]],"pts2":[[0,100],[1,58],[3,40],[7,30],[16,24],[30,21]],"xl":"days","l1":"spaced reviews","l2":"one exposure"}},{"h":"The schedule that works","p":"Review after about a day, then three days, then a week, then two, then a month, stretching each gap when you recall it easily and shrinking it when you don't. Apps like Anki do the scheduling; an index-card box with five slots does it on paper.","v":{"t":"cols","unit":" d","items":[["1st",1],["2nd",3],["3rd",7],["4th",14],["5th",30],["6th",90]]}},{"h":"Testing beats rereading","p":"Pulling something out of memory strengthens it far more than putting it in again. Students who read a passage once and then tested themselves remembered more a week later than students who reread it four times, even though the rereaders felt more confident. Close the book and recall.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"%","max":100,"items":[["Reread 4 times: felt confident",40,"≈ 40% a week later"],["Read once, self-tested 3 times",60,"≈ 60%"]]}},{"h":"Mix it up","p":"Practising one skill in a block feels productive; mixing related skills (interleaving) feels harder and works better, because you have to choose the method each time, which is what the exam or real life demands. Shuffle the problem types, the chord changes, the vocabulary themes.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Blocked","AAAA BBBB CCCC","feels smooth","fades fast"],"b":["Interleaved","ABCA CBAB CABC","feels harder","lasts longer"]}},{"h":"Make it mean something","p":"Isolated facts slip; connected ones stick. Explain the idea to someone else in your own words, ask 'why is that true?', attach it to something you already know, or turn it into an image. The weirder and more vivid the image, the better memory likes it.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🗣","Explain it aloud"],["❓","Ask why it's true"],["🔗","Link to what you know"],["🖼","Make a vivid picture"]]}},{"h":"The memory palace","p":"Memory athletes place items along a route they know, the rooms of a childhood home, and walk it to recall them in order. The technique is ancient and works because spatial memory is excellent. Twenty grocery items in a palace beat twenty on a list, for most people, on the first try.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Pick a route you know cold","Put one item at each stop as a vivid scene","Walk the route to recall","Reuse the route once the old items fade"]}},{"h":"Sleep is when it's filed","p":"During sleep the brain replays the day and moves memories from short-term holding into longer storage. An all-nighter before an exam undoes the studying; a nap after learning helps. Learn, sleep, test yourself the next day: that is the whole method in one line.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Learn","Sleep","Self-test","Space the reviews","Sleep again"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The forgetting curve shows that without review, most new material is lost…","a":["Within a day or two","After about a year","Never; it's just hard to find"],"c":0,"why":"Ebbinghaus found the steepest loss in the first hours and days. Each review slows the next decline."},{"q":"Which studying method produced better recall a week later?","a":["Rereading the passage four times","Reading once and self-testing three times","Highlighting"],"c":1,"why":"Retrieval strengthens memory more than re-exposure, even though rereading feels more confident."},{"q":"Interleaving means…","a":["Studying one topic until it's perfect","Mixing related problem types in one session","Taking breaks every 25 minutes"],"c":1,"why":"Choosing the method each time is harder and transfers better to real use."}],"take":["Review on a stretching schedule: 1, 3, 7, 14, 30 days.","Close the book and recall; testing beats rereading.","Mix topics, make it vivid, and sleep on it."],"src":[["Forgetting curve — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forgetting_curve"],["Testing effect — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Testing_effect"],["Spaced repetition — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spaced_repetition"]],"cat":"life"},{"id":"cognitive-biases","title":"Eight biases that run your decisions","dek":"The shortcuts the brain takes, and the questions that catch them","icon":"🪞","slides":[{"h":"Confirmation bias","p":"We seek and remember what agrees with us and explain away what doesn't. It's why two people read the same report and both feel vindicated. The counter: ask what evidence would change your mind, then go looking for exactly that.","v":{"t":"quote","q":"What would I expect to see if I were wrong?","by":"the one question that catches most of them"}},{"h":"Anchoring","p":"The first number sets the scale, even when it's random. In one study, spinning a wheel before guessing how many African countries are in the UN shifted the guesses toward the wheel's number. Prices, salaries and estimates all drift toward whatever was said first.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":45,"items":[["Wheel landed on 10 → median guess",25],["Wheel landed on 65 → median guess",45]]}},{"h":"Availability","p":"Vivid, recent and memorable things feel common. Plane crashes feel likelier than car crashes; shark attacks than falling coconuts. Whatever the news showed you this week feels like a trend. The counter is a base rate: how often does this actually happen per thousand, per year?","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Feels dangerous","flying","sharks","strangers"],"b":["Is more dangerous","driving","cows and dogs","people you know (for most crimes)"]}},{"h":"Loss aversion","p":"Losing $100 hurts about twice as much as gaining $100 feels good. So we hold losing stocks to avoid 'making it real', keep subscriptions we don't use, and accept bad deals framed as avoiding a loss. Reframe: if I didn't already own this, would I buy it today?","v":{"t":"big","n":"≈ 2×","c":"a loss weighs about twice as much as an equal gain"}},{"h":"Sunk cost","p":"Money and time already spent are gone whatever you do next, yet they drag us into finishing bad films, failing projects and dead relationships 'because we've come this far'. Only the future costs and benefits should count. The question: knowing what I know now, would I start this today?","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Spent $500","Gone either way","Decide on what's ahead","Not on what's behind"]}},{"h":"Survivorship bias","p":"We study the winners because they're the ones still around. The famous example: WWII analysts wanted to armor the parts of returning bombers that were full of holes, until Abraham Wald pointed out those planes came back; armor the places with no holes, where hit planes never returned.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["✈️","Planes that returned","holes in wings, tail"],["🕳","Planes that didn't","hit in engines, cockpit"],["🛡","Armor goes","where the survivors have no holes"]]}},{"h":"Dunning–Kruger and the expert's blind spot","p":"People with little skill in an area tend to overrate themselves because the skill needed to judge is the skill they lack; experts, knowing how much there is, often underrate themselves slightly. Confidence is a poor guide to competence, in yourself and in anyone selling you something.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[0,20],[10,80],[30,45],[60,55],[100,75]],"xl":"actual skill →","yl":"confidence","l1":"expert"}},{"h":"Hindsight, and how to beat all of it","p":"After the fact, everything looks like it was obvious, which stops us learning. Write down your prediction and your confidence before the result; review it after. A decision journal and a pre-mortem ('it's a year from now and this failed; why?') catch more biases than knowing their names.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Write the prediction and a confidence number first","Run a pre-mortem: assume it failed, list why","Decide, then revisit the journal later","Score the decision, not just the outcome"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Wanting to finish a boring film 'because we paid for it' is…","a":["Loss aversion","Sunk cost","Anchoring"],"c":1,"why":"The ticket money is gone either way. Only the next two hours should count in the decision."},{"q":"The bomber-armor story illustrates…","a":["Confirmation bias","Survivorship bias","Availability"],"c":1,"why":"The data came only from planes that survived. The missing planes, hit where the survivors had no holes, were the lesson."},{"q":"The single question most useful against confirmation bias is…","a":["Who agrees with me?","What would I expect to see if I were wrong?","How confident do I feel?"],"c":1,"why":"It forces you to name disconfirming evidence and then look for it."}],"take":["Ask what you'd expect to see if you were wrong, and look for it.","Ignore sunk costs; count only what's ahead.","Write predictions down before the result; confidence isn't competence."],"src":[["Daniel Kahneman, Thinking, Fast and Slow — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thinking,_Fast_and_Slow"],["Survivorship bias — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Survivorship_bias"],["List of cognitive biases — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_cognitive_biases"]],"cat":"life"},{"id":"hard-conversations","title":"Having the hard conversation","dek":"A structure for saying the difficult thing without starting a war","icon":"💬","slides":[{"h":"Decide what the conversation is for","p":"Before you open your mouth, finish this sentence: 'By the end, I want us to…' If the honest answer is 'win' or 'make them feel bad', wait. Good goals are to be understood, to understand, to agree on a change, or to repair. Say the goal out loud at the start; it lowers the temperature.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","'I want us to find a way that works for both of us.'"],["✓","'I want to understand what happened.'"],["✗","'I want you to admit you were wrong.'"],["✗","'I want to get this off my chest.'"]]}},{"h":"Pick the time and the place","p":"Not in front of others, not by text, not at the end of a long day, not when either of you is hungry or rushing. Ask for a time: 'Can we talk about the schedule tomorrow after lunch?' Giving notice lets the other person arrive ready instead of ambushed.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🕑","Not late, not hungry"],["🚪","Private"],["📵","In person, not text"],["📅","With notice"]]}},{"h":"Lead with what you saw, not what you concluded","p":"'You left early three times this week' is an observation; 'you don't care about this team' is a verdict, and verdicts start fights. Describe the specific, checkable behavior. Then say its effect on you. Then stop and let them respond.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Observation","'The report came in Thursday, not Tuesday'","'You raised your voice'","'The dishes stayed three days'"],"b":["Verdict","'You're unreliable'","'You're aggressive'","'You're lazy'"]}},{"h":"The four-part sentence","p":"When I see [the specific thing], I feel [one word], because I need [the underlying need]. Would you be willing to [a concrete request]? It feels stiff at first. It also removes blame, names the real issue and asks for something doable instead of a change of character.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["When… (the observable fact)","I feel… (one honest word, not 'that you…')","Because I need… (the thing underneath)","Would you be willing to… (specific, doable, soon)"]}},{"h":"Listen to understand, then prove it","p":"After you've said your piece, the other person will say theirs, often heatedly. Don't rebut. Reflect it back: 'So from where you sit, the deadline moved without anyone telling you.' When they say 'yes, exactly', the defensive part of the conversation is usually over.","v":{"t":"cycle","c":"until they say 'yes, that's it'","items":["They speak","You reflect it back","They correct or confirm","You ask what else"]}},{"h":"Separate the facts from the story","p":"Most conflicts are two people telling different stories about the same facts. 'You didn't reply' is a fact; 'you were ignoring me' is a story. Ask for theirs: 'Help me see how it looked from your side.' You're not conceding; you're collecting the missing half.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Same facts","Your story","Their story","A third story you both can hold"]}},{"h":"When it gets hot, slow it down","p":"A raised voice, interruptions, 'you always', 'you never': the conversation has left the subject. Name it gently and pause. 'This matters to me too much to do badly; can we take ten minutes?' Come back when you said you would. A break is not a loss.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["🔥","Absolutes: always, never"],["🔥","Bringing up old, unrelated grievances"],["🔥","Mind-reading: 'you did that because…'"],["❄️","Name it, pause, set a time to resume"]]}},{"h":"End with something concrete","p":"Agreements that work are small, specific and dated: 'I'll send the agenda by Monday; you'll flag conflicts within a day.' Thank them for having the conversation. Then follow up in a week; the follow-up is what tells them it was real, and not just a mood.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["One specific change each","A date to check how it's going","Thank them for the conversation","Actually check in on that date"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which opening is an observation rather than a verdict?","a":["'You never listen.'","'You looked at your phone while I was talking.'","'You're being disrespectful.'"],"c":1,"why":"It describes something both of you could see. Verdicts about character invite defence instead of discussion."},{"q":"The best response when the other person pushes back heatedly is to…","a":["Restate your point more firmly","Reflect back what they said until they confirm you've got it","End the conversation"],"c":1,"why":"Feeling understood defuses defensiveness. You can still disagree afterwards; you'll do it with the full picture."},{"q":"A useful way to close a hard conversation is…","a":["A general promise to 'do better'","One small, specific, dated change each, and a check-in date","Agreeing never to discuss it again"],"c":1,"why":"Specific and dated agreements can be kept and checked; vague ones evaporate."}],"take":["Name the goal first, pick a good moment, describe behavior rather than character.","When / I feel / because I need / would you be willing to.","Reflect before you rebut; end with one specific, dated change."],"src":[["Nonviolent Communication — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nonviolent_Communication"],["Difficult Conversations (Stone, Patton, Heen) — Harvard Negotiation Project","https://www.pon.harvard.edu/"],["Crucial Conversations — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crucial_Conversations"]],"cat":"life"},{"id":"how-sleep-works","title":"How sleep works","dek":"The 90-minute cycle, the two clocks and what actually helps","icon":"🌙","slides":[{"h":"A night is four to six laps","p":"Sleep runs in cycles of roughly 90 minutes: light sleep, deep slow-wave sleep, then REM, where most vivid dreaming happens. Deep sleep dominates the first half of the night; REM stretches out toward morning. Cutting the night short mostly cuts REM.","v":{"t":"cycle","c":"≈ 90 min","items":["N1 drowsy","N2 light","N3 deep","REM dreams"]}},{"h":"Deep sleep repairs; REM files","p":"Slow-wave sleep is when growth hormone is released, tissue repairs and the brain clears metabolic waste. REM is when memories are consolidated and emotions processed. You need both, which is why a full night beats a long nap.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Deep (N3)","front half of the night","body repair, waste clearance","hard to wake from"],"b":["REM","back half of the night","memory, mood, learning","muscles paralysed; dreams"]}},{"h":"Two clocks decide when you're sleepy","p":"Sleep pressure (adenosine) builds the longer you're awake; caffeine blocks it. The circadian clock, set by light, makes you alert in the morning and drowsy at night regardless. You fall asleep easily when both say yes, at your usual bedtime after a full day awake.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[6,10],[12,40],[18,70],[23,95],[24,95]],"pts2":[[6,20],[10,80],[15,60],[18,70],[23,20],[24,15]],"xl":"hour of day","l1":"sleep pressure","l2":"circadian alertness"}},{"h":"Seven to nine hours, for most adults","p":"Large studies put the sweet spot for adults at seven to nine hours; teenagers need eight to ten and sleep later by biology, not laziness. Short sleepers who 'do fine on five' mostly aren't: the deficit shows in reaction tests even when they feel okay.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":" h","max":17,"items":[["Newborn",16,"14–17"],["School age",10,"9–12"],["Teen",9,"8–10"],["Adult",8,"7–9"],["65+",7.5,"7–8"]]}},{"h":"Light is the lever","p":"Bright light in the morning, ideally outdoors within an hour of waking, anchors the clock and makes the next night's sleep come earlier. Dim light in the evening lets melatonin rise. Screens matter less for their blue light than for keeping you alert and awake.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🌅","Morning light","10 min outside"],["💡","Dim evenings","lamps, not overheads"],["📱","Screens","the content wakes you"],["🕶","Dark bedroom","or a mask"]]}},{"h":"Same wake time, every day","p":"A fixed wake time, including weekends, does more than any gadget. Sleeping in by three hours on Saturday is a time-zone shift the clock has to undo on Monday ('social jet lag'). If you must catch up, nap early and short: 20 minutes, before 3 p.m.","v":{"t":"big","n":"same hour","c":"wake time, seven days a week; the clock sets itself from it"}},{"h":"Caffeine, alcohol and the cool room","p":"Caffeine's half-life is about five hours, so a 4 p.m. coffee is a half-dose at 9 p.m. Alcohol makes you fall asleep faster and then fragments the second half of the night, cutting REM. Keep the room cool (about 65–68 °F / 18–20 °C); core temperature has to drop to sleep.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","Last coffee by early afternoon"],["✓","Room cool, dark, quiet"],["✗","A nightcap as a sleep aid"],["✗","Big meals or hard workouts right before bed"]]}},{"h":"If you can't sleep, get up","p":"Lying awake teaches the brain that bed is where you worry. After about twenty minutes, get up, do something dull in dim light, and return when sleepy. Chronic insomnia responds best to cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I), which works better than pills over time.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Awake 20 minutes? Get up","Dim light, boring task","Back to bed only when sleepy","Still bad after weeks? Ask about CBT-I"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"One sleep cycle lasts about…","a":["30 minutes","90 minutes","4 hours"],"c":1,"why":"Light, deep and REM sleep repeat roughly every 90 minutes, four to six times a night."},{"q":"Cutting a night from eight hours to five mostly loses…","a":["Deep sleep","REM sleep","Neither; sleep just gets more efficient"],"c":1,"why":"Deep sleep is front-loaded; REM stretches out toward morning, so it's the part the alarm removes."},{"q":"The single most effective habit for better sleep is…","a":["A fixed wake time every day","Sleeping in on weekends","A glass of wine before bed"],"c":0,"why":"The clock sets itself from when you wake and see light. Weekend lie-ins are a self-inflicted time-zone change."}],"take":["90-minute cycles; deep sleep early, REM late. Seven to nine hours.","Morning light and a fixed wake time anchor the clock.","Caffeine's half-life is five hours; alcohol wrecks the second half of the night."],"src":[["NIH: Brain basics: understanding sleep","https://www.ninds.nih.gov/health-information/public-education/brain-basics/brain-basics-understanding-sleep"],["CDC: About sleep","https://www.cdc.gov/sleep/about/"],["Sleep cycle — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sleep_cycle"]],"cat":"health"},{"id":"immune-system-vaccines","title":"How vaccines train the immune system","dek":"Two lines of defense, memory cells and herd immunity","icon":"🛡️","slides":[{"h":"The first line is general-purpose","p":"Skin, mucus, stomach acid and fever block or slow most invaders. If something gets through, innate immune cells attack anything that looks foreign within hours. It's fast and broad, but it doesn't learn, which is why a second cold is just as miserable as the first.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Skin & mucus","Acid, enzymes","Innate cells","Inflammation"]}},{"h":"The second line is specific, and slow the first time","p":"The adaptive system builds antibodies and T cells that fit one particular pathogen. The first time it meets a new germ, that takes a week or two, while you are sick. Afterward it keeps memory cells that respond in days the next time, often before you notice.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[0,0],[7,20],[14,50],[21,30],[30,15],[40,15],[42,95],[48,100],[60,70]],"xl":"days (second exposure at day 40)","yl":"antibodies","l1":"memory response"}},{"h":"A vaccine is a safe first meeting","p":"It shows the adaptive system a harmless version or piece of the germ (killed, weakened, a protein, or mRNA instructions to make one protein) so the slow first response happens without the disease. When the real thing arrives, the memory cells are already there.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🧫","Inactivated","polio shot, flu shot"],["🌱","Live-attenuated","MMR, chickenpox"],["🧩","Protein subunit","hepatitis B, HPV"],["📜","mRNA","COVID-19"]]}},{"h":"Side effects are the training in progress","p":"A sore arm, tiredness or a day of mild fever are the immune system doing what it was asked to do, not the disease. Serious reactions are rare and monitored. Nothing in a vaccine can cause the illness it prevents except a few live vaccines in people with weakened immunity, who are screened.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Common, 1–2 days","sore arm","tiredness, headache","mild fever"],"b":["Rare, monitored","allergic reaction (≈ 1 in a million)","treated on the spot","why you wait 15 minutes"]}},{"h":"Why some need boosters","p":"Memory fades at different rates. Measles protection lasts a lifetime after two doses; tetanus needs a top-up every ten years; flu mutates so fast that last year's memory doesn't fit this year's virus. Boosters remind the system or update it.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":100,"items":[["Measles (2 doses)",100,"lifelong"],["Hepatitis B",90,"decades+"],["Tetanus",50,"booster every 10 yrs"],["Flu",5,"yearly, new strains"]]}},{"h":"Herd immunity protects the people who can't be","p":"When enough people are immune, a germ can't find its next host and chains of infection break. The threshold depends on how contagious the disease is: about 95 percent for measles, lower for less contagious ones. Newborns and people on chemotherapy rely on everyone else.","v":{"t":"scale","min":0,"max":100,"unit":"%","zones":[[0,80,"outbreaks spread"],[80,95,"slowed"],[95,100,"measles stops"]],"marks":[[95,"measles ≈ 95%"],[80,"polio ≈ 80%"]]}},{"h":"What vaccines have done","p":"Smallpox killed about 300 million people in the 20th century; it was eradicated in 1980. Polio is down more than 99 percent since 1988. Measles deaths fell by more than 80 percent between 2000 and 2017. These are the largest public-health numbers there are.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1796","Jenner's cowpox vaccine"],["1955","Salk polio vaccine licensed"],["1963","Measles vaccine"],["1980","Smallpox declared eradicated"],["2020","mRNA vaccines authorised"]]}},{"h":"Keeping your own system in shape","p":"There's no supplement that 'boosts' immunity; the things that help are the dull ones. Sleep (people sleeping under six hours catch more colds), moving most days, not smoking, enough protein and vegetables, washing hands, and staying up to date on the shots your doctor recommends for your age.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","7–9 hours of sleep"],["✓","Regular moderate exercise"],["✓","Hand washing, 20 seconds"],["✓","Vaccines current for your age"],["✗","'Immune-boosting' supplements"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A vaccine works by…","a":["Killing germs already in the body","Letting the adaptive immune system meet a harmless version first","Replacing the immune system for a year"],"c":1,"why":"The slow first response happens safely, leaving memory cells ready for the real pathogen."},{"q":"A sore arm and tiredness the day after a shot are…","a":["Signs of the disease starting","The immune response being trained","A sign the vaccine failed"],"c":1,"why":"Mild, short reactions are the system responding. Serious reactions are rare and are why you wait fifteen minutes."},{"q":"Measles needs about what share of a community immune to stop spreading?","a":["50%","75%","95%"],"c":2,"why":"Measles is one of the most contagious diseases known, so its herd-immunity threshold is very high."}],"take":["Innate immunity is fast and general; adaptive immunity is specific and remembers.","A vaccine is the first meeting without the illness; side effects are the training.","Herd immunity protects those who can't be vaccinated; for measles that takes about 95%."],"src":[["CDC: How vaccines work (Understanding how vaccines work)","https://www.cdc.gov/vaccines/basics/explaining-how-vaccines-work.html"],["WHO: How do vaccines work?","https://www.who.int/news-room/feature-stories/detail/how-do-vaccines-work"],["Herd immunity — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herd_immunity"]],"cat":"health"},{"id":"caffeine","title":"What caffeine does","dek":"How it keeps you awake, how long it lasts and how much is too much","icon":"⚡","slides":[{"h":"It blocks the sleepy signal","p":"Adenosine builds up in the brain while you're awake and makes you drowsy. Caffeine fits the same receptors without activating them, so the signal is muffled. The adenosine is still there, piling up behind the blockade, which is why the crash arrives when the caffeine wears off.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Adenosine builds","Caffeine blocks receptors","You feel alert","Caffeine fades","Adenosine floods in"]}},{"h":"Half of it is still there five hours later","p":"Caffeine's half-life averages about five hours in healthy adults, ranging from three to seven by genetics, and much longer in pregnancy and with some medications. A 200 mg coffee at 3 p.m. leaves about 100 mg at 8 p.m. and 50 mg at 1 a.m.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[0,200],[5,100],[10,50],[15,25],[20,12]],"xl":"hours after a 200 mg coffee","yl":"mg in the body","l1":"≈ 12 mg at 20 h"}},{"h":"How much is in what","p":"Numbers vary by brew and brand: a home drip coffee is about 95 mg a cup, espresso about 65 mg a shot, black tea 45, green tea 30, a cola 35, an energy drink 80 to 300. 'Decaf' still has 2 to 15 mg.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":" mg","max":300,"items":[["Energy drink (16 oz)",160,"80–300"],["Drip coffee (8 oz)",95],["Espresso (1 shot)",65],["Black tea",45],["Cola (12 oz)",35],["Green tea",30],["Decaf coffee",5,"2–15"]]}},{"h":"400 mg a day, for most healthy adults","p":"That's the FDA's figure for an amount not generally linked to harm: about four cups of coffee. Pregnancy guidance is 200 mg. Children and teens should have little or none. Energy drinks are the usual way people blow past it without noticing.","v":{"t":"scale","min":0,"max":600,"unit":" mg","zones":[[0,200,"pregnancy limit"],[200,400,"most healthy adults"],[400,600,"jitters, poor sleep, palpitations"]],"marks":[[400,"FDA: 400 mg"]]}},{"h":"The benefits are real, and modest","p":"Caffeine improves reaction time, alertness and endurance performance, and habitual moderate coffee drinking is associated with lower risk of several diseases in large observational studies. It also raises heart rate and blood pressure briefly and can worsen anxiety and reflux.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Upside","alertness, reaction time","endurance exercise","associations with lower risk of type 2 diabetes, Parkinson's"],"b":["Downside","sleep, hours later","anxiety, jitters","reflux, palpitations","withdrawal headaches"]}},{"h":"Tolerance and withdrawal","p":"Regular use grows more adenosine receptors, so the same dose does less; the morning cup then mostly reverses overnight withdrawal rather than adding alertness. Stopping suddenly brings headaches and fatigue for two to nine days. Taper by a cup every few days instead.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Days 1–3: swap one cup for half-caf or tea","Days 4–7: drop another","Expect a headache; it passes","Resume at a lower dose, or not"]}},{"h":"Timing it well","p":"Delay the first coffee until an hour or so after waking, when the natural cortisol rise has done its work. Keep the last one eight to ten hours before bed. A 'coffee nap' (drink, then nap 20 minutes while it kicks in) is the sharpest afternoon trick there is.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🌅","First cup","~1 h after waking"],["☕","Coffee nap","drink, nap 20 min"],["🕑","Last cup","8–10 h before bed"],["💧","Water alongside","it's not a diuretic for regulars"]]}},{"h":"When to be careful","p":"Pregnancy, heart rhythm problems, anxiety disorders, reflux, insomnia and some medications (certain antibiotics, oral contraceptives) all change the calculus. Pure caffeine powder has killed people: a teaspoon is roughly 28 cups of coffee. Energy drinks plus alcohol hide how drunk you are.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["⚠️","Pregnancy: 200 mg"],["⚠️","Arrhythmia or anxiety: talk to a clinician"],["⚠️","Never pure caffeine powder"],["⚠️","Energy drinks + alcohol mask intoxication"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Caffeine keeps you awake by…","a":["Adding energy to cells","Blocking the receptors adenosine uses to signal sleepiness","Lowering body temperature"],"c":1,"why":"The sleepiness signal is muffled, not removed. When the caffeine clears, the built-up adenosine hits at once."},{"q":"After a 200 mg coffee at 3 p.m., roughly how much is still in you at 8 p.m.?","a":["None","About 100 mg","About 200 mg"],"c":1,"why":"The average half-life is about five hours, so half remains, and a quarter at 1 a.m."},{"q":"The FDA's guideline for healthy adults is up to about…","a":["100 mg a day","400 mg a day","1,000 mg a day"],"c":1,"why":"About four cups of coffee. Pregnancy guidance is 200 mg; children should have little or none."}],"take":["It blocks adenosine; the crash is the backlog arriving.","Half-life about five hours: last coffee eight to ten hours before bed.","400 mg a day for most adults, 200 in pregnancy; energy drinks add up fast."],"src":[["FDA: Spilling the beans: how much caffeine is too much?","https://www.fda.gov/consumers/consumer-updates/spilling-beans-how-much-caffeine-too-much"],["Caffeine — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caffeine"],["Mayo Clinic: Caffeine content for coffee, tea, soda and more","https://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/in-depth/caffeine/art-20049372"]],"cat":"health"},{"id":"exercise-heart-rate","title":"Exercise: zones, minutes and strength","dek":"The 150-minute guideline, heart-rate zones and the talk test","icon":"🏃","slides":[{"h":"The guideline fits on one line","p":"At least 150 minutes a week of moderate activity (or 75 vigorous), plus muscle-strengthening work on two days. Spread however you like; even bouts of a few minutes count. Going from nothing to a little is the step with the biggest health payoff.","v":{"t":"stack","c":"one week","parts":[["150 min moderate cardio (5 × 30)",70],["2 strength sessions (~2 × 30)",30]]}},{"h":"Moderate and vigorous, by the talk test","p":"Moderate: you can talk but not sing (brisk walk, easy cycling). Vigorous: you can say a few words before needing a breath (running, hard cycling, singles tennis). No device needed; the test tracks heart rate closely enough for anyone who isn't training for a race.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Moderate","talk, can't sing","brisk walking, 3+ mph","doubles tennis, mowing","about 64–76% of max HR"],"b":["Vigorous","a few words at a time","jogging, running","swimming laps, hiking uphill","about 77–93% of max HR"]}},{"h":"Estimating your maximum heart rate","p":"The rough rule is 220 minus your age: about 180 for a 40-year-old. It's an average with a wide spread; your real max may be 10 or more beats either way. Zones based on it are approximate, which is fine for health. Athletes test it properly.","v":{"t":"cols","unit":" bpm","items":[["20",200],["30",190],["40",180],["50",170],["60",160],["70",150]]}},{"h":"The five zones","p":"Zone 1 is recovery; zone 2 (roughly 60–70 percent of max) is the long, conversational effort that builds the aerobic engine and burns the most fat per minute; zones 3–4 build pace and lactate tolerance; zone 5 is short all-out intervals. Most training for most people belongs in zone 2.","v":{"t":"scale","min":50,"max":100,"unit":"%","zones":[[50,60,"Z1 easy"],[60,70,"Z2 base"],[70,80,"Z3 tempo"],[80,90,"Z4 hard"],[90,100,"Z5 max"]],"marks":[[65,"most minutes here"]]}},{"h":"Strength is not optional","p":"Muscle mass falls from around age 30 and faster after 60. Two sessions a week of the big movements (squat, hinge, push, pull, carry) preserve it, protect bones, joints and blood sugar, and are the strongest predictor of staying independent later. Bodyweight counts.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🦵","Squat","chair stands"],["🏋️","Hinge","deadlift, hip bridge"],["🤸","Push","push-ups"],["🧗","Pull","rows, bands"],["🧳","Carry","farmer's walk"],["🧘","Balance","one-leg stands"]]}},{"h":"Resting heart rate is a free report card","p":"A normal adult resting pulse is about 60 to 100; fit people often sit in the 50s. As you train, yours drifts down. A morning pulse 7 to 10 beats above your usual is an early sign of overtraining, poor sleep or an illness coming on. Measure it before getting up.","v":{"t":"scale","min":40,"max":110,"unit":" bpm","zones":[[40,60,"athletic"],[60,100,"normal"],[100,110,"see a clinician if persistent"]],"marks":[[55,"trained"]]}},{"h":"Recovery is where you get fitter","p":"The workout is the stress; the adaptation happens afterward, during sleep and easy days. Hard days need easy days after them. Soreness that lasts more than three days, a rising resting pulse or getting slower while training harder all mean back off, not push through.","v":{"t":"cycle","c":"the training loop","items":["Stress (workout)","Recover (sleep, food)","Adapt (fitter)","Slightly more stress"]}},{"h":"Starting from zero","p":"Walk. Ten minutes after meals, then twenty, then brisk. Add two days of chair stands, wall push-ups and a band. Build over weeks, not days; the injuries happen when enthusiasm outruns tendons. Anyone with heart disease, chest pain or dizziness on exertion should check in with a clinician first.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Weeks 1–2: 10-minute walks, most days","Weeks 3–4: 20–30 minutes, brisk","Add 2 short strength sessions","Month 2+: one harder session a week"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The weekly guideline for adults is…","a":["30 minutes total","150 minutes moderate plus 2 strength days","An hour of vigorous exercise daily"],"c":1,"why":"150 moderate or 75 vigorous minutes, plus muscle work on two days. Any amount above zero already helps."},{"q":"During moderate exercise you should be able to…","a":["Sing comfortably","Talk but not sing","Say only a word or two"],"c":1,"why":"That's the talk test. A few words at a time means vigorous; singing means easy."},{"q":"A morning resting pulse 10 beats above your usual most likely means…","a":["You're getting fitter","You need more recovery or you're getting sick","Nothing; it's random"],"c":1,"why":"Resting heart rate falls with fitness; a jump is an early warning of overtraining, poor sleep or illness."}],"take":["150 moderate minutes a week plus two strength days; the talk test sets the intensity.","Most training time belongs in the easy, conversational zone.","Strength preserves independence; recovery is where fitness is built."],"src":[["CDC: Adult physical activity guidelines","https://www.cdc.gov/physical-activity-basics/guidelines/adults.html"],["American Heart Association: Target heart rates","https://www.heart.org/en/healthy-living/fitness/fitness-basics/target-heart-rates"],["Heart rate — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heart_rate"]],"cat":"health"},{"id":"first-aid-basics","title":"First aid: the five things to know","dek":"CPR, choking, bleeding, burns and when to call","icon":"🩹","slides":[{"h":"Call first, or call fast","p":"An unresponsive adult who isn't breathing normally: call emergency services (911 in the U.S.) or have someone call, and send someone for an AED. Put the phone on speaker; dispatchers talk you through everything on these cards. These are reminders, not a substitute for a course.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Check: is the scene safe?","Tap and shout: any response?","Not breathing normally? Call 911, get an AED","Start compressions"]}},{"h":"CPR: push hard, push fast","p":"Heel of one hand on the center of the chest, other hand on top, arms straight. Push at least 2 inches deep, 100 to 120 times a minute (the beat of 'Stayin' Alive'), letting the chest fully rise between pushes. Hands-only CPR is recommended for untrained bystanders with adults.","v":{"t":"big","n":"100–120","c":"compressions a minute, at least 2 inches deep, center of the chest"}},{"h":"The AED does the thinking","p":"Turn it on and do what it says. It reads the heart rhythm and shocks only if a shock will help. Attach the pads to bare skin where the pictures show, make sure nobody is touching the person, press the button if told, then go straight back to compressions.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Power on","Pads on bare chest","Everyone clear","Shock if advised","Resume CPR"]}},{"h":"Choking: ask, then act","p":"Can they cough or speak? Encourage coughing and stay close. If they can't breathe, cough or speak: five firm back blows between the shoulder blades, then five abdominal thrusts (fist above the navel, pull in and up), and repeat. If they collapse, start CPR. Infants get back blows and chest thrusts instead.","v":{"t":"cycle","c":"until it's out or they collapse","items":["5 back blows","5 abdominal thrusts","Check the mouth"]}},{"h":"Severe bleeding: press, and don't let go","p":"Direct, firm pressure with a cloth or your hands, straight onto the wound, for at least ten minutes without peeking. If it soaks through, add more on top. For a limb wound that won't stop, a tourniquet two to three inches above the wound, tightened until bleeding stops; note the time.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Call 911 for heavy bleeding","Press hard, directly on the wound","Don't lift to look; add layers","Limb still bleeding? Tourniquet high and tight, note the time"]}},{"h":"Burns: cool water, not ice","p":"Run cool (not cold) water over the burn for 20 minutes; it limits damage even if you start late. Remove rings and watches before swelling. Don't use ice, butter or toothpaste; don't pop blisters. Cover loosely with cling film or a clean dry dressing. Face, hands, genitals, large or deep burns: hospital.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Do","cool running water, 20 min","remove jewellery","cover loosely","pain relief"],"b":["Don't","ice or iced water","butter, oil, toothpaste","pop blisters","pull stuck clothing off"]}},{"h":"Stroke and heart attack: the clock is the treatment","p":"Stroke: FAST. Face drooping, Arm weakness, Speech slurred, Time to call 911; note when symptoms started. Heart attack: chest pressure, pain spreading to arm, jaw or back, sweating, breathlessness; women often get nausea or fatigue. Call, don't drive. Chew an aspirin if advised and not allergic.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["😐","Face droops"],["💪","Arm weak"],["🗣","Speech slurred"],["⏱","Time: call now"]]}},{"h":"The recovery position, and a real course","p":"Someone unconscious but breathing: roll them onto their side, head tilted back, top knee bent, so the airway stays open and vomit drains. Then take a course. Four hours with a mannequin and an instructor makes all of this automatic, which is what counts when it's your kitchen.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","Unconscious, breathing: recovery position"],["✓","A first-aid kit you've opened before"],["✓","An AED location you know (work, gym)"],["✓","A CPR/first-aid course every two years"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The rate for chest compressions is…","a":["60 a minute","100–120 a minute","As fast as possible"],"c":1,"why":"100 to 120 per minute, at least two inches deep, letting the chest fully recoil."},{"q":"The first thing to do for a burn is…","a":["Apply ice","Run cool water over it for 20 minutes","Cover it in butter"],"c":1,"why":"Cool running water limits the depth of the burn; ice damages tissue, and oils trap heat."},{"q":"Someone is choking and cannot speak, cough or breathe. You should…","a":["Give them water","Alternate five back blows and five abdominal thrusts","Wait for it to clear on its own"],"c":1,"why":"Repeat until the object comes out. If they collapse, start CPR."}],"take":["Call 911, start compressions at 100–120 a minute, send for the AED.","Choking: back blows and abdominal thrusts. Bleeding: hard, direct pressure.","Burns: cool water for 20 minutes. Stroke: FAST. Then take a real course."],"src":[["American Red Cross: First aid steps","https://www.redcross.org/take-a-class/first-aid/performing-first-aid/first-aid-steps"],["American Heart Association: Hands-only CPR","https://cpr.heart.org/en/cpr-courses-and-kits/hands-only-cpr"],["NHS: Burns and scalds","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/burns-and-scalds/"]],"cat":"health"},{"id":"rome-republic-to-empire","title":"Rome: from republic to empire","dek":"Five centuries of a republic, and the decade that ended it","icon":"🏛","slides":[{"h":"A city that threw out its king","p":"Tradition dates the Roman Republic to 509 BC, when the last king was expelled and power passed to elected magistrates. The founding fear was one man ruling alone; the whole design was built to prevent it, which makes how it ended the point of the story.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["753 BC","Legendary founding of Rome"],["509 BC","Kings expelled; the Republic begins"],["264–146 BC","Punic Wars: Carthage destroyed"],["49 BC","Caesar crosses the Rubicon"],["27 BC","Octavian becomes Augustus"]]}},{"h":"Power split so nobody kept it","p":"Two consuls served one year and could veto each other. The Senate of former magistrates advised, controlled money and held real authority. Assemblies of citizens passed laws and elected officials; tribunes could veto on behalf of the plebeians. In an emergency a dictator served six months, then stepped down.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["👥","Two consuls","one year, mutual veto"],["🏛","Senate","~300 ex-magistrates"],["🗳","Assemblies","citizens vote laws"],["✋","Tribunes","veto for the plebs"]]}},{"h":"Conquest made the republic rich and unstable","p":"By 146 BC Rome had destroyed Carthage and taken Greece. Plunder and slaves poured in; wealthy senators bought up farmland into vast estates worked by slaves, and the small farmers who had filled the legions drifted landless into the city. The army and the land became the political questions.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":60,"items":[["Italy",5,"by 270 BC"],["Sicily, Sardinia, Spain",15,"by 200 BC"],["Greece, N. Africa",30,"by 146 BC"],["Gaul, Syria, Egypt",60,"by 30 BC (relative reach)"]]}},{"h":"Reformers, and the first political murders","p":"In 133 BC the tribune Tiberius Gracchus pushed land redistribution and was clubbed to death by senators; his brother Gaius followed a decade later. The norm against killing political opponents, once broken, never came back. Each generation's violence set the floor for the next.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["133 BC","Tiberius Gracchus killed"],["121 BC","Gaius Gracchus dies in street fighting"],["88 BC","Sulla marches his army on Rome"],["82 BC","Sulla's proscriptions: enemies' names posted, killed"]]}},{"h":"Soldiers who answered to generals","p":"Around 107 BC Gaius Marius opened the legions to the landless poor, who served for pay and for the land their general could win them at retirement. Their loyalty followed the man, not the state. Sulla, Pompey and Caesar each commanded armies that were, in practice, theirs.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Landless recruits","Paid by the general","Land at retirement","Loyal to him","Army as political weapon"]}},{"h":"The Rubicon, 49 BC","p":"Julius Caesar, back from conquering Gaul, was ordered to give up his command and face prosecution. Instead he led a legion across the Rubicon, the stream marking the limit of his province, into Italy. Civil war followed; Pompey lost; Caesar became dictator for life in 44 BC and was stabbed to death a month later.","v":{"t":"quote","q":"Alea iacta est — the die is cast.","by":"attributed to Caesar at the Rubicon, 10 January 49 BC"}},{"h":"Augustus keeps the forms, takes the power","p":"Caesar's heir Octavian defeated Antony and Cleopatra at Actium in 31 BC. In 27 BC he 'restored the Republic' to the Senate, which in return named him Augustus and left him command of the armies and the provinces that had them. Consuls were still elected. They just no longer decided anything.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Kept","the Senate","consuls and elections","the word 'republic'","'first citizen', not king"],"b":["Taken","command of the legions","the frontier provinces","treasury and grain supply","tribune's veto, for life"]}},{"h":"Why it matters","p":"The Republic lasted almost five hundred years; the slide from political murder to private armies to one-man rule took about a century. Its institutions, the two consuls, the vetoes, the term limits, were copied by the American founders, who had read how it ended and tried to design around it.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Norms break: opponents killed (133 BC)","Armies become personal (107 BC on)","Generals march on Rome (88, 49 BC)","One man holds all the offices (27 BC)"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The Roman Republic was designed above all to prevent…","a":["Foreign invasion","One man ruling alone","Slave revolts"],"c":1,"why":"Two consuls with mutual vetoes, one-year terms and a time-limited dictatorship were all guards against a king."},{"q":"Marius's army reform mattered because soldiers became loyal to…","a":["The Senate","Their general, who paid them and promised land","The gods"],"c":1,"why":"A private army is a political weapon. Sulla, Pompey and Caesar each used one."},{"q":"Caesar crossing the Rubicon in 49 BC was significant because…","a":["It was a major river","It meant bringing an army into Italy, which was treason","It was the border with Gaul"],"c":1,"why":"Generals had to leave their armies at the provincial border. Crossing it under arms began the civil war."}],"take":["509 BC to 27 BC: a republic built to prevent one-man rule, undone in about a century.","Broken norms, then private armies, then a general in the city.","Augustus kept every institution and emptied them of power."],"src":[["Roman Republic — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/place/Roman-Republic"],["Crisis of the Roman Republic — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crisis_of_the_Roman_Republic"],["Augustus — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Augustus-Roman-emperor"]],"cat":"history"},{"id":"silk-road","title":"The Silk Road","dek":"Not one road, not mostly silk: fifteen centuries of exchange","icon":"🐫","slides":[{"h":"A network, named in 1877","p":"The German geographer Ferdinand von Richthofen coined 'Seidenstraße' for routes that linked China to the Mediterranean from about 130 BC, when the Han dynasty opened trade westward, to 1453, when the Ottomans took Constantinople. No one travelled the whole length; goods passed hand to hand.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["138 BC","Zhang Qian's mission west for the Han"],["1st c. AD","Roman elites buy Chinese silk"],["7th–9th c.","Tang peak; Buddhism moves east"],["1271–95","Marco Polo's journey"],["1453","Constantinople falls; overland routes decline"]]}},{"h":"The route, in six stops","p":"From Chang'an (Xi'an) along the Gansu corridor, around the Taklamakan desert by the north or south oasis towns, over the Pamirs to Samarkand and Bukhara, through Persia to Baghdad, and on to Antioch and the Mediterranean ports. A parallel maritime route ran from south China through India to the Red Sea.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Chang'an","Dunhuang","Kashgar","Samarkand","Baghdad → Antioch"]}},{"h":"What moved east, what moved west","p":"Westward: silk, paper, porcelain, tea, gunpowder, the compass. Eastward: horses, glass, wool, gold and silver, grapes, and crops like alfalfa. Most trade was in short hops between neighbouring markets; a bolt of silk might change hands a dozen times before Rome.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["West from China","silk, porcelain","paper (reaches Europe ~12th c.)","gunpowder, compass","tea, rhubarb"],"b":["East toward China","horses (the Han's great want)","glassware","wool, linen","grapes, walnuts, alfalfa"]}},{"h":"Ideas travelled with the goods","p":"Buddhism went from India into Central Asia and China along the trade routes; the cave temples at Dunhuang are its milestones. Islam spread east along the same roads after the 7th century. Nestorian Christianity reached Tang China. Mathematics, astronomy and medicine moved in both directions.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["☸️","Buddhism","India → China, 1st c. on"],["☪️","Islam","7th c. onward"],["✝️","Nestorian Christianity","to Tang China, 635"],["🔢","Numerals, astronomy","India ↔ Persia ↔ China"]]}},{"h":"The oasis cities got rich on the traffic","p":"Samarkand, Bukhara, Merv and Kashgar lived by caravanserais, markets and tolls. Sogdian merchants from around Samarkand were the great middlemen for centuries; their language was the lingua franca of the route. Empires that controlled the chokepoints, Persia especially, taxed everything.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":100,"items":[["Samarkand",100,"Sogdian hub"],["Bukhara",85],["Merv",80,"largest city in the world c. 1150"],["Kashgar",70,"where the desert routes rejoin"],["Dunhuang",60,"gateway to China"]]}},{"h":"Paper and gunpowder changed the West","p":"Papermaking reached the Islamic world after 751 (Chinese prisoners taken at Talas, according to tradition) and Europe by the 12th century, making books cheap enough for the printing press to matter. Gunpowder arrived in the 13th century and remade warfare and castles within two hundred years.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["105","Paper credited to Cai Lun in Han China"],["751","Papermaking reaches Samarkand"],["1150s","Paper mills in Spain"],["1267","Roger Bacon describes gunpowder"],["1450s","Gutenberg prints on paper"]]}},{"h":"The Mongol peace, and the plague","p":"In the 13th century the Mongol Empire unified the route from Korea to Hungary; travel was safer than ever and Marco Polo's generation went to the Yuan court. The same open roads carried the Black Death westward in the 1340s, killing a third or more of Europe. Connection cut both ways.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Pax Mongolica","one authority, Korea to Hungary","postal relays every 25 miles","Europeans reach Beijing"],"b":["The cost","plague moves with caravans","Caffa, 1346 → Sicily, 1347","a third of Europe dead by 1351"]}},{"h":"Why it faded, and why it's back","p":"After 1453 the Ottomans controlled the western end, the Mongol order was gone, and Portuguese ships found the way round Africa in 1498; the sea was cheaper. Today China's 'Belt and Road' borrows the name for ports, railways and loans across the same geography, with the same arguments about who profits.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["1453: Ottomans take Constantinople","Mongol unity fractures; tolls and bandits rise","1498: Vasco da Gama reaches India by sea","2013: 'Belt and Road' revives the brand"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The term 'Silk Road' was coined…","a":["By Chinese Han officials","By a German geographer in 1877","By Marco Polo"],"c":1,"why":"Ferdinand von Richthofen named it long after the routes had declined. People who used it had no single name for it."},{"q":"Most goods on the Silk Road travelled…","a":["The full length with one merchant","In short hops between neighbouring markets","By sea only"],"c":1,"why":"Almost nobody went end to end. Silk changed hands many times, rising in price at each stop."},{"q":"Besides goods, the routes carried…","a":["Only soldiers","Religions, technologies and, in the 1340s, plague","Nothing else of note"],"c":1,"why":"Buddhism, Islam, paper and gunpowder moved along them; so did the Black Death under the Mongol peace."}],"take":["A network of hops, not one road, from about 130 BC to 1453.","Silk and paper went west; horses and glass went east; religions and plague went both ways.","It declined when the sea route round Africa made it unnecessary."],"src":[["Silk Road — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Silk-Road-trade-route"],["UNESCO: About the Silk Roads","https://en.unesco.org/silkroad/about-silk-roads"],["Silk Road — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silk_Road"]],"cat":"history"},{"id":"black-death","title":"The Black Death","dek":"Four years that killed a third of Europe and rearranged everything after","icon":"🕯","slides":[{"h":"A bacterium carried by fleas","p":"Yersinia pestis lives in rodents and the fleas that bite them. Bubonic plague causes swollen lymph nodes (buboes), fever and death in half of cases untreated; the pneumonic form spreads person to person by cough and kills nearly everyone. Antibiotics treat it today; in 1347 nobody knew what it was.","v":{"t":"flow","items":[["🐀","Rodents"],["🦟","Fleas"],["🧍","People"],["😮‍💨","Coughs (pneumonic)"]]}},{"h":"From the steppe to Sicily in a few years","p":"The outbreak probably began in Central Asia in the 1330s and travelled the Mongol trade routes. In 1346 it reached the Genoese port of Caffa on the Black Sea; ships carried it to Messina in Sicily in October 1347, and from there to every port in the Mediterranean within months.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1346","Caffa, Crimea"],["Oct 1347","Messina, Sicily"],["Jan 1348","Marseille, Genoa, Venice"],["Jun 1348","Paris; England by summer"],["1349","Scandinavia, Ireland"],["1351","Russia; first wave ends"]]}},{"h":"How many died","p":"Estimates for Europe run from a third to a half of the population between 1347 and 1351: roughly 25 to 50 million people. Cities were worst hit; Florence may have lost 60 percent. The Middle East and North Africa suffered comparably. World population didn't recover for about two centuries.","v":{"t":"donut","c":"Europe, 1347–51","parts":[["died",40],["survived",60]]}},{"h":"What people thought was happening","p":"Physicians blamed bad air (miasma) and the planets; the Church called it divine punishment. Flagellants whipped themselves through towns. Jewish communities were accused of poisoning wells and massacred in dozens of cities despite papal condemnation. Quarantine, from the Venetian for forty days, was the one idea that worked.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🌫","Miasma","'bad air' theory"],["🪐","The planets","a 1345 conjunction"],["⛓","Flagellants","public penance"],["⛵","Quarantine","quaranta giorni: 40 days"]]}},{"h":"Labor became scarce, and valuable","p":"With a third of the workers gone, survivors could demand wages; English rural wages roughly doubled over the following decades. Lords tried to freeze pay by law (the Statute of Labourers, 1351) and failed. Peasants moved to better terms; the bond of serfdom loosened across Western Europe.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[1300,100],[1340,100],[1350,130],[1370,170],[1400,200],[1450,210]],"xl":"year","yl":"real wages (index)","l1":"roughly doubled"}},{"h":"Revolts, and a different church","p":"Pressure between wage laws and labor shortages fed the Jacquerie in France (1358) and the English Peasants' Revolt (1381). The Church lost clergy at high rates and credibility with them; the questions survivors asked about suffering and authority fed into the Reformation a century and a half later.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1351","Statute of Labourers caps wages"],["1358","Jacquerie, northern France"],["1381","Peasants' Revolt reaches London"],["1517","Luther's theses: longer arc"]]}},{"h":"It kept coming back","p":"The 1347 wave was the first of many: plague returned every decade or two for three centuries. The Great Plague of London in 1665 killed perhaps a quarter of the city. A third pandemic began in China in 1855 and carried the disease worldwide; the bacterium was identified in Hong Kong in 1894.","v":{"t":"cols","unit":"","items":[["1348",100,"first wave"],["1361",20,"'children's plague'"],["1400",10],["1500",8],["1665",25,"London"],["1720",10,"Marseille"]]}},{"h":"The long shadow","p":"Historians argue about how much the plague caused: higher wages, land for the taking, a shift from labor to capital, more meat in the diet, the Renaissance's patrons. It is still with us: a few thousand cases a year worldwide, treatable with antibiotics if caught early, including a handful in the American West.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["→","Wages up, serfdom weakened in the West"],["→","Labor-saving tools worth inventing"],["→","Quarantine as public policy"],["→","Still endemic in rodents; ~1,000–3,000 cases a year"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The Black Death was caused by…","a":["A virus spread by birds","A bacterium carried by fleas on rodents","Contaminated water"],"c":1,"why":"Yersinia pestis, identified in 1894. Bubonic plague came from flea bites; pneumonic plague passed by cough."},{"q":"Between 1347 and 1351, Europe lost roughly…","a":["5% of its population","A third to a half","90%"],"c":1,"why":"Estimates are 25 to 50 million dead. Some cities lost more than half."},{"q":"One lasting economic effect of the plague was…","a":["Falling wages for survivors","Rising wages and weaker serfdom in the West","No change; populations recovered within a decade"],"c":1,"why":"Scarce labor could bargain. Wage caps like England's Statute of Labourers failed to hold."}],"take":["A flea-borne bacterium that moved along trade routes: Caffa 1346, Messina 1347, all of Europe by 1351.","A third to a half of Europe died; the dead left scarce, better-paid labor behind.","Quarantine was the one idea that worked; the disease is still here and now treatable."],"src":[["Black Death — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/event/Black-Death"],["CDC: Plague","https://www.cdc.gov/plague/"],["Black Death — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Death"]],"cat":"history"},{"id":"printing-press","title":"The printing press","dek":"How a goldsmith's machine in Mainz made the modern world argue","icon":"📜","slides":[{"h":"Before 1450, a book was a year of someone's life","p":"Manuscripts were copied by hand, mostly in monasteries, on parchment made from animal skins. A Bible took a scribe about a year and cost as much as a farm. Europe had perhaps a few hundred thousand books in total. Knowledge moved at the pace of the copyist.","v":{"t":"big","n":"≈ 1 year","c":"for one scribe to copy one Bible"}},{"h":"Printing wasn't new; movable metal type was","p":"China printed from carved wood blocks from the 9th century and Bi Sheng made ceramic movable type around 1040; Korea cast metal type by 1377. Chinese script's thousands of characters limited the payoff. Gutenberg's innovation, around 1440 in Mainz, was a system: cast metal type, oil-based ink and a screw press.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["868","Diamond Sutra, earliest dated printed book (China)"],["c. 1040","Bi Sheng's movable type"],["1377","Jikji, metal type, Korea"],["c. 1440","Gutenberg's press, Mainz"],["1455","The 42-line Bible"]]}},{"h":"The system, piece by piece","p":"A hand mould cast identical letters from a lead-tin-antimony alloy, thousands an hour once set up. Letters were composed into pages, inked with a sticky oil-based ink that stuck to metal, and pressed onto paper with a screw press adapted from wine making. Every part existed; the combination didn't.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Cast type","Set a page","Ink it","Press on paper","Repeat ~250×/day"]}},{"h":"The numbers","p":"Gutenberg's Bible ran to about 180 copies. By 1500, presses in some 270 European cities had printed an estimated 20 million books, more than all the scribes of the previous thousand years. By 1600, as many as 200 million. The price of a book fell by perhaps two-thirds within decades.","v":{"t":"cols","unit":"","items":[["1450",0,"~0"],["1500",20,"20M"],["1550",80,"~80M"],["1600",200,"~200M"]]}},{"h":"Luther's theses went viral","p":"In 1517 Martin Luther nailed up ninety-five academic theses in Latin. Printers translated and reprinted them; within weeks they were across Germany, within months across Europe. Luther wrote in German for the press, and pamphlets were a third of German printing by the 1520s. The Reformation was a media event.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":100,"items":[["Luther's works printed 1518–25",100,"≈ 3,000 editions"],["Everything else in German, same years",60]]}},{"h":"Science needs the same text in many hands","p":"Printed books let scholars in different cities check the same diagrams and tables, spot errors and build on each other. Vesalius's anatomy (1543) and Copernicus's heliocentric model (1543) depended on exact, repeatable illustrations. Standardised maps, star tables and mathematics followed.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🫀","Vesalius, 1543","anatomy, same plates everywhere"],["☀️","Copernicus, 1543","heliocentrism circulates"],["🗺","Mercator, 1569","a standard map"],["📐","Euclid in print","1482; a thousand editions"]]}},{"h":"Literacy, languages and nations","p":"Cheap texts in the vernacular gave people reasons to read, and printers standardised spelling and grammar as they went: printing fixed English, French and German into their modern forms. Reading shared news and books in the same language is part of how people came to think of themselves as nations.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[1500,10],[1600,20],[1700,35],[1800,55],[1900,85]],"xl":"year","yl":"literacy, W. Europe (approx. %)","l1":"≈ 85%"}},{"h":"Censorship arrived with the press","p":"Authorities answered with licensing, indexes of banned books (the Catholic Index from 1559) and the first copyright laws (England, 1710). Every later information technology, the telegraph, radio, the internet, has replayed the cycle: cheap copies, a flood of pamphlets, panic about misinformation, new rules.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["New copying technology","Flood of cheap, unvetted material","Establishment alarm; 'misinformation'","Licensing, indexes, copyright","A new normal, more literate than before"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Gutenberg's key contribution was…","a":["Inventing printing from scratch","Combining cast metal type, oil ink and a screw press into one system","Inventing paper"],"c":1,"why":"Block printing and movable type existed in Asia centuries earlier. The workable, scalable combination was his."},{"q":"By 1500, roughly how many books had European presses produced?","a":["20,000","20 million","2 billion"],"c":1,"why":"About 20 million in fifty years, more than all the manuscripts of the previous millennium."},{"q":"The press mattered to the Reformation because…","a":["Luther owned a printing company","Pamphlets spread his arguments across Europe in months","It printed indulgences"],"c":1,"why":"Printers reprinted and translated his theses within weeks. He wrote for the press in German."}],"take":["Around 1440, Gutenberg made a system of cast type, oil ink and a screw press.","Twenty million books by 1500; the Reformation and modern science rode on them.","Cheap copies brought literacy, standard languages, and censorship: a pattern every new medium repeats."],"src":[["Johannes Gutenberg — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Johannes-Gutenberg"],["Printing press — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Printing_press"],["British Library: Gutenberg Bible","https://www.bl.uk/collection-items/gutenberg-bible"]],"cat":"history"},{"id":"renaissance","title":"The Renaissance","dek":"Florence, the Medici, perspective and the recovery of the ancient world","icon":"🎨","slides":[{"h":"A rebirth, named later","p":"'Rinascita' was used by the painter-biographer Vasari in 1550 for the revival of ancient Greek and Roman art and learning in Italy. Historians date it from roughly the 1300s to the 1600s, beginning in the city-states of northern Italy, above all Florence, and spreading north across the Alps after 1500.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1304–74","Petrarch, humanism's father"],["1397","Medici bank founded"],["1420s","Brunelleschi's dome and perspective"],["1453","Fall of Constantinople; Greek scholars flee west"],["1503–12","Mona Lisa; Sistine ceiling"],["1527","Sack of Rome; the High Renaissance ends"]]}},{"h":"Why Florence","p":"Wool and banking made it rich; the Medici family's bank funded popes and kings, and the family spent on artists and scholars for prestige and piety. It was a republic of guilds with a competitive civic pride; cathedral doors, domes and chapels were commissions fought over publicly.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🧶","Wool & cloth","the first fortune"],["🏦","Banking","Medici, papal bankers"],["🏛","Guild republic","civic competitions"],["⛪","Church patronage","chapels as status"]]}},{"h":"Humanism: the ancients as teachers","p":"Petrarch and his followers hunted monastic libraries for lost Latin texts and, after 1400, learned Greek. Humanism put the study of language, history, poetry and ethics (the 'humanities') at the center of education, with human dignity and achievement as worthy subjects, not a turn away from religion.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Medieval emphasis","logic and theology","authority of commentaries","Latin as Church language"],"b":["Humanist emphasis","rhetoric, history, poetry","return to original sources","classical Latin and Greek"]}},{"h":"Perspective: painting with mathematics","p":"Around 1415 Brunelleschi demonstrated linear perspective: parallel lines converge at a vanishing point, so a flat panel can show measurable depth. Alberti wrote the rules down in 1435. Masaccio's Trinity fresco (c. 1427) is the first great painting built on them. Space became a subject.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Horizon line at the viewer's eye level","Vanishing point on it","Receding lines converge there","Objects shrink in proportion to distance"]}},{"h":"The dome nobody knew how to build","p":"Florence's cathedral had stood for a century with a 140-foot hole in its roof; no timber centering could span it. Brunelleschi won the 1418 competition with a double-shell dome of self-supporting brick rings laid in herringbone, built without scaffolding from the ground. Finished in 1436, it is still the largest brick dome.","v":{"t":"big","n":"45 m","c":"across, 1420–36, no wooden centering"}},{"h":"Leonardo, Michelangelo, Raphael","p":"The High Renaissance, roughly 1495 to 1527, compressed the three into one generation in Florence and Rome: Leonardo's Last Supper (1498) and Mona Lisa, Michelangelo's David (1504) and Sistine ceiling (1512), Raphael's School of Athens (1511). Patrons were popes and dukes; the artist became a famous individual.","v":{"t":"grid","cols":["Lived","Famous for"],"rows":[["Leonardo","1452–1519","Last Supper, Mona Lisa, notebooks"],["Michelangelo","1475–1564","David, Sistine ceiling, St Peter's dome"],["Raphael","1483–1520","School of Athens, Madonnas"]]}},{"h":"The money behind it","p":"The Medici were bankers who became rulers: Cosimo ran Florence from 1434 without holding office, Lorenzo 'the Magnificent' from 1469. Two became popes (Leo X, Clement VII) and two became queens of France. Their patronage bought legitimacy; their fall in 1494 and return in 1512 track the city's politics.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1434","Cosimo de' Medici takes control"],["1469–92","Lorenzo the Magnificent"],["1494","Medici expelled; Savonarola's bonfires"],["1513","Giovanni de' Medici becomes Pope Leo X"],["1569","Medici become Grand Dukes of Tuscany"]]}},{"h":"North of the Alps, and what it left","p":"Printing carried humanism north: Erasmus, Thomas More, Dürer's engravings, the Flemish painters' oil technique (which the Italians adopted). The Renaissance left the idea that antiquity could be recovered and improved on, the artist as genius, perspective in every image since, and the humanities on every curriculum.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["→","The individual artist, signed and celebrated"],["→","Perspective and anatomy in art"],["→","Original sources over commentaries"],["→","Oil paint from the north, printing everywhere"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Renaissance humanism was mainly a return to…","a":["Pagan religion","The study of ancient Greek and Roman texts and the humanities","Rejecting the Church"],"c":1,"why":"Humanists recovered classical sources and put language, history and ethics at the center of learning; most were devout."},{"q":"Brunelleschi is remembered for…","a":["The Mona Lisa","Linear perspective and the Florence cathedral dome","Founding the Medici bank"],"c":1,"why":"He demonstrated perspective around 1415 and built the largest brick dome in the world without centering, 1420–36."},{"q":"The Medici's power in Florence rested on…","a":["A hereditary crown","Banking wealth and patronage, often without formal office","Military conquest"],"c":1,"why":"Cosimo ruled from behind the scenes from 1434. Later Medici became popes, queens and finally dukes."}],"take":["Roughly 1300–1600, Florence first: wool and banking money, civic competition, recovered classics.","Perspective (1415) and the dome (1436) made art measurable; the artist became a name.","Printing carried it north and fixed its ideas into Western education."],"src":[["Renaissance — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/event/Renaissance"],["Filippo Brunelleschi — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filippo_Brunelleschi"],["House of Medici — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Medici-family"]],"cat":"history"},{"id":"french-revolution","title":"The French Revolution","dek":"From a bankrupt king to the guillotine to Napoleon in ten years","icon":"🇫🇷","slides":[{"h":"A kingdom that couldn't pay its bills","p":"By 1788 France spent about half its revenue on interest, much of it from helping the Americans beat Britain. Nobles and clergy paid almost no tax; the commoners (the Third Estate) paid nearly all of it. Bad harvests in 1788 doubled the price of bread. Louis XVI called the Estates-General for the first time since 1614.","v":{"t":"donut","c":"population","parts":[["Third Estate: everyone else",97],["nobility",2],["clergy",1]]}},{"h":"1789: the summer everything moved","p":"The Third Estate declared itself a National Assembly in June and swore not to disband until France had a constitution. On July 14 a Paris crowd stormed the Bastille. In August the Assembly abolished feudal privileges and declared the Rights of Man. In October women marched on Versailles and brought the king to Paris.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["5 May","Estates-General opens"],["20 Jun","Tennis Court Oath"],["14 Jul","Bastille falls"],["4 Aug","Feudal privileges abolished"],["26 Aug","Declaration of the Rights of Man"],["5–6 Oct","March on Versailles"]]}},{"h":"Liberty, equality, and the question of the king","p":"A constitutional monarchy was written in 1791. Louis tried to flee abroad in June and was caught at Varennes, which destroyed trust. War with Austria and Prussia began in April 1792; defeats and fear of invasion radicalised Paris. In August the crowd stormed the Tuileries and the monarchy was suspended.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Flight to Varennes, 1791","War, Apr 1792","Tuileries stormed, Aug","Republic, Sept 1792"]}},{"h":"The king's head, January 1793","p":"The new National Convention tried Louis XVI for treason and voted for death by a narrow margin. He was guillotined on 21 January 1793; Marie Antoinette followed in October. Every monarchy in Europe joined the war against France. The revolution could no longer be undone by compromise.","v":{"t":"big","n":"21 Jan 1793","c":"Louis XVI executed; Europe's crowns declare war"}},{"h":"The Terror","p":"Facing invasion, civil war in the Vendée and famine, the Committee of Public Safety under Robespierre ruled by emergency decree from 1793 to 1794. About 17,000 were executed after trial and tens of thousands more died in prison or the provinces. It ended when the Convention turned on Robespierre in July 1794 and guillotined him.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":40000,"items":[["Executed after trial",17000,"≈ 17,000"],["Died in prison or without trial",25000,"≈ 10,000–25,000+"],["Vendée civil war dead",40000,"far more; estimates vary widely"]]}},{"h":"What the revolution changed, concretely","p":"Feudal dues, noble titles and church tithes were abolished; church lands were sold; the metric system was created (1795); law was made uniform; careers opened to talent; Jews were emancipated (1791). Slavery was abolished in the colonies in 1794 (Napoleon restored it in 1802).","v":{"t":"list","items":[["→","Equality before the law; privileges gone"],["→","Metric system, decimal money"],["→","Church property nationalised"],["→","Citizenship for Protestants and Jews"],["→","A conscript army of citizens"]]}},{"h":"Napoleon, 1799","p":"Five years of unstable Directory government, coups and war fatigue ended when a young general, Napoleon Bonaparte, seized power on 18 Brumaire (9 November 1799). He kept the revolution's legal equality and codified it (the Civil Code, 1804), crowned himself emperor the same year, and exported code and conquest across Europe.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1795","Directory takes over"],["1796–97","Napoleon conquers northern Italy"],["Nov 1799","Coup of 18 Brumaire"],["1804","Civil Code; Napoleon crowned emperor"],["1815","Waterloo; Bourbons restored"]]}},{"h":"Why it still matters","p":"The revolution gave politics its vocabulary: left and right (from where deputies sat), nationalism, ideology, the citizen-soldier, human rights as a declaration. It showed a people could remove a king and that a revolution could devour its own. Every revolution since has been argued through its example.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["↔️","Left and right","seating in the Assembly"],["📜","Rights declared","universal, on paper"],["🪖","Nation in arms","mass conscription"],["⚖️","Civil Code","copied across the world"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The immediate trigger for calling the Estates-General in 1789 was…","a":["A foreign invasion","Royal bankruptcy and the need for new taxes","The storming of the Bastille"],"c":1,"why":"Debt from war, a privileged class that paid little tax and bad harvests forced Louis XVI to summon it."},{"q":"The Terror ended when…","a":["Napoleon took power","The Convention arrested and executed Robespierre in July 1794","Louis XVI returned"],"c":1,"why":"The Thermidorian reaction turned the emergency machinery on its chief architect."},{"q":"The political terms 'left' and 'right' come from…","a":["Napoleon's battle formations","Where deputies sat in the revolutionary assemblies","French road signs"],"c":1,"why":"Supporters of the king sat to the president's right, radicals to the left."}],"take":["Bankruptcy and privilege brought the Estates-General; 1789 moved from oath to Bastille to rights in four months.","War radicalised it: the republic in 1792, the king's execution in 1793, the Terror in 1793–94.","It ended in Napoleon, but left equality before the law, the metric system and the words left and right."],"src":[["French Revolution — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/event/French-Revolution"],["Reign of Terror — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reign_of_Terror"],["Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen — Yale Avalon Project","https://avalon.law.yale.edu/18th_century/rightsof.asp"]],"cat":"history"},{"id":"industrial-revolution","title":"The Industrial Revolution","dek":"Coal, cotton and steam: how Britain changed how everyone lives","icon":"🏭","slides":[{"h":"The hockey stick","p":"For most of history, income per person barely moved; a peasant in 1700 lived roughly as one in the year 1000. From about 1760 in Britain, output per person began rising and never stopped. Almost every chart of human material life bends upward at this point.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[1000,1],[1500,1.1],[1700,1.3],[1760,1.5],[1800,2],[1850,3.5],[1900,7],[1950,12],[2000,30]],"xl":"year","yl":"GDP per person (index)","l1":"× 30 since 1000"}},{"h":"Why Britain, why then","p":"Cheap coal near the surface; high wages that made labor-saving machines worth building; a market of 10 million people under one law; colonies and the Atlantic trade supplying cotton and buying cloth; secure property, patents, banks; and a culture of tinkering engineers. No one factor was enough; Britain had all of them.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["⛏","Cheap coal"],["💷","High wages"],["🧵","Cotton from the colonies"],["⚖️","Patents, property"],["🏦","Credit"],["🔧","Engineers"]]}},{"h":"Cotton first","p":"Spinning was the bottleneck: it took several spinners to feed one weaver. Hargreaves's spinning jenny (1764), Arkwright's water frame (1769) and Crompton's mule (1779) multiplied one worker's output by hundreds. Cloth prices collapsed; Britain's cotton exports grew about a hundredfold between 1760 and 1830.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1733","Kay's flying shuttle (weaving)"],["1764","Spinning jenny"],["1769","Arkwright's water frame; Watt's engine patent"],["1779","Crompton's mule"],["1785","Cartwright's power loom"],["1793","Whitney's cotton gin (U.S.)"]]}},{"h":"Steam makes the factory free of the river","p":"Newcomen's engines pumped mines from 1712; James Watt's separate condenser (1769) made them efficient, and his rotary engine (1781) could drive machinery anywhere coal could be carted. Factories left the fast streams for the cities. Coal output rose from about 3 million tons in 1700 to 65 million by 1850.","v":{"t":"cols","unit":" Mt","items":[["1700",3],["1750",5],["1800",15],["1850",65],["1900",225]]}},{"h":"Iron and rails","p":"Smelting iron with coke instead of charcoal (Darby, 1709) and Cort's puddling process (1784) made iron cheap. Put it under a steam engine on rails: Stephenson's Rocket (1829) and the Liverpool–Manchester line (1830) started a boom; Britain had 6,000 miles of track by 1850. Goods, people and news moved at thirty miles an hour.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[1830,100],[1840,1500],[1850,6000],[1860,9000],[1870,13500]],"xl":"year","yl":"miles of rail, Britain","l1":"13,500 by 1870"}},{"h":"Life in the mill towns","p":"Manchester grew from about 25,000 people in 1770 to 300,000 by 1850. Twelve- to fourteen-hour days, child labor from age six, cholera in the slums; average height actually fell for a generation. Factory Acts from 1833 limited children's hours and sent inspectors; the ten-hour day came in 1847.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["The cost","children in mills and mines","14-hour days","cholera, smoke, slums","wages flat until ~1820s"],"b":["The reform","1833 Factory Act: inspectors","1842: no women or children underground","1847: ten-hour day","sanitation acts from 1848"]}},{"h":"It spreads","p":"Belgium, France and the German states industrialised from the 1830s, the United States with New England textiles and then railroads, Japan after 1868. A second wave (steel, electricity, chemicals, oil) ran from about 1870. Industrial power became military power: the gap between industrialised and not decided the age of empires.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1760s","Britain"],["1830s","Belgium, France, Germany, U.S. Northeast"],["1868","Japan's Meiji restoration"],["1870s","Second revolution: steel, electricity, chemicals"],["1890s","U.S. overtakes Britain in output"]]}},{"h":"The world it made","p":"Most people now live in cities, work for wages, measure time by clocks, and expect their children to live better than they did; none of that was true in 1750. The same engines began loading carbon into the air, which is the bill now arriving. It is the most consequential change in human life since farming.","v":{"t":"stack","c":"share of people living in cities, Britain","parts":[["1750: urban ≈ 20%",20],["",30],["1851: urban > 50%",50]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The first industry transformed by machines in Britain was…","a":["Steel","Cotton textiles","Railways"],"c":1,"why":"The spinning jenny, water frame and mule multiplied one spinner's output by hundreds from the 1760s."},{"q":"James Watt's improvements to the steam engine mattered because…","a":["He invented the steam engine","They made engines efficient enough to drive factories anywhere coal could be brought","They ran on oil"],"c":1,"why":"Newcomen's engines existed from 1712; Watt's separate condenser and rotary motion freed factories from rivers."},{"q":"Average heights fell for a generation in early industrial Britain because…","a":["Of a change in diet fashion","Urban slums, long hours and disease outpaced wage gains","Of emigration"],"c":1,"why":"Living standards for workers lagged output for decades; Factory and sanitation acts came from the 1830s."}],"take":["From about 1760 in Britain, output per person began rising and never stopped.","Cotton, then steam, iron and rail; coal underneath all of it.","Cities, wage work and rising expectations are its legacy; so is the carbon."],"src":[["Industrial Revolution — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/event/Industrial-Revolution"],["Our World in Data: Economic growth","https://ourworldindata.org/economic-growth"],["Factory Acts — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Factory_Acts"]],"cat":"history"},{"id":"how-wwi-started","title":"How the First World War started","dek":"An assassination, five weeks of telegrams and a war nobody could stop","icon":"🕊","slides":[{"h":"Europe in two camps","p":"By 1914 the great powers were locked into alliances: Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy in the Triple Alliance; France, Russia and Britain in the Triple Entente. Military plans assumed the next war would be short and that mobilising first would win it. A quarrel between any two could pull in all six.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Central Powers","Germany","Austria-Hungary","Ottoman Empire (Nov 1914)","Bulgaria (1915)"],"b":["Allies / Entente","France","Russia","Britain and the Empire","Italy (1915), U.S. (1917)"]}},{"h":"Sarajevo, 28 June 1914","p":"Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to Austria-Hungary, was shot in Sarajevo by Gavrilo Princip, a Bosnian Serb nationalist linked to a secret society in Serbia. Vienna saw its chance to crush Serbia, the magnet for its restless South Slavs, and asked Berlin for backing.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["28 Jun","Assassination in Sarajevo"],["5–6 Jul","Germany's 'blank cheque' to Austria"],["23 Jul","Austrian ultimatum to Serbia"],["28 Jul","Austria declares war on Serbia"],["1 Aug","Germany declares war on Russia"],["4 Aug","Britain declares war on Germany"]]}},{"h":"The ultimatum","p":"Austria's 48-hour ultimatum of 23 July was written to be refused. Serbia accepted nearly all of it; Vienna declared war anyway on 28 July and shelled Belgrade. Russia, Serbia's protector, began to mobilise. Once Russia moved, German plans required an immediate strike at France through Belgium.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Austria → Serbia","Russia mobilises","Germany → Russia & France","Belgium invaded","Britain → Germany"]}},{"h":"The timetable problem","p":"Mobilising millions by rail took days of fixed schedules. Germany's Schlieffen Plan had one option: knock out France in six weeks via Belgium, then turn east before slow Russia was ready. There was no plan for a war against Russia alone. When the Kaiser asked for one on 1 August, he was told it did not exist.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Russia mobilises (30 July)","Germany must beat France first: the only plan","The route is through neutral Belgium","Britain had guaranteed Belgium since 1839","Five declarations of war in eight days"]}},{"h":"Why they thought it would be short","p":"Recent wars (1866, 1870) had been decided in weeks. Generals believed offence and morale would overwhelm defence. Few had absorbed what machine guns, magazine rifles and quick-firing artillery had done in the American Civil War, the Boer War and the Russo-Japanese War. 'Home by Christmas' was sincere.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":1560,"items":[["Austro-Prussian War, 1866",49,"7 weeks"],["Franco-Prussian War, 1870–71",300,"~10 months"],["Expected in 1914",90,"'by Christmas'"],["Actual, 1914–18",1560,"4 years, 3 months"]]}},{"h":"The Marne, and the trenches","p":"The German advance was stopped on the Marne in September 1914, twenty miles from Paris. Both sides then tried to outflank each other north to the sea and failed. By November a line of trenches ran 440 miles from Switzerland to the Channel. It moved less than ten miles either way in the next three years.","v":{"t":"big","n":"440 mi","c":"of trenches, Switzerland to the sea, by November 1914"}},{"h":"The arithmetic of attrition","p":"Verdun (1916) cost about 700,000 casualties; the Somme about a million, with 57,000 British casualties on the first day alone. By 1918 some 9 to 10 million soldiers and perhaps 7 million civilians were dead. Four empires, the German, Austro-Hungarian, Russian and Ottoman, did not survive it.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":2000000,"items":[["Germany",2000000,"≈ 2.0M military dead"],["Russia",1800000,"≈ 1.8M"],["France",1400000,"≈ 1.4M"],["Austria-Hungary",1200000,"≈ 1.2M"],["Britain & Empire",1000000,"≈ 1.0M"],["United States",116000,"≈ 116,000"]]}},{"h":"The lesson historians still argue over","p":"Was it German ambition, Austrian recklessness, Russian mobilisation, alliance systems, rigid plans, or sleepwalking leaders who all thought someone else would back down? The record supports pieces of each. What's not in dispute: the machinery of alliances and mobilisation turned a Balkan murder into a world war in five weeks.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🇩🇪","Blank cheque"],["🇦🇹","Ultimatum built to fail"],["🇷🇺","Early mobilisation"],["🚂","Rigid rail timetables"],["🤝","Alliance chains"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Germany invaded neutral Belgium in August 1914 because…","a":["Belgium had attacked Germany","Its only war plan required defeating France quickly by that route","Britain asked it to"],"c":1,"why":"The Schlieffen Plan assumed a two-front war and struck France first through Belgium, which brought Britain in."},{"q":"The war's first weeks ended with…","a":["German troops in Paris","A stalemate and 440 miles of trenches","A Russian victory in Berlin"],"c":1,"why":"The Marne stopped the German advance; the race to the sea produced a line that barely moved for three years."},{"q":"How many empires collapsed as a result of the war?","a":["One","Two","Four"],"c":2,"why":"German, Austro-Hungarian, Russian and Ottoman. The map of Europe and the Middle East was redrawn."}],"take":["Alliances plus mobilisation timetables turned Sarajevo into a world war in five weeks.","Everyone planned for a short war; the machine gun and the trench made it four years.","Nine to ten million soldiers dead, four empires gone, and the seeds of 1939."],"src":[["World War I — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/event/World-War-I"],["July Crisis — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/July_Crisis"],["Imperial War Museums: How the First World War began","https://www.iwm.org.uk/history/how-the-first-world-war-began"]],"cat":"history"},{"id":"cold-war","title":"The Cold War in ten cards","dek":"Forty-five years of rivalry that never became a direct war, and nearly did","icon":"🧊","slides":[{"h":"Allies who didn't trust each other","p":"The United States and the Soviet Union defeated Germany together and divided Europe between them at Yalta and Potsdam in 1945. Within two years the partnership was over: Stalin installed communist governments across Eastern Europe, and Washington decided to contain Soviet power wherever it pushed.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1945","Yalta, Potsdam; Hiroshima"],["1946","Churchill's 'iron curtain' speech"],["1947","Truman Doctrine; Marshall Plan announced"],["1948–49","Berlin blockade and airlift"],["1949","NATO founded; Soviet atomic bomb; China goes communist"]]}},{"h":"Containment and the Marshall Plan","p":"George Kennan's 1947 analysis argued Soviet expansion could be held in check by patient counter-pressure. The Truman Doctrine funded Greece and Turkey; the Marshall Plan put about $13 billion (roughly $150 billion today) into rebuilding Western Europe, which also made it a market and a bulwark.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":3.3,"items":[["Britain",3.3,"$3.3B"],["France",2.3,"$2.3B"],["West Germany",1.4,"$1.4B"],["Italy",1.2,"$1.2B"],["Netherlands",1.1,"$1.1B"]]}},{"h":"Berlin: the airlift and the Wall","p":"In 1948 Stalin cut road and rail access to West Berlin, an island inside the Soviet zone. For eleven months Allied planes flew in everything, landing every few minutes, until he gave up. In 1961 East Germany built a wall through the city to stop its people leaving; 140 or more died trying to cross it before 1989.","v":{"t":"big","n":"277,000","c":"flights in the Berlin Airlift, June 1948 – May 1949"}},{"h":"The bomb, and the balance of terror","p":"The Soviets tested an atomic bomb in 1949, both sides had hydrogen bombs by the mid-1950s, and intercontinental missiles by the 1960s. Mutual assured destruction meant neither side could strike without being destroyed. The arsenals peaked at about 70,000 warheads in 1986.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[1950,300],[1960,20000],[1970,38000],[1986,70000],[2000,32000],[2020,13000]],"xl":"year","yl":"nuclear warheads, world","l1":"70,000 in 1986"}},{"h":"Hot wars by proxy","p":"The superpowers fought through others: Korea (1950–53, about 3 million dead), Vietnam (U.S. combat 1965–73), Afghanistan after the Soviet invasion of 1979, plus coups and civil wars from Guatemala and Iran to Angola and Nicaragua. The Cold War was cold in Europe and very hot elsewhere.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1950–53","Korea"],["1953, 1954","Coups in Iran, Guatemala"],["1965–73","U.S. combat in Vietnam"],["1975","Angola; Saigon falls"],["1979–89","Soviets in Afghanistan"]]}},{"h":"October 1962: thirteen days","p":"U.S. spy planes found Soviet nuclear missiles in Cuba. Kennedy imposed a naval 'quarantine'; Soviet ships approached it. On 27 October a U-2 was shot down and a Soviet submarine officer refused to fire a nuclear torpedo. Khrushchev withdrew for a public U.S. pledge not to invade Cuba and a secret one on missiles in Turkey.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["16 Oct: missiles photographed","22 Oct: quarantine announced on TV","24 Oct: Soviet ships stop at the line","27 Oct: U-2 shot down; 'Black Saturday'","28 Oct: Khrushchev agrees to withdraw"]}},{"h":"Space, spies and détente","p":"Sputnik (1957) and Gagarin (1961) shocked America into the Moon programme. The 1970s brought arms treaties (SALT I, 1972), Nixon in China (1972) and the Helsinki Accords (1975), then renewed tension after Afghanistan and Reagan's buildup. The economic race was being lost by a Soviet system that couldn't produce consumer goods.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Soviet strengths","heavy industry, missiles","space firsts: Sputnik, Gagarin","control of Eastern Europe"],"b":["Soviet weaknesses","consumer goods, farming","computing, by the 1980s","a budget drained by arms and Afghanistan"]}},{"h":"Gorbachev, and the end","p":"Mikhail Gorbachev took over in 1985 and tried reform: glasnost (openness), perestroika (restructuring), arms cuts, withdrawal from Afghanistan, and a refusal to use force to prop up Eastern European regimes. In 1989 they fell one after another; the Berlin Wall opened on 9 November. The Soviet Union dissolved on 26 December 1991.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["Mar 1985","Gorbachev general secretary"],["Dec 1987","INF Treaty scraps a missile class"],["Jun 1989","Poland's semi-free election"],["9 Nov 1989","Berlin Wall opens"],["Dec 1991","USSR dissolved"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"'Containment' meant…","a":["Invading the Soviet Union","Holding Soviet expansion in check with steady counter-pressure","Isolating the United States"],"c":1,"why":"Kennan's 1947 doctrine shaped U.S. policy for four decades: aid, alliances and proxy wars rather than direct war."},{"q":"The Cuban Missile Crisis ended with…","a":["A U.S. invasion of Cuba","A Soviet withdrawal in exchange for U.S. pledges, one public and one secret","Nuclear strikes on both sides"],"c":1,"why":"Khrushchev pulled the missiles out; the U.S. promised not to invade Cuba and quietly removed missiles from Turkey."},{"q":"The Berlin Wall came down in…","a":["1985","1989","1991"],"c":1,"why":"9 November 1989, after Gorbachev declined to back the East German regime with force. The USSR dissolved two years later."}],"take":["1947–1991: containment, alliances and proxy wars, held in check by mutual destruction.","Berlin, Korea, Cuba 1962 and Afghanistan were the flashpoints.","It ended when a Soviet leader chose reform over force and the system could not survive openness."],"src":[["Cold War — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/event/Cold-War"],["U.S. State Department, Office of the Historian: Kennan and containment","https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/kennan"],["JFK Library: Cuban Missile Crisis","https://www.jfklibrary.org/learn/about-jfk/jfk-in-history/cuban-missile-crisis"]],"cat":"history"},{"id":"space-race","title":"The Space Race","dek":"From Sputnik's beep to Apollo 11 in twelve years","icon":"🚀","slides":[{"h":"A beep from orbit, October 1957","p":"Sputnik 1 was a 184-pound polished sphere that broadcast a radio pulse anyone could tune in. The rocket that launched it could also carry a warhead across an ocean, which is what alarmed Washington. Within a year the U.S. had passed the National Defense Education Act and created NASA.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["4 Oct 1957","Sputnik 1"],["3 Nov 1957","Laika, first animal in orbit"],["31 Jan 1958","Explorer 1, first U.S. satellite"],["1 Oct 1958","NASA opens"],["12 Apr 1961","Gagarin, first human in space"]]}},{"h":"The Soviets kept winning firsts","p":"First satellite, first animal, first man (Yuri Gagarin, 1961), first woman (Valentina Tereshkova, 1963), first spacewalk (Alexei Leonov, 1965), first probe to hit the Moon and photograph its far side. Their chief designer, Sergei Korolev, was a state secret until he died in 1966.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Soviet firsts","satellite (1957)","human in orbit (1961)","woman in space (1963)","spacewalk (1965)","soft landing on the Moon (1966)"],"b":["American firsts","weather & comms satellites","rendezvous and docking (1965–66)","humans around the Moon (1968)","humans on the Moon (1969)"]}},{"h":"Kennedy raises the stakes, May 1961","p":"Six weeks after Gagarin and a month after the Bay of Pigs, Kennedy asked Congress to commit to landing a man on the Moon 'before this decade is out'. The U.S. had fifteen minutes of human spaceflight experience. It was a bet that a long, hard goal would play to American industrial strength.","v":{"t":"quote","q":"We choose to go to the Moon in this decade and do the other things, not because they are easy, but because they are hard.","by":"John F. Kennedy, Rice University, 12 September 1962"}},{"h":"Mercury, Gemini, Apollo","p":"Mercury (1961–63) proved a person could survive orbit. Gemini (1965–66) practised the hard parts: rendezvous, docking, spacewalks, two-week flights. Apollo was the Moon programme itself, built around the Saturn V, a 363-foot rocket that remains the most powerful ever successfully flown until 2022.","v":{"t":"flow","items":[["1","Mercury: survive orbit"],["2","Gemini: rendezvous, EVA"],["3","Apollo: the Moon"]]}},{"h":"The cost, in money and lives","p":"Apollo cost about $25 billion by 1973, roughly $250 billion today; at the peak NASA took over 4 percent of the federal budget and employed 400,000 people through contractors. Three astronauts died in the Apollo 1 cabin fire in January 1967; the Soviets lost Vladimir Komarov in Soyuz 1 three months later.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[1958,0.1],[1960,0.5],[1962,1.2],[1964,3.5],[1966,4.4],[1968,2.6],[1970,1.9],[1973,1.3]],"xl":"year","yl":"NASA share of U.S. budget (%)","l1":"4.4% in 1966"}},{"h":"Apollo 8: around the Moon at Christmas","p":"In December 1968 Borman, Lovell and Anders became the first humans to leave Earth orbit and circle the Moon, ten times. Anders's photograph 'Earthrise' and the crew's Christmas Eve broadcast reached a quarter of the world's population. The Soviets' giant N1 rocket, meanwhile, failed on all four launches.","v":{"t":"big","n":"240,000 mi","c":"from Earth; Apollo 8 was the first crew to make the trip"}},{"h":"20 July 1969","p":"Eagle landed in the Sea of Tranquility with about 25 seconds of fuel margin, after Armstrong flew past a boulder field by hand. Six hours later he stepped onto the surface; Aldrin followed. They spent two and a half hours outside, collected 47 pounds of rock and were home on 24 July. About 600 million people watched.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["16 Jul: launch from Kennedy Space Center","19 Jul: lunar orbit","20 Jul 20:17 UTC: 'The Eagle has landed'","21 Jul 02:56 UTC: first step","24 Jul: splashdown in the Pacific"]}},{"h":"What it left behind","p":"Six landings and twelve people walked on the Moon by December 1972; nobody has since. The programme left weather and communications satellites, integrated circuits pushed to maturity, 842 pounds of Moon rock, and in 1975 a handshake in orbit (Apollo-Soyuz). The new race, to return and to Mars, has more players.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1969–72","Six landings, 12 moonwalkers"],["1975","Apollo-Soyuz docking"],["1981","Space Shuttle"],["1998","International Space Station begins"],["2022","Artemis I flies uncrewed around the Moon"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Sputnik alarmed the United States mainly because…","a":["It was spying on cities","The rocket that launched it could carry a warhead across an ocean","It was larger than any U.S. satellite"],"c":1,"why":"The satellite was a radio beacon. The R-7 launcher was an intercontinental missile."},{"q":"Gemini's purpose was to…","a":["Land on the Moon","Practise rendezvous, docking and spacewalks","Test animals in orbit"],"c":1,"why":"Everything Apollo needed except the Moon itself was rehearsed in Gemini's ten crewed flights in 1965–66."},{"q":"How many people have walked on the Moon?","a":["2","12","24"],"c":1,"why":"Twelve, on six Apollo landings from 1969 to 1972. Twenty-four flew to the Moon; none has returned since."}],"take":["Sputnik 1957, Gagarin 1961, Kennedy's pledge 1961, Apollo 11 1969.","Mercury proved orbit, Gemini practised the manoeuvres, Apollo went.","Twelve moonwalkers by 1972, none since; satellites and chips were the lasting payoff."],"src":[["NASA: Apollo 11 mission overview","https://www.nasa.gov/mission/apollo-11/"],["Space Race — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Space-Race"],["Sputnik 1 — NASA","https://www.nasa.gov/history/sputnik-and-the-dawn-of-the-space-age/"]],"cat":"history"},{"id":"checks-and-balances","title":"How the U.S. Constitution is wired","dek":"Three branches, the checks between them and how it gets amended","icon":"⚖️","slides":[{"h":"Seven articles, written in a summer","p":"Fifty-five delegates met in Philadelphia from May to September 1787 to fix the failing Articles of Confederation and wrote a new government instead. The document has seven articles: the legislature, the executive, the courts, the states, amendments, federal supremacy and ratification. It took effect in 1789.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1781","Articles of Confederation: no taxing power, no executive"],["May–Sep 1787","Constitutional Convention"],["Jun 1788","Ninth state ratifies; it's law"],["Apr 1789","Washington inaugurated"],["Dec 1791","Bill of Rights ratified"]]}},{"h":"Three branches, three jobs","p":"Congress makes law, controls spending and declares war. The President executes the law, commands the military, conducts foreign policy and appoints officials. The courts interpret the law and decide cases. Each is chosen differently, on a different clock, so no single election captures all three.","v":{"t":"grid","cols":["Chosen by","Term"],"rows":[["House","districts","2 years"],["Senate","states","6 years"],["President","Electoral College","4 years, max 2"],["Federal judges","President + Senate","life"]]}},{"h":"Checks: each branch can stop the others","p":"The President vetoes bills; Congress overrides with two-thirds of both houses. The Senate confirms judges and treaties. Courts strike down laws and executive acts. Congress impeaches (House, majority) and removes (Senate, two-thirds) presidents and judges. Congress holds the purse; the President holds the pen.","v":{"t":"cycle","c":"each checks the next","items":["Congress: purse, override, impeach","President: veto, appoint, pardon","Courts: review, strike down"]}},{"h":"Judicial review wasn't in the text","p":"The Constitution doesn't say courts can strike down laws. Chief Justice John Marshall asserted it in Marbury v. Madison (1803) by voiding part of a statute, and the practice stuck. The Supreme Court's nine justices (a number set by Congress, not the Constitution) now decide the meaning of the whole document.","v":{"t":"quote","q":"It is emphatically the province and duty of the judicial department to say what the law is.","by":"Chief Justice John Marshall, Marbury v. Madison, 1803"}},{"h":"Federalism: two governments at once","p":"Powers not given to the federal government are reserved to the states (Tenth Amendment). States run elections, schools, most policing and most courts; Washington handles defense, currency, interstate commerce and much else through the Commerce Clause. Federal law wins where the two conflict (Article VI).","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Federal","defense, foreign affairs","currency, interstate commerce","immigration, patents","post office"],"b":["States","elections (even federal ones)","schools, licensing","most criminal law","property, family law"]}},{"h":"The Bill of Rights, and the rest of the amendments","p":"The first ten amendments (1791) list what government may not do: speech, press, religion, assembly, arms, searches, trials, punishments. Later amendments ended slavery (13th), guaranteed equal protection and due process against states (14th), and extended the vote by race (15th), sex (19th) and age 18 (26th).","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1791","1st–10th: Bill of Rights"],["1865–70","13th, 14th, 15th: slavery, equal protection, vote"],["1913","16th income tax; 17th elected senators"],["1920","19th: women's suffrage"],["1951","22nd: two-term limit"],["1971","26th: vote at 18"]]}},{"h":"Amending it is hard on purpose","p":"Two-thirds of both houses of Congress propose, three-quarters of the states (38) ratify; or two-thirds of states call a convention, which has never happened. More than 11,000 amendments have been proposed; 27 have passed. The last substantive one, the 26th, was in 1971; the 27th, on congressional pay, took 203 years.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["⅔ House + ⅔ Senate","¾ of states (38)","Amendment"]}},{"h":"Why the design looks the way it does","p":"The framers had read how the Roman Republic died and how Britain's king had been checked. They assumed ambition and designed so that, in Madison's phrase, ambition would counteract ambition. The result is slow by intent: big changes need broad, durable majorities across institutions elected on different timetables.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["→","Separate powers, so no one body rules"],["→","Different electorates and terms, so no one election decides all"],["→","Supermajorities for the biggest moves"],["→","Rights that majorities can't vote away"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Congress can override a presidential veto with…","a":["A simple majority of the House","Two-thirds of both houses","A Supreme Court ruling"],"c":1,"why":"The veto check is itself checked by the override, which requires broad agreement in both chambers."},{"q":"Judicial review, the power of courts to strike down laws…","a":["Is spelled out in Article III","Was established by the Supreme Court in Marbury v. Madison (1803)","Was added by amendment in 1913"],"c":1,"why":"The text is silent; Marshall's opinion asserted the power and it has been accepted ever since."},{"q":"Ratifying an amendment requires…","a":["A national referendum","Three-quarters of the states","The President's signature"],"c":1,"why":"Two-thirds of Congress proposes, 38 states ratify. The President has no formal role, which is why only 27 have passed."}],"take":["Three branches with separate powers, elected on different clocks, each able to block the others.","Judicial review came from a case, not the text; federalism splits power with the states.","Amendment needs ⅔ of Congress and ¾ of the states, which is why there are only 27."],"src":[["National Archives: The Constitution of the United States","https://www.archives.gov/founding-docs/constitution"],["Library of Congress: Constitution Annotated","https://constitution.congress.gov/"],["Marbury v. Madison — Oyez","https://www.oyez.org/cases/1789-1850/5us137"]],"cat":"society"},{"id":"economic-numbers","title":"GDP, inflation, unemployment: the numbers","dek":"What the headline statistics measure, who counts them and where they mislead","icon":"📊","slides":[{"h":"GDP: everything made, added up once","p":"Gross domestic product is the market value of all final goods and services produced in a country in a period, usually a quarter. 'Final' avoids double counting: the flour is in the bread. U.S. GDP is about $29 trillion a year; the growth rate you hear is quarter-over-quarter, annualised, adjusted for inflation.","v":{"t":"donut","c":"by spending","parts":[["consumer spending",68],["business investment",17],["government",15]]}},{"h":"Real vs nominal","p":"Nominal GDP rises when prices rise even if nothing more is made. Real GDP strips out inflation to measure actual output. The same goes for wages: a 4 percent raise with 5 percent inflation is a 1 percent pay cut. Whenever a number spans years, ask whether it's real.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Nominal","current prices","rises with inflation","what the cash register says"],"b":["Real","constant prices","inflation removed","what was actually produced or bought"]}},{"h":"Inflation: a basket, priced monthly","p":"The Bureau of Labor Statistics prices a basket of about 80,000 items each month in cities across the country to build the Consumer Price Index. 'Core' inflation drops food and energy, which swing, to see the trend. The Fed's 2 percent target is on a related index, PCE, which weights things differently.","v":{"t":"stack","c":"CPI basket weights (approx.)","parts":[["shelter",35],["food",14],["transport",16],["medical",8],["everything else",27]]}},{"h":"Inflation falling is not prices falling","p":"When inflation drops from 8 percent to 3, prices are still rising, just more slowly. Prices falling (deflation) is rare and usually a sign of a slump. People compare today's prices to a few years ago and feel the level; economists report the rate. Both are right about different things.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[2019,100],[2020,101],[2021,106],[2022,114],[2023,119],[2024,122],[2025,125]],"xl":"year","yl":"price level (2019 = 100)","l1":"level keeps rising as the rate slows"}},{"h":"Unemployment: two surveys, two numbers","p":"The unemployment rate comes from a survey of about 60,000 households: you count as unemployed if you have no job and looked for one in the past four weeks. The 'jobs added' figure comes from a separate survey of about 120,000 employers. They're released together on the first Friday of the month and sometimes disagree.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Household survey","~60,000 households","unemployment rate","counts people, including self-employed"],"b":["Establishment survey","~120,000 employers","payroll jobs added","counts jobs; one person can hold two"]}},{"h":"Who isn't counted","p":"People who stopped looking aren't unemployed; they're 'out of the labor force', along with students, retirees and carers. The labor-force participation rate (about 62 percent) tracks them. Broader measures (U-6) add discouraged and involuntary part-time workers and run several points higher than the headline.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"%","max":8,"items":[["U-3 headline rate",4,"≈ 4%"],["U-6, incl. discouraged & part-time for economic reasons",7.5,"≈ 7–8%"]]}},{"h":"The Fed's two jobs and one tool","p":"The Federal Reserve is charged with maximum employment and stable prices. Its main tool is the federal funds rate, which sets the cost of overnight borrowing between banks and ripples into mortgages, car loans and business credit. Raising it cools demand and inflation; cutting it does the reverse, with a lag of a year or more.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Fed funds rate","Bank lending rates","Spending & hiring","Inflation & jobs"]}},{"h":"Reading the release without being fooled","p":"One month is noise; look at the three-month trend. Numbers get revised, often. Seasonal adjustment is why January layoffs don't show as a crash. A statistic that's good for the economy can be bad for you: GDP can grow while your wage doesn't. Check which number, which period, real or nominal, and whether it was revised.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","Three-month average, not one print"],["✓","Watch the revisions to prior months"],["✓","Real or nominal?"],["✓","Rate or level?"],["✓","Which survey?"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"If inflation falls from 8% to 3%, prices…","a":["Fall by 5%","Keep rising, more slowly","Return to where they were"],"c":1,"why":"Inflation is a rate of change. Only deflation means prices actually fall, and that's rare."},{"q":"Someone who gave up looking for work is counted as…","a":["Unemployed","Out of the labor force","Employed part-time"],"c":1,"why":"The headline rate counts only those who searched in the past four weeks; broader measures like U-6 include the discouraged."},{"q":"The jobs-added number and the unemployment rate come from…","a":["The same survey","Two different surveys, of employers and of households","Tax returns"],"c":1,"why":"Payrolls come from about 120,000 employers; the rate from about 60,000 households. They can point different ways in a given month."}],"take":["GDP counts final output; always ask real or nominal.","Inflation is a rate: falling inflation still means rising prices.","Unemployment counts active job seekers; payroll jobs come from a different survey. Watch trends and revisions."],"src":[["Bureau of Economic Analysis: GDP","https://www.bea.gov/data/gdp/gross-domestic-product"],["Bureau of Labor Statistics: How the government measures unemployment","https://www.bls.gov/cps/cps_htgm.htm"],["Federal Reserve: Monetary policy","https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy.htm"]],"cat":"society"},{"id":"stoicism","title":"Stoicism in five ideas","dek":"A Greek porch, a Roman emperor and a philosophy built for bad days","icon":"🏺","slides":[{"h":"Born on a painted porch","p":"Zeno of Citium began teaching in Athens around 300 BC at the Stoa Poikile, the painted colonnade, which gave the school its name. It ran for five centuries. Its best-known writers were a Roman statesman (Seneca), a freed slave (Epictetus) and an emperor (Marcus Aurelius) who wrote notes to himself in a tent on the Danube.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["c. 300 BC","Zeno teaches at the Stoa"],["c. 4 BC–AD 65","Seneca: letters, tutor to Nero"],["c. 50–135","Epictetus: the Discourses, Enchiridion"],["121–180","Marcus Aurelius: the Meditations"]]}},{"h":"1. Some things are up to you; most aren't","p":"Epictetus opens his handbook with it: our judgments, intentions and efforts are ours; our bodies, reputations, property and other people are not fully. Misery comes from treating the second kind as the first. Put your energy where it has purchase and hold the rest loosely.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Up to you","your judgments","your choices and effort","how you treat people","what you pay attention to"],"b":["Not up to you","outcomes","others' opinions","health, weather, markets","the past"]}},{"h":"2. It's not the event, it's the judgment","p":"'People are disturbed not by things but by the views they take of things.' The traffic jam is a fact; the fury is an opinion added to it. The Stoics practised noticing the gap between the two and choosing what to add. Modern cognitive behavioral therapy was built on exactly this idea.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Event","Impression","Judgment (yours)","Emotion","Action"]}},{"h":"3. Virtue is the only real good","p":"Wisdom, justice, courage and self-control are good in themselves; health, wealth and reputation are 'preferred indifferents', worth pursuing but not worth selling your character for. The point isn't austerity. Seneca was rich. The point is that a good life can't be taken from you by bad luck.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🦉","Wisdom"],["⚖️","Justice"],["🦁","Courage"],["🧘","Temperance"]]}},{"h":"4. Rehearse the bad in advance","p":"Premeditatio malorum: each morning, picture the day's likely setbacks, the rude colleague, the delay, the loss, so they arrive expected rather than as insults. Seneca went further and recommended occasional days of plain food and rough clothes, to learn that the thing you fear is survivable.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Morning: what could go wrong today?","Picture handling it well","Meet it when it comes, already prepared","Evening: review what you did and would change"]}},{"h":"5. Remember you will die","p":"Memento mori wasn't morbid; it was a lens. Marcus reminds himself that everyone he envies and everyone who annoys him will be dust, and so will he, so he had better do the day's work with care and not waste it on resentment. Mortality is the deadline that makes priorities obvious.","v":{"t":"quote","q":"You could leave life right now. Let that determine what you do and say and think.","by":"Marcus Aurelius, Meditations 2.11"}},{"h":"What it is not","p":"Not suppressing emotion: the Stoics wanted fewer destructive passions, not numbness, and cared intensely about justice and other people. Not passivity: Marcus ran an empire and fought wars. Not 'stoic' in the modern stiff-upper-lip sense. It's a training programme for attention and judgment.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✗","Having no feelings"],["✗","Accepting injustice"],["✗","Withdrawing from the world"],["✓","Choosing your judgments, acting on duty, holding results loosely"]]}},{"h":"A one-week trial","p":"Each morning, name one thing in your control and one that isn't. Each evening, three lines: what went well, what didn't, what you'd do differently. When something stings, say 'this is an impression' and wait a beat. That is the whole practice, and it is older than the Roman Empire.","v":{"t":"cycle","c":"daily","items":["Morning: in/out of my control","Day: notice the impression","Evening: three-line review"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The dichotomy of control says to focus effort on…","a":["Outcomes","Your own judgments, choices and efforts","Other people's opinions"],"c":1,"why":"Those are the things that are fully up to you. Outcomes and others' views are influenced, never controlled."},{"q":"'People are disturbed not by things but by the views they take of things' is the root of…","a":["Epicureanism","Cognitive behavioral therapy","Utilitarianism"],"c":1,"why":"CBT's founders credited the Stoics directly for the idea that the judgment, not the event, drives the emotion."},{"q":"Marcus Aurelius wrote the Meditations as…","a":["A public philosophy textbook","Private notes to himself","Letters to his son"],"c":1,"why":"They were never meant for publication, which is why they read like someone talking himself through hard days."}],"take":["Separate what's up to you from what isn't; spend yourself on the first.","The judgment, not the event, makes the emotion; there's a gap, and you can use it.","Rehearse setbacks, remember mortality, review the day: a practice, not a mood."],"src":[["Stoicism — Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/stoicism/"],["Epictetus, Enchiridion — MIT Classics","http://classics.mit.edu/Epictetus/epicench.html"],["Marcus Aurelius — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Marcus-Aurelius-Roman-emperor"]],"cat":"culture"},{"id":"how-to-look-at-a-painting","title":"How to look at a painting","dek":"Six questions that turn a glance into seeing","icon":"🖼","slides":[{"h":"Give it more than eight seconds","p":"Museum visitors spend a median of about 15 to 30 seconds in front of a work, much of it reading the label. The method on these cards takes three or four minutes and works on anything from a Vermeer to your kid's drawing. Look first; read the label last.","v":{"t":"big","n":"3 min","c":"look first, label last"}},{"h":"1. Where does your eye go first, and then?","p":"Painters steer the eye: with the brightest spot, the sharpest contrast, a face, a pointing hand, lines that lead. Notice your first landing and the path after it. That route is usually the story the painter wants told in order.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🔆","Brightest spot"],["⬛","Strongest contrast"],["👁","A face or eyes"],["➡️","Lines and gestures"]]}},{"h":"2. How is it arranged?","p":"Composition is the skeleton. Look for the big shapes: a triangle (stable, classical), a diagonal (energy), a horizontal band (calm), a frame within the frame. Where is the horizon? Is the subject centered or pushed to an edge, and what does the empty space do?","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Stable","triangle, pyramid","horizontals","centered subject","symmetry"],"b":["Dynamic","diagonals","off-center subject","cropped edges","asymmetry"]}},{"h":"3. Where is the light coming from?","p":"Find the shadows and you find the light source. Single hard light from the side (Caravaggio, Rembrandt) is drama; soft light from a window (Vermeer) is intimacy; flat, even light (much modern painting) refuses to tell you what to feel. Light is the painter's mood lighting.","v":{"t":"grid","cols":["Source","Effect"],"rows":[["Chiaroscuro","one hard light, deep shadow","drama, focus"],["Window light","soft, from one side","calm, intimacy"],["Plein air","sunlight, outdoors","freshness, time of day"],["Flat","no clear source","modern, graphic"]]}},{"h":"4. What is the color doing?","p":"Is the palette warm or cool, saturated or muted, and is there one color that breaks the rule? Complementary pairs (red against green, orange against blue) make each other vibrate; a single warm note in a cool picture is often the point. Squint and the color scheme reveals itself.","v":{"t":"cycle","c":"complements sit opposite","items":["Red","Orange","Yellow","Green","Blue","Violet"]}},{"h":"5. How was it made?","p":"Get close. Thick, visible strokes (impasto) say speed and feeling; glassy invisible surfaces say patience and control. Is it oil, which blends and glows; tempera, which is crisp; watercolor, which stains and can't be undone; or fresco, painted into wet plaster? The hand is in the surface.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🖌","Impasto","thick, fast, emotional"],["🪞","Smooth glaze","layered, patient"],["💧","Watercolor","transparent, unforgiving"],["🧱","Fresco","into wet plaster, by the day"]]}},{"h":"6. What was it for, and who paid?","p":"Now read the label. An altarpiece was made to be seen by candlelight from below; a portrait was flattery with a bill attached; a history painting was propaganda for a salon; an Impressionist canvas was made to be sold to a new middle class. Purpose explains size, subject and style.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["Medieval","altarpieces, the Church"],["Renaissance","patrons: Medici, popes"],["17th c.","Dutch merchants: still lifes, interiors"],["18th–19th","academies, salons, the state"],["1870s on","dealers, galleries, the market"]]}},{"h":"Then say one true thing about it","p":"Out loud or in a notebook: 'The light comes from the left and the only warm color is her sleeve.' Not whether it's good, not what it means, just one thing you saw. Do that with ten paintings and you'll start seeing the eleventh before the label can tell you what to think.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Eye path, then composition","Light, then color","Surface: how it was made","Purpose: who paid, read the label","One true sentence"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The fastest way to find a painting's light source is to…","a":["Read the label","Find the shadows","Look at the frame"],"c":1,"why":"Shadows point away from the light. Hard single shadows mean one dramatic source; soft ones mean diffuse light."},{"q":"A composition built on strong diagonals usually feels…","a":["Calm and stable","Energetic and in motion","Flat"],"c":1,"why":"Horizontals and triangles read as stable; diagonals and cropped edges read as movement."},{"q":"Complementary colors placed side by side…","a":["Cancel each other out","Make each other look more intense","Turn grey"],"c":1,"why":"Red beside green, orange beside blue: each heightens the other, which painters use to make a small note sing."}],"take":["Look before you read; follow where your eye is led.","Composition, light, color, surface: the four things to find in any picture.","Finish with one true sentence about what you saw."],"src":[["National Gallery (London): How to look at paintings","https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/"],["Composition (visual arts) — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Composition_(visual_arts)"],["Chiaroscuro — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/art/chiaroscuro"]],"cat":"culture"},{"id":"classical-music-eras","title":"Classical music: the eras and what to hear","dek":"Six periods in six cards, with one piece to start each","icon":"🎻","slides":[{"h":"'Classical' is six centuries, not one style","p":"The label covers Western art music from medieval chant to living composers. The useful map is six eras, each with a sound you can learn to recognise in a few listens. Dates overlap; styles arrived in different places at different times. Start anywhere.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["500–1400","Medieval"],["1400–1600","Renaissance"],["1600–1750","Baroque"],["1750–1820","Classical"],["1820–1900","Romantic"],["1900–","Modern & contemporary"]]}},{"h":"Medieval and Renaissance: voices first","p":"Gregorian chant is a single melodic line, unaccompanied, for worship. From about 1100 composers stacked independent voices (polyphony), and by the Renaissance (Josquin, Palestrina, Tallis) four to forty voices wove together in church and in secular madrigals. Instruments were mostly for dancing.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Medieval","chant: one line","early polyphony: Pérotin","modes, not keys","Hildegard of Bingen"],"b":["Renaissance","smooth imitative polyphony","Josquin, Palestrina, Byrd","Tallis's Spem in alium: 40 voices","madrigals, lute songs"]}},{"h":"Baroque (1600–1750): ornament and engine","p":"Opera is invented (Monteverdi, 1607). Music gets a driving bass line (basso continuo), terraced loud-and-soft dynamics, and endless ornament. Bach builds fugues, Vivaldi writes 500 concertos, Handel writes Messiah in 24 days. The harpsichord's pluck is the era's signature sound.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🎹","Harpsichord","plucked, no dynamics"],["🔁","Fugue","one theme, chasing itself"],["🎭","Opera is born","Monteverdi, 1607"],["🎻","Concerto","soloist vs. orchestra"]]}},{"h":"Classical (1750–1820): clarity and form","p":"Reaction against Baroque density: clear melodies over simple accompaniment, balanced phrases, and sonata form (a theme stated, developed, returned) as the engine of symphonies, string quartets and sonatas. Haydn invents much of it, Mozart perfects it, Beethoven breaks it open. The piano replaces the harpsichord.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Exposition: themes stated","Development: taken apart","Recapitulation: themes return","Coda"]}},{"h":"Romantic (1820–1900): bigger, longer, personal","p":"Beethoven's late works point the way: music as the composer's inner life. Orchestras double in size, symphonies run an hour, harmony stretches toward breaking (Wagner's Tristan chord, 1865). Chopin writes for the piano alone; Verdi and Wagner build opera into national theatre; Tchaikovsky, Brahms and Dvořák fill concert halls.","v":{"t":"cols","unit":"","items":[["Haydn",25,"~25 players"],["Beethoven",45],["Brahms",70],["Mahler",100,"100+"]]}},{"h":"Modern (1900–): every rule questioned","p":"Debussy dissolves the key; Stravinsky's Rite of Spring causes a riot in 1913 with its pounding rhythms; Schoenberg abandons tonality for the twelve-tone row; Bartók folds in folk music; Shostakovich writes under Stalin; after 1945 minimalists (Reich, Glass) build hypnotic repetition, and film scores carry the orchestra to all.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1894","Debussy, Prélude à l'après-midi d'un faune"],["1913","Stravinsky, The Rite of Spring"],["1923","Schoenberg's twelve-tone method"],["1936","Barber, Adagio for Strings"],["1976","Reich, Music for 18 Musicians"]]}},{"h":"What to listen for in any piece","p":"Find the pulse and whether it's in twos or threes. Find the tune and notice when it comes back, and changed how. Notice which instrument has it and who's accompanying. Listen for the loudest moment and ask how long the composer made you wait for it. That's most of music appreciation.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","Pulse: twos or threes?"],["✓","The tune, and its returns"],["✓","Who has the melody now?"],["✓","Tension and release: where's the peak?"],["✓","Texture: one line, or many?"]]}},{"h":"Six pieces to start","p":"One per era, each under about fifteen minutes or with a famous movement: Hildegard's O virtus Sapientiae; Tallis's Spem in alium; Bach's Brandenburg Concerto No. 3; Mozart's Symphony No. 40, first movement; Dvořák's Symphony No. 9, Largo; Reich's Music for 18 Musicians, opening. Then follow whatever you liked.","v":{"t":"grid","cols":["Piece"],"rows":[["Medieval","Hildegard: O virtus Sapientiae"],["Renaissance","Tallis: Spem in alium"],["Baroque","Bach: Brandenburg No. 3"],["Classical","Mozart: Symphony 40, I"],["Romantic","Dvořák: Symphony 9, Largo"],["Modern","Reich: Music for 18 Musicians"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"The harpsichord's plucked sound is the signature of which era?","a":["Renaissance","Baroque","Romantic"],"c":1,"why":"Bach, Handel and Vivaldi wrote for it; the piano, which can play soft and loud, took over in the Classical era."},{"q":"Sonata form, the engine of Classical-era symphonies, is…","a":["A dance in three-quarter time","Themes stated, developed and returned","A piece for solo piano only"],"c":1,"why":"Exposition, development, recapitulation. Haydn, Mozart and Beethoven built symphonies, quartets and sonatas on it."},{"q":"Which premiere famously caused a riot in Paris in 1913?","a":["Beethoven's Ninth","Stravinsky's The Rite of Spring","Handel's Messiah"],"c":1,"why":"Its pounding rhythms and dissonance, plus Nijinsky's choreography, split the audience into shouting factions."}],"take":["Six eras: chant and polyphony, Baroque ornament, Classical form, Romantic feeling, Modern questions.","Listen for pulse, tune, who has it, and where the peak is.","One piece per era, then follow your ear."],"src":[["Classical music — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/art/classical-music"],["Sonata form — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonata_form"],["BBC: A brief history of classical music","https://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/articles/1L7yRbRbTcZh1WGJn7pB0wD/a-brief-history-of-classical-music"]],"cat":"culture"},{"id":"jazz-history","title":"Jazz in eight cards","dek":"From New Orleans to bebop to now: the styles, the names, the records","icon":"🎷","slides":[{"h":"New Orleans, around 1900","p":"Jazz grew out of ragtime, blues, marching bands and the Caribbean rhythms of a port city, in the Black neighbourhoods of New Orleans. Early bands improvised together over simple chords: trumpet on the tune, clarinet weaving above, trombone below, a rhythm section driving. Buddy Bolden is the legend; no recording of him survives.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🎺","Ragtime","Scott Joplin's syncopation"],["🎸","Blues","bent notes, 12 bars"],["🥁","Brass bands","parades, funerals"],["🌴","Caribbean","habanera rhythms"]]}},{"h":"Louis Armstrong invents the soloist","p":"Armstrong's Hot Five and Hot Seven records (1925–28) turned jazz from group improvisation into a soloist's art: swinging rhythm, dazzling trumpet lines, and scat singing. 'West End Blues' (1928) is the one to hear. Nearly every jazz musician since has been answering him.","v":{"t":"quote","q":"If you have to ask what jazz is, you'll never know.","by":"attributed to Louis Armstrong"}},{"h":"Swing: the 1930s dance music of America","p":"Big bands of 15 or so players with written arrangements and room for soloists: Duke Ellington (the composer), Count Basie (the rhythm), Benny Goodman (the crossover), Ella Fitzgerald and Billie Holiday (the voices). For a decade jazz was pop music, and Goodman's 1938 Carnegie Hall concert made it respectable.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1927","Ellington at the Cotton Club"],["1935","Goodman's Palomar date: swing era begins"],["1937","Basie's band hits New York"],["1938","Carnegie Hall concert"],["1939","Holiday records 'Strange Fruit'"]]}},{"h":"Bebop: fast, hard, for listening","p":"In Harlem after-hours clubs in the early 1940s, Charlie Parker, Dizzy Gillespie, Thelonious Monk and Bud Powell built a virtuoso music in small groups: fast tempos, complex chords, long improvised lines. It wasn't for dancing. Parker's 'Ko-Ko' (1945) announced it; Monk's tunes are still the repertoire's hardest puzzles.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Swing","big band, written charts","for dancing","medium tempos","the tune out front"],"b":["Bebop","quintet, head arrangements","for listening","very fast","the chords as a playground"]}},{"h":"1959: the year everything happened","p":"Miles Davis's Kind of Blue, built on modes instead of chord changes, became the best-selling jazz record ever. Dave Brubeck's Time Out put 5/4 on the radio with 'Take Five'. Ornette Coleman's The Shape of Jazz to Come threw out the chords altogether. Charles Mingus's Mingus Ah Um looked back to gospel and forward to protest.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["💿","Miles Davis, Kind of Blue (modal)"],["💿","Dave Brubeck, Time Out (odd meters)"],["💿","Ornette Coleman, The Shape of Jazz to Come (free)"],["💿","Charles Mingus, Mingus Ah Um"],["💿","John Coltrane, Giant Steps (recorded 1959)"]]}},{"h":"Coltrane, and the search","p":"John Coltrane went from the hardest chord changes ever written ('Giant Steps') to modal hymns (A Love Supreme, 1965) to free improvisation in five years. His quartet with McCoy Tyner, Jimmy Garrison and Elvin Jones set the template for intensity. He died at 40 in 1967; a church in San Francisco is named for him.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1957","Blue Train"],["1960","Giant Steps released"],["1961","My Favorite Things"],["1965","A Love Supreme"],["1966","Ascension: free jazz"]]}},{"h":"Fusion and after","p":"Miles plugged in: Bitches Brew (1970) mixed jazz improvisation with rock and funk, and his sidemen founded Weather Report, Return to Forever and the Headhunters. The music also went back to the tradition (Wynton Marsalis, 1980s), into hip-hop (A Tribe Called Quest, Robert Glasper, Kendrick Lamar) and around the world.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Bitches Brew, 1970","Fusion bands","Neo-traditionalists, 1980s","Jazz + hip-hop, 1990s–","Now: Kamasi, Esperanza, Shabaka"]}},{"h":"How to listen","p":"Find the tune (the 'head'), played at the start. Then each soloist improvises over the same chords for a chorus or several, trading the spotlight; the band comments behind them. The head returns at the end. Count the choruses, hear who's quoting whom, and notice the drummer's conversation with the soloist. That's the form.","v":{"t":"cycle","c":"a standard performance","items":["Head (the tune)","Solo 1","Solo 2","Trading fours","Head out"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Louis Armstrong's main contribution was…","a":["Inventing the saxophone","Turning jazz into a soloist's art with swing and scat","Writing the first big-band arrangements"],"c":1,"why":"The Hot Five and Hot Seven records (1925–28) made the improvising soloist the center of the music."},{"q":"Bebop differed from swing in that it was…","a":["Slower and for dancing","Faster, harmonically complex, played in small groups for listening","Entirely written out"],"c":1,"why":"Parker, Gillespie and Monk built a musicians' music in Harlem clubs in the early 1940s."},{"q":"Kind of Blue (1959) was built on…","a":["Rapid chord changes","Modes, with few chords, leaving space to improvise","Electric instruments"],"c":1,"why":"Modal jazz gave soloists scales rather than dense changes. It's the best-selling jazz album ever made."}],"take":["New Orleans → Armstrong's soloist → swing → bebop → 1959's modal and free turns → fusion → now.","The form: head, solos over the same chords, head out.","Start with West End Blues, Kind of Blue and A Love Supreme."],"src":[["Jazz — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/art/jazz"],["Smithsonian: Jazz history","https://americanhistory.si.edu/smithsonian-jazz"],["Kind of Blue — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kind_of_Blue"]],"cat":"culture"},{"id":"architecture-styles","title":"Eight building styles you can name on sight","dek":"From Greek columns to glass boxes: what to look for and when it was built","icon":"🏗","slides":[{"h":"Classical: the three orders","p":"Greek temples (c. 600–100 BC) and Roman buildings after them use columns in three orders: Doric (plain capital, no base), Ionic (scroll capital), Corinthian (acanthus leaves). Pediments, symmetry and proportion. The Romans added the arch, the dome and concrete. Banks and courthouses still borrow the look.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["▬","Doric","plain, sturdy; Parthenon"],["⌒","Ionic","scrolls; Erechtheion"],["❦","Corinthian","leaves; Pantheon porch"]]}},{"h":"Romanesque, then Gothic","p":"Romanesque churches (c. 1000–1150) are heavy: round arches, thick walls, small windows, like fortresses. Gothic (c. 1140–1500) solved the weight problem with pointed arches and flying buttresses that carry the roof's thrust outside, so walls could become glass: Chartres, Notre-Dame, Cologne, the soaring rib vault.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Romanesque","round arches","thick walls, small windows","barrel vaults","Durham, Speyer"],"b":["Gothic","pointed arches","flying buttresses","huge stained glass, rose windows","Chartres, Notre-Dame"]}},{"h":"Renaissance and Baroque","p":"Renaissance (15th–16th c.) revived Roman order: symmetry, columns, round arches, domes, proportion by number (Brunelleschi, Palladio, whose villas became the model for every columned house in Virginia). Baroque (17th c.) took the same vocabulary and made it move: curves, drama, gold, oval plans, Bernini's colonnade, Versailles.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1436","Brunelleschi's dome, Florence"],["1570","Palladio's Four Books"],["1626","St Peter's completed"],["1660s–","Versailles"],["1700s","Rococo: lighter, more ornate"]]}},{"h":"Neoclassical and the 19th-century revivals","p":"From about 1750, archaeology and the Enlightenment brought back strict Greek and Roman forms: the U.S. Capitol, the Panthéon in Paris, the British Museum. The 19th century then revived everything in turn: Gothic for churches and parliaments (Westminster), Romanesque for libraries, Renaissance for banks. Style became a choice.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🏛","Neoclassical","Capitol, Panthéon"],["⛪","Gothic Revival","Westminster, Oxford colleges"],["🏰","Romanesque Revival","Smithsonian Castle"],["🏦","Beaux-Arts","Grand Central, Paris Opéra"]]}},{"h":"Art Nouveau and Art Deco","p":"Art Nouveau (1890–1910) used iron and glass for plant-like curves: Paris Métro entrances, Gaudí's Barcelona, Horta's Brussels. Art Deco (1920s–30s) went the other way: streamlined, geometric, zigzags and sunbursts, chrome and black glass, the Chrysler Building and Miami Beach hotels. Both are easy to spot and date.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["Art Nouveau, 1890–1910","whiplash curves","plants, vines, insects","Guimard, Gaudí, Mackintosh"],"b":["Art Deco, 1920s–30s","zigzags, sunbursts","machine age, speed","Chrysler Building, Radio City"]}},{"h":"Modernism: form follows function","p":"The Bauhaus (1919) and Le Corbusier stripped ornament away: flat roofs, white walls, ribbon windows, open plans, steel and glass, the building as a 'machine for living in'. After 1945 the glass-and-steel tower (Mies's Seagram Building, 1958) became the world's office building. Brutalism poured the same ideas in raw concrete.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1919","Bauhaus founded"],["1931","Villa Savoye"],["1952","Lever House: the glass curtain wall"],["1958","Seagram Building"],["1950s–70s","Brutalism: Barbican, Boston City Hall"]]}},{"h":"Postmodern and contemporary","p":"By the 1970s modernism's boxes felt cold. Postmodernism (Venturi, Graves, Philip Johnson's AT&T Building with its broken pediment, 1984) brought back color, ornament and jokes. Since the 1990s computers made curves cheap: Gehry's Bilbao (1997), Hadid's swoops, and a green turn toward timber, insulation and reuse.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🎨","Postmodern","ornament returns, 1970s–90s"],["🌊","Deconstructivist","Gehry, Hadid, 1990s–"],["🌿","Sustainable","mass timber, passive design"],["🔁","Adaptive reuse","factories to lofts"]]}},{"h":"How to date a building in ten seconds","p":"Look at the windows and the roofline. Small windows and round arches: Romanesque or its revival. Pointed arches: Gothic or revival. Columns and a pediment: classical, in some century. Zigzags: Deco. Ribbon windows and no ornament: modernist. A curtain of glass: post-1950. A shape you couldn't draw with a ruler: post-1990.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Windows?","Arches?","Columns?","Ornament?","Material?"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Flying buttresses allowed Gothic cathedrals to have…","a":["Thicker walls","Huge windows, because the roof's thrust was carried outside the wall","Flat roofs"],"c":1,"why":"With the load moved to external supports, walls could dissolve into stained glass."},{"q":"Zigzags, sunbursts and chrome on a 1930 skyscraper point to…","a":["Art Nouveau","Art Deco","Brutalism"],"c":1,"why":"Deco is the streamlined geometry of the 1920s and 30s; Nouveau is the plant-like curves two decades earlier."},{"q":"'Form follows function' and ribbon windows belong to…","a":["Neoclassicism","Modernism","Baroque"],"c":1,"why":"The Bauhaus and Le Corbusier stripped ornament to express structure and use."}],"take":["Columns and pediments: classical. Pointed arches and buttresses: Gothic. Curves of plants: Nouveau. Zigzags: Deco.","No ornament, ribbon windows, glass: modern. Shapes a ruler can't draw: after 1990.","Windows and roofline date a building fastest."],"src":[["Western architecture — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/art/Western-architecture"],["Gothic architecture — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gothic_architecture"],["Bauhaus — Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Bauhaus"]],"cat":"culture"},{"id":"how-the-internet-works","title":"How the internet works","dek":"Packets, addresses, DNS and the cables under the sea","icon":"🌐","slides":[{"h":"Everything is chopped into packets","p":"A web page, a video call or an email is broken into small packets, each with the sender's and receiver's addresses and a sequence number. Packets travel independently, often by different routes, and are reassembled at the far end. If one is lost, only that one is resent. This is why the network survives broken links.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Your message","Split into packets","Routed separately","Reassembled","Delivered"]}},{"h":"Every device has an address","p":"An IP address is the number packets are sent to: 142.250.72.14 in the older IPv4 system (about 4.3 billion addresses, long since exhausted) or a much longer IPv6 one. Your home usually has one public address shared by all its devices, which is what your router manages.","v":{"t":"compare","a":["IPv4","192.0.2.44","4.3 billion addresses","ran out around 2011","still most traffic"],"b":["IPv6","2001:db8::8a2e:370:7334","340 undecillion","rolling out since 1999","about half of Google's traffic"]}},{"h":"DNS: the phone book","p":"You type a name; the network needs a number. The Domain Name System turns 'example.com' into an IP address through a chain of lookups: your resolver asks the root servers, which point to the .com servers, which point to the site's own name servers. The answer is cached so it takes milliseconds next time.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Browser asks the resolver (your ISP's or 1.1.1.1 / 8.8.8.8)","Resolver asks a root server: who handles .com?",".com servers: who handles example.com?","Example.com's name server: here's the IP","Cached for next time"]}},{"h":"Routers pass packets hop by hop","p":"No router knows the whole path. Each one looks at a packet's destination, consults a table of which neighbour is closer, and forwards it. A packet from Ohio to Tokyo takes perhaps fifteen to twenty hops. The tables are built by routing protocols (BGP between networks) that constantly share who can reach what.","v":{"t":"big","n":"15–20","c":"hops, each a router deciding only the next step"}},{"h":"TCP makes it reliable; HTTP makes it a web","p":"The protocols stack. IP delivers packets (no promises). TCP on top numbers them, acknowledges receipt, resends losses and controls speed. HTTP on top of that asks for pages and sends them back; HTTPS wraps it in encryption (TLS), the padlock, so the coffee shop can't read your bank login.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Physical: fiber, copper, radio","IP: addressing and routing","TCP: reliable, ordered delivery (or UDP: fast, lossy)","TLS: encryption","HTTP: the web's requests and pages"]}},{"h":"It mostly runs under the sea","p":"Satellites carry a sliver of traffic. More than 95 percent of intercontinental data travels through roughly 500 submarine fiber-optic cables, each about the thickness of a garden hose, carrying light pulses across oceans in tens of milliseconds. Ships' anchors and earthquakes are their enemies; repair ships splice them.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":99,"items":[["Submarine cables",99,"≈ 95–99% of intercontinental traffic"],["Satellites",1,"about 1%"]]}},{"h":"Nobody owns it","p":"The internet is tens of thousands of independent networks (ISPs, universities, companies, governments) that agree to exchange traffic, often at exchange points in the same building. Standards come from open bodies: the IETF writes the protocols, ICANN coordinates names and numbers, the W3C the web. No one can switch it off.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🏢","~75,000 networks","autonomous systems"],["🔀","Exchange points","where they connect"],["📜","IETF","the protocols (RFCs)"],["🏷","ICANN","names & numbers"]]}},{"h":"From your tap to the page","p":"Type a URL. DNS finds the address. TCP opens a connection; TLS agrees a key. HTTP asks for the page; the server, perhaps a content-delivery node a few miles away, sends it as hundreds of packets through a dozen routers. Your browser reassembles and draws it. Elapsed: a few hundred milliseconds.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["0 ms","You press Enter"],["~20 ms","DNS answer"],["~60 ms","TCP + TLS handshake"],["~100 ms","First bytes arrive"],["~300 ms","Page drawn"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Data crosses the internet as…","a":["One continuous stream on one path","Small packets that may take different routes","Radio waves to satellites"],"c":1,"why":"Packets are routed independently and reassembled at the end, which is why the network survives broken links."},{"q":"DNS is the system that…","a":["Encrypts your traffic","Turns names like example.com into IP addresses","Stores web pages"],"c":1,"why":"It's the phone book: a chain of lookups from root servers down to the site's own name servers, cached along the way."},{"q":"Most data between continents travels by…","a":["Satellite","Undersea fiber-optic cables","Microwave towers"],"c":1,"why":"Roughly 500 submarine cables carry about 95–99 percent of it; satellites are a small fraction."}],"take":["Packets, each addressed and numbered, routed hop by hop and reassembled.","DNS turns names into numbers; TCP makes delivery reliable; TLS makes it private.","Undersea cables carry nearly all of it, and no single body owns the network."],"src":[["Mozilla: How the Internet works","https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Howto/Web_mechanics/How_does_the_Internet_work"],["Cloudflare: What is DNS?","https://www.cloudflare.com/learning/dns/what-is-dns/"],["Submarine communications cable — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Submarine_communications_cable"]],"cat":"science"},{"id":"greenhouse-effect","title":"The greenhouse effect","dek":"Why a trace gas matters, how we know, and what the numbers are","icon":"🌍","slides":[{"h":"Sunlight in, heat out","p":"The Sun's light passes through the air and warms the ground. The ground radiates that energy back as infrared heat. Certain gases, water vapour, carbon dioxide, methane, absorb infrared and re-emit it in all directions, including back down. Without them Earth would average about −18 °C; with them, about +15 °C.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["☀️ Sunlight in","Ground warms","Infrared out","Gases absorb & re-emit","Some returns down"]}},{"h":"Known since the 1850s","p":"Eunice Foote (1856) and John Tyndall (1859) measured that CO₂ and water vapour trap heat. In 1896 Svante Arrhenius calculated that doubling CO₂ would warm the planet by several degrees. The physics is old, laboratory-tested and not in dispute; what changed is how much CO₂ we add.","v":{"t":"timeline","items":[["1856","Foote: CO₂ warms in sunlight"],["1859","Tyndall measures infrared absorption"],["1896","Arrhenius: doubling CO₂ warms the Earth"],["1958","Keeling starts measuring at Mauna Loa"],["1988","IPCC founded"]]}},{"h":"The Keeling curve","p":"Since 1958 the observatory on Mauna Loa has measured CO₂ in the air. The line saws up and down with the Northern Hemisphere's seasons and climbs relentlessly: about 315 parts per million in 1958, 420-plus now, against about 280 before industry. Air bubbles in ice cores show it's the highest in at least 800,000 years.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[1958,315],[1970,325],[1980,338],[1990,354],[2000,369],[2010,389],[2020,414],[2025,425]],"xl":"year","yl":"CO₂, parts per million","l1":"≈ 425 ppm"}},{"h":"A trace gas with a large effect","p":"0.04 percent sounds small, but the air's main gases (nitrogen, oxygen) are transparent to infrared; CO₂ and its companions do all the trapping. Water vapour traps more in total but follows temperature; CO₂ sets the thermostat and water amplifies it. Methane traps about 80 times more per ton over 20 years but decays in a decade.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"","max":80,"items":[["CO₂",1,"baseline; lasts centuries"],["Methane (20-yr)",80,"~80× per ton, ~12-year life"],["Nitrous oxide",60,"~270× per ton (100-yr scale), ~110 years"]]}},{"h":"How much warmer, so far","p":"Global average surface temperature is about 1.2 to 1.3 °C above the late-1800s average, with the last decade the warmest on record and 2024 the warmest year. The land warms faster than the sea, the Arctic two to four times faster than the global average. The ocean has absorbed about 90 percent of the extra heat.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[1880,-0.2],[1910,-0.4],[1940,0.05],[1970,0],[1990,0.4],[2010,0.7],[2024,1.3]],"xl":"year","yl":"°C vs 1850–1900","l1":"+1.3 °C (2024)"}},{"h":"How we know it's us","p":"Fossil carbon has a different isotope signature, and the air's carbon is shifting toward it. Oxygen is falling as CO₂ rises, as burning predicts. The upper atmosphere is cooling while the surface warms, which a brighter Sun would not do. The amount added matches the fossil fuels burned, less what oceans and plants absorb.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["✓","Isotopes: the extra carbon is fossil carbon"],["✓","Oxygen falls as CO₂ rises: combustion"],["✓","Stratosphere cools as surface warms: not the Sun"],["✓","Nights warm faster than days: trapped heat, not more sunlight"]]}},{"h":"Where the emissions come from","p":"Roughly three-quarters of greenhouse emissions come from energy: electricity and heat, transport, industry and buildings. Agriculture, forestry and land use are most of the rest, with methane from cattle and rice and nitrous oxide from fertiliser. Cement alone is about 7 percent of CO₂.","v":{"t":"donut","c":"global emissions","parts":[["energy: power, industry, transport, buildings",73],["agriculture & land use",18],["waste",3],["industrial processes (cement, chemicals)",6]]}},{"h":"The numbers that frame the choices","p":"Each 1,000 billion tons of CO₂ adds about 0.45 °C. Holding warming to 1.5 °C leaves a budget of only a few years of current emissions; 2 °C, a couple of decades. Solar and wind are now the cheapest new power in most places, which is why the argument has moved from physics to speed. CO₂ already emitted stays for centuries.","v":{"t":"scale","min":0,"max":3,"unit":" °C","zones":[[0,1.3,"warming so far"],[1.3,1.5,"Paris goal"],[1.5,2,"'well below 2'"],[2,3,"current policies' track"]],"marks":[[1.3,"now"],[2.7,"policies as of 2020s"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Without any greenhouse gases, Earth's average temperature would be about…","a":["+15 °C, as now","−18 °C","+40 °C"],"c":1,"why":"The natural greenhouse effect keeps the planet about 33 degrees warmer than it would otherwise be. The problem is adding to it."},{"q":"CO₂ in the air has risen from about 280 ppm before industry to…","a":["About 300 ppm","About 420 ppm","About 1,000 ppm"],"c":1,"why":"Mauna Loa measured 315 in 1958 and passed 420 in the 2020s, the highest in at least 800,000 years of ice-core records."},{"q":"Which observation rules out the Sun as the cause of recent warming?","a":["Oceans are warming","The upper atmosphere is cooling while the surface warms","Ice is melting"],"c":1,"why":"A brighter Sun would warm all layers. Trapped heat warms the surface and cools the stratosphere, which is what's measured."}],"take":["Greenhouse gases let sunlight in and slow heat out; the physics has been measured since the 1850s.","CO₂: 280 ppm before industry, 420-plus now; warming about 1.3 °C so far.","The added carbon is fossil carbon, and it stays for centuries."],"src":[["NASA: Climate change evidence","https://science.nasa.gov/climate-change/evidence/"],["NOAA: Mauna Loa CO₂ record","https://gml.noaa.gov/ccgg/trends/"],["IPCC AR6 Synthesis Report","https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/syr/"]],"cat":"science"},{"id":"how-gps-works","title":"How GPS works","dek":"Thirty-one satellites, four signals and a correction from Einstein","icon":"🛰","slides":[{"h":"A constellation that broadcasts the time","p":"About 31 U.S. GPS satellites orbit at roughly 20,200 km, each circling twice a day, arranged so at least four are above the horizon anywhere on Earth. Each carries atomic clocks and continuously broadcasts its position and the exact time. Europe's Galileo, Russia's GLONASS and China's BeiDou do the same; phones use all of them.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🇺🇸","GPS","31 satellites, since 1978"],["🇪🇺","Galileo","~28"],["🇷🇺","GLONASS","~24"],["🇨🇳","BeiDou","~35"]]}},{"h":"Your receiver only listens","p":"A GPS chip never transmits. It picks up the satellites' signals, each stamped with when it left, and compares that to its own clock to work out how long each one took to arrive. Light travels about 30 cm per nanosecond, so a time difference gives a distance. The satellites don't know you exist.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["Satellite sends time + position","Signal takes ~67 ms","Receiver notes arrival","Delay × speed of light = distance"]}},{"h":"Three spheres meet at a point","p":"Knowing you're 20,300 km from one satellite puts you on a sphere. A second satellite narrows it to a circle, a third to two points, one of which is in space and discarded. That's trilateration. In practice a fourth satellite is needed, for a reason on the next card.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["1 satellite: you're on a sphere","2 satellites: a circle","3 satellites: two points (one absurd)","4 satellites: a point, and the time"]}},{"h":"The fourth satellite fixes your cheap clock","p":"The satellites carry atomic clocks accurate to nanoseconds; your phone's clock is off by far more than that, and a microsecond of error is 300 meters. So the receiver treats its own clock error as a fourth unknown and solves for latitude, longitude, altitude and time together. Every GPS fix is also an atomic-clock sync.","v":{"t":"big","n":"1 µs = 300 m","c":"why the receiver must solve for its own clock error"}},{"h":"Relativity is in the firmware","p":"Moving fast makes the satellites' clocks run slow by about 7 microseconds a day (special relativity); being higher in Earth's gravity makes them run fast by about 45 (general relativity). Net: 38 microseconds a day fast, which would put positions off by about 10 km a day. The clocks are deliberately set slow before launch.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":" µs/day","max":45,"items":[["Gravity (higher orbit): clocks run fast",45,"+45"],["Speed (14,000 km/h): clocks run slow",7,"−7"],["Net correction built in",38,"+38 µs/day"]]}},{"h":"What limits accuracy","p":"The ionosphere slows the signal; buildings reflect it (multipath); a satellite's orbit is known only so well. Civilian GPS alone gets about 5 meters outdoors. Phones add Wi-Fi and cell positioning, corrections broadcast from ground stations, and newer dual-frequency chips to get down to a meter or two.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":" m","max":10,"items":[["Civilian GPS alone",5,"≈ 5 m"],["Dual-frequency phone",1.5,"≈ 1–2 m"],["With ground corrections (RTK)",0.02,"≈ 2 cm"],["Urban canyon, single frequency",10,"10 m or worse"]]}},{"h":"It runs more than maps","p":"Because every receiver gets atomic time for free, GPS timestamps bank transactions, keeps cell towers and power grids in sync and times stock trades. Farmers steer tractors to the centimeter; airliners land with it; surveyors watch continents drift at fingernail speed. A GPS outage would be a timing crisis first.","v":{"t":"icons","items":[["🏦","Finance","timestamps"],["📡","Cell networks","tower sync"],["⚡","Power grid","phase timing"],["🚜","Farming","cm-level steering"],["✈️","Aviation","approaches"],["🌋","Geoscience","plate motion"]]}},{"h":"Weaknesses","p":"The signal reaching you is weaker than the background noise, which is why it fails indoors and can be jammed with a cheap device or spoofed with a fake signal, both increasingly common near conflict zones. Newer satellites, encrypted military codes and backups like eLoran are the responses. Keep a paper map in the glovebox.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["⚠️","Indoors, under trees, between tall buildings"],["⚠️","Jamming: a $20 device can blank a highway"],["⚠️","Spoofing: ships and planes shown miles off"],["✓","Multi-constellation, dual-frequency receivers help"]]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A GPS receiver works out its position by…","a":["Sending a signal to the satellites and waiting for a reply","Timing how long signals from several satellites took to arrive","Reading its location from a cell tower"],"c":1,"why":"It only listens. Each signal's travel time gives a distance; several distances pin down a point."},{"q":"A fourth satellite is needed because…","a":["Three are never visible at once","The receiver's own clock is inaccurate and must be solved for","Earth is not a perfect sphere"],"c":1,"why":"A microsecond of clock error is 300 meters, so the receiver treats its clock offset as a fourth unknown."},{"q":"Without correcting for relativity, GPS positions would drift by about…","a":["10 cm a day","10 m a day","10 km a day"],"c":2,"why":"The satellites' clocks gain about 38 microseconds a day net; that's roughly 10 kilometers of error daily if uncorrected."}],"take":["Satellites broadcast time and position; the receiver listens and turns delays into distances.","Four satellites: three for position, one to correct the receiver's cheap clock.","Relativity is corrected by 38 µs a day; accuracy is a few meters, and the signal is easily jammed."],"src":[["GPS.gov: How GPS works","https://www.gps.gov/systems/gps/"],["Global Positioning System — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Positioning_System"],["Physics Central: Relativity and GPS","https://www.aps.org/archives/publications/apsnews/201110/physicshistory.cfm"]],"cat":"science"},{"id":"probability-sense","title":"Probability for real life","dek":"The gambler's fallacy, expected value, base rates and the house edge","icon":"🎲","slides":[{"h":"The coin has no memory","p":"After five heads, the chance of a sixth is still one half. Dice, roulette wheels and lottery balls don't 'owe' anything. The gambler's fallacy is the feeling that a run must correct itself; the hot-hand version is the feeling that it must continue. Independent events are independent either way.","v":{"t":"cols","unit":"","items":[["H",50,"50%"],["H",50,"50%"],["H",50,"50%"],["H",50,"50%"],["H",50,"50%"],["next?",50,"still 50%"]]}},{"h":"Expected value: the average over many tries","p":"Multiply each outcome by its probability and add them up. A $2 lottery ticket with a 1-in-300-million jackpot of $300 million has an expected value of about $1 before taxes and shared prizes: you lose on average. Insurance has negative expected value too, and is still worth buying, because it covers losses you can't absorb.","v":{"t":"big","n":"Σ p × value","c":"what a bet is worth on average, over many plays"}},{"h":"The house edge, by game","p":"Every casino game pays slightly less than true odds. American roulette has 38 pockets and pays 35 to 1: the house keeps 5.26 percent of every dollar bet, on average, forever. Blackjack with perfect basic strategy is about 0.5 percent; slots run 2 to 15. The edge is small per bet and certain over thousands of bets.","v":{"t":"bars","unit":"%","max":15,"items":[["Blackjack, basic strategy",0.5],["Craps (pass line)",1.4],["European roulette",2.7],["American roulette",5.3],["Slots",8,"2–15%"],["State lottery",50,"≈ 50% (off scale)"]]}},{"h":"Law of large numbers","p":"Over few trials, anything happens; over many, the average settles toward the expected value. That is why the casino wins and a single gambler can win a night. It's also why a small sample of anything, five reviews, one season, one study, says less than it feels like it does.","v":{"t":"line","pts":[[1,20],[5,72],[10,40],[25,56],[50,47],[100,52],[500,50.3],[1000,50.1]],"xl":"coin flips (log-ish)","yl":"% heads so far","l1":"settles to 50%"}},{"h":"Base rates: the test that's '99% accurate'","p":"A disease affects 1 in 1,000 people. A test catches 99 percent of cases and gives 1 percent false positives. You test positive. Your chance of having it is about 9 percent, not 99: out of 1,000 people, about 1 true positive and about 10 false positives. The rarer the condition, the more a positive result lies.","v":{"t":"flow","items":["1,000 people","1 sick → 1 positive","999 well → ~10 positives","11 positives, 1 real","≈ 9%"]}},{"h":"Small risks, big numbers","p":"A one-in-a-million chance per day is about one in 2,700 per year. Rare events happen constantly to someone; with eight billion people, a million-to-one coincidence happens eight thousand times a day. 'What are the odds?' usually has the answer: over enough trials, high.","v":{"t":"cols","unit":"","items":[["1 day",1,"1 in 1,000,000"],["1 year",365,"1 in 2,700"],["80 years",29200,"1 in 34"]]}},{"h":"Correlation, chance and the bigger sample","p":"Two things moving together might be cause, coincidence or a third thing driving both. Ice cream and drowning rise together every summer. Before believing a pattern, ask how many comparisons were tried (one in twenty looks 'significant' by luck), whether it replicated, and what the base rate was.","v":{"t":"list","items":[["?","Could a third factor drive both?"],["?","How many things were compared?"],["?","Has it been seen again, elsewhere?"],["?","How big was the sample?"]]}},{"h":"Thinking in bets","p":"Say probabilities as numbers ('70%', not 'probably'), write them down, and score yourself later. Prefer decisions with good expected value and survivable downside; never bet the house, even on good odds, because ruin ends the game. A good decision can have a bad outcome, and the reverse; judge the process.","v":{"t":"steps","items":["Name the odds as a number","Multiply by what's at stake","Check: can I survive the worst case?","Decide; record it; score it later"]}}],"quiz":[{"q":"After red comes up six times in a row at roulette, the next spin is…","a":["More likely black","More likely red","Just as likely either, as always"],"c":2,"why":"Spins are independent. The wheel doesn't remember; the feeling that it must even out is the gambler's fallacy."},{"q":"A test is 99% accurate for a disease that 1 in 1,000 people have. You test positive. Your chance of having it is about…","a":["99%","50%","9%"],"c":2,"why":"Among 1,000 people there's about one true positive and ten false positives. Rare conditions make positives unreliable."},{"q":"The house edge on American roulette is about…","a":["0.5%","5.3%","25%"],"c":1,"why":"38 pockets, paid as if there were 36: 2/38 = 5.26% of every dollar bet, on average, forever."}],"take":["Independent events don't remember; the house wins on volume.","Expected value = probability × payoff, summed; survivability matters more than EV.","Rare conditions make positive tests misleading; ask for the base rate."],"src":[["Gambler's fallacy — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gambler%27s_fallacy"],["Base rate fallacy — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Base_rate_fallacy"],["Khan Academy: Expected value","https://www.khanacademy.org/math/statistics-probability/random-variables-stats-library/expected-value-lib/v/expected-value-of-a-discrete-random-variable"]],"cat":"science"}]};
